window.repoFiles = {"content":{"1-changing-population":{"anti-trafficking.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/anti-trafficking.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Anti trafficking\"\nparent: challenges-and-opportunities\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/anti-trafficking.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Which countries have [**the worst records**](https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/ng-interactive/2014/jun/20/countries-worst-record-human-trafficking) (comment on the distribution of these countries)?\n\n**Case Study:** Anti-trafficking policies in Vietnam: Independently explore the resources.\n\n[**Class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1jYtqYtWc_K9e6GZ5e36OS8P6jsjLrdmV6Oxb9TnpuQA/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce the issue.\na) What does [**Antislavery.org**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/10skXbTeJLZn3TnJadBh4w0nlysFXNkkk/view?usp=sharing) suggest about Vietnam's trafficking profile? \nb) How do NGOs like [**Blue Dragon**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ciXozznB16MIU-utV7bKmeLO0ObJUlij/view?usp=sharing) respond to trafficking? \nc) Summarize [**Vietnamese government's response to trafficking**](https://drive.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuueVN0RnJIOXFJSFU/view?usp=sharing) under the following headings:\ninstitutional framework\ncriminal justice response\nservices provided to victims d) Evaluate the methods used to combat trafficking for our particular case study. \n[**Extension resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/160-K9vTbsFeE5JRThFOl7oqnd2ve44HI?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary:** [**How many slaves work for you?**](http://slaveryfootprint.org/) \nExplore the connection between migration and trafficking. \nOutline the main methods of tackling the trafficking of people. \nCritique the methods used to combat trafficking.\n\n**Extension/ Extra reading:** \nThe IOM Broken Dreamers (2017) [**video by MTV.**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gH6j-QLVXv0) \nWhich [**SDG (global goals)**](http://www.globalgoals.org/) contains anti trafficking measures? Which populations are most at risk of trafficking? Why?\n**Something for at home:** Guesstimate before watching Matt Friedman:Why are there [**so many slaves**](https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/video/2017/jul/31/why-are-millions-of-people-still-trapped-in-slavery-video?CMP=Share_AndroidApp_Gmail)?\n\n{{youtube iU9TeVofkDo}}\n"},"challenges-and-opportunities.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/challenges-and-opportunities.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Challenges and opportunities\"\nparent: home\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/challenges-and-opportunities.html\n---\n\n**For the third (final) section of this unit, visit the following links:**\n\n[Population policies](https://geogjon.weebly.com/population-policies.html)\n\n[The 'demographic dividend'](https://geogjon.weebly.com/demographic-dividend.html)\n\n[Anti-trafficking policies](https://geogjon.weebly.com/anti-trafficking.html)\n\n[Gender equality policies](https://geogjon.weebly.com/gender-equality.html)\n"},"changing-population-and-places.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/changing-population-and-places.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Changing population and places\"\nparent: home\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/changing-population-and-places.html\n---\n\n**Visit the links below:**\n\n[**Demographic Transition and population structure**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/dtm.html)\n\n[**Population Pyramids**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/old-age-dependency-ratio.html)\n\n[**The old age dependency ratio**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/old-age-dependency-ratio.html)\n\n[**Megacity growth**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/megacity-growth.html)\n\n[**Forced migration/Internally displaced persons**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/forced-migration-idp.html)\n"},"china.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/china.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"China\"\nparent: economic-development\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/china.html\n---\n\n**Why is China's population unevenly distributed?**\n\n**Introduction:**\nWith a population of 1.37 billion people (set 2016; source CIA World Facebook), China remains the world's most populous country. The growth rate in 2017 was estimated by the UN to be 0.43%, significantly lower than a world average of approximately 1.11% pa. The removal of the One Child Policy in 2016 may lead to an increase in this rate of growth in future years as China changes policy to tackle its ageing population.\n\nAlthough China has the world's largest total population, it has a land area of almost 9.6 million km sq; China's population density, at 141 people per square kilometer means that it ranks 79th in the world in terms of overall population density.\n\nChina's population is unevenly spread. This is a result of both human and physical factors which will be explored below.\n\n**Task**: Define megacity. Which of China’s urban areas qualify?\n\n**Population density:**\nUse the ﻿﻿[**maps provided**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B1D3UOqNjNuuX19uYzloUlN2bU0?usp=sharing)﻿﻿ (you must print and complete the [**blank China map**](https://docs.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/document/d/1ubrHBBq9sqmxJzt-sHKTk4LAHi62NP6MHInL8zx92Q4/edit?usp=sharing) first) map to create a fully annotated (detailed labels) map which shows:\nareas with population densities of more than 250 people per square kilometer\n**megacites:** Shanghai, Beijing, Chongqing, Guizhou, Shenzhen, Chengdu, Xian, Wuhan (annotate each of these with their populations and why they are considered megacities).\nthe **Hu Huanyong line**; find out what this line represents and explain this in your annotations \nNow write a description of China's population distribution. You should refer to data, province and city names, the Hu Huanyong Line and refer clearly to north/ south/ east/ west of the country.\n\n**Physical factors: Relief and climate** \nUse the maps showing China’s relief and climate to begin to explain the variations in population density you have already identified. \nHow does relief connect to areas of low and high density? What densities are found in areas of lowland and how does this compare to mountainous areas. Remember to name the areas/provinces you refer to in your answer. Explain the connections you identify.\nHow do the patterns of precipitation and temperatures (climate) connect to variations in population density?\n**Human factors: Economic** \nWhile all areas of the country have benefited from economic growth, most growth has been focused in the east, especially in the coastal provinces. It is estimated that more than 700 million people have moved out of poverty in the last 30 years with hugely improved access to education, health care, electricity and running water. The focus of this growth remains in the east and the gap between the richest and poorest in Chinese society has grown. This has led to massive, often state sponsored migration to regional cities and from poorer to richer provinces.\n\nCoastal provinces therefore tend to be more outward looking, have much greater access to world markets, education and expertise and have benefited greatly from Foreign Direct Investment. This has enhanced the benefits these provinces already have through physical characteristics of relief, soil, rainfall and temperatures as well as their coastal locations.\nIs there a correlation between pop density and GDP?\nIdentify and describe the pattern shown. What are the overall trends? Quote data from your maps to support what you say. \nWhich provinces are anomalies? Suggest reasons.\nWhat are your overall conclusions? Explain. Support videos: ﻿[**Video 1**](https://youtu.be/bzBa947k2yY)﻿ &[**video 2**](https://youtu.be/KNXg-kYk-LU)**.**\n"},"demographic-dividend.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/demographic-dividend.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Demographic dividend\"\nparent: challenges-and-opportunities\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/demographic-dividend.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** What is meant by demographic dividend? \nWhat are the key factors which lead to a demographic dividend? \nHow does it relate to the Demographic Transition Model?\n\n{{youtube Z5Blp58zCSo}}\n\n**Task :** Use [**this document**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1hXWHAwVffmb-P_oMKcnIjjmTvRGl68d6/view?usp=sharing) to investigate the demographic dividend and how it is benefiting Rwanda. \nThink about: \nThe demographic transition model.\nEmpowerment, education & employment (a different 3E). \nFuture challenges. \n**Extra:** Read this - [\"](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qGM0soTksiazD5tdn4C-UOaN1Svr5OlZFIZNdNJeMA8/edit?usp=sharing)[**Why have four children when you can have seven**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qGM0soTksiazD5tdn4C-UOaN1Svr5OlZFIZNdNJeMA8/edit?usp=sharing)[?\"](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qGM0soTksiazD5tdn4C-UOaN1Svr5OlZFIZNdNJeMA8/edit?usp=sharing) The experience of Niger. The Guardian 2017. Based on this article, why is Niger unlikely to experience a demographic dividend?\n\n**Plenary:** \nHow can family planning lead to an economic dividend? \nThink of future challenges: how might a demographic dividend now lead to future problems? What might be the solutions?\n"},"dont-panic---how-to-end-poverty-in-15-years-2016--bbc.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/dont-panic---how-to-end-poverty-in-15-years-2016--bbc.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Don't Panic - How to End Poverty in 15 Years (2016) | BBC\"\nparent: global-patterns-of-economic-development\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/dont-panic---how-to-end-poverty-in-15-years-2016--bbc.html\n---\n\n"},"dtm.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/dtm.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"DTM\"\nparent: changing-population-and-places\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/dtm.html\n---\n\n**Starter**\na) Access [**this link**](https://www.worldometers.info/) to check out some real time data. \nb) Definitions - complete this [**task sheet.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1o2XunDbEdo1BCmAGu3A51cV2kc3mVLh0vv0RPmY86gY/edit?usp=sharing)\nc) Discuss this [**video**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WJXNuM2-y9U). What are the issues being discussed? How has this situation come about?\n\n**Task 1 - Building your own model:** In pairs use this [**video**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d9xrwtqhTmw&t=84s) to complete \nAdd the birth rate (BR), death rate (DR) and total population (TP) lines to the blank Demographic Transition Model (DTM) below (you will need to be able to draw onto it and use the video to help). \nAdd stage 5 using broken/ dotted lines. \nAnnotate (detailed labels) each stage with 2 countries. \nExplain the changes which take place (BR, DR, NI, ND & total population) and why these changes happen. \n**Blank DTM:** Clip or print out to complete. Use three different colors to match the key).\n\n![Picture](assets/dmt-blank_orig.png)\n\n**Task 2 - Understanding the model**  \nIn stage 1 of the DTM, why does the population remain low even though the BR is extremely high?\nOutline why death rates initially decline? \nWhat causes the population from stages 1-3 to increase rapidly? \nIn HIC why are birth rates often low? Describe the main changes shown by the DTM?\nWhere is your home country on this model?\nWhat are the potential problems for countries in stage 5?\nWhat might be potential solutions to low birth rates in stage 5?\nCritique (criticize) the DTM (**tip:** you might want to research to find out as much as possible).\n\nPlenary: Add these words to your glossary & define them as we use them in class (not all now!).\n\nHomework: [**Watch this to explore the Japanese Case Study.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WvISDPr8YNBR8MlfFNh581joTXVBA7vb/view?usp=sharing) **Extension: Must read articles:****BBC (November 2018):**[\"](https://www.bbc.com/news/health-46118103?fbclid=IwAR06mFgLQWWw8oEqGWtinOWUqk9DYsvthp5P-MOR08QIdSD9H-5TK4vkhPg)[**Remarkable decline in fertility rates**](https://www.bbc.com/news/health-46118103?fbclid=IwAR06mFgLQWWw8oEqGWtinOWUqk9DYsvthp5P-MOR08QIdSD9H-5TK4vkhPg)[\"](https://www.bbc.com/news/health-46118103?fbclid=IwAR06mFgLQWWw8oEqGWtinOWUqk9DYsvthp5P-MOR08QIdSD9H-5TK4vkhPg). \n**The Independent (November 2018):** '[Baby Bust': Watershed as birth rates plunge forcing more reliance on immigration](https://www.independent.co.uk/news/health/birth-rates-immigration-life-expectancy-disease-pregnancy-fertility-health-lancet-study-a8624961.html) \nThe Economist (December 2021): [Why the demographic transition is speeding up](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1z3vtYyn5dDpy1Ixz20Z6ye2Yi__-DGZH/view?usp=sharing)\n"},"economic-classifications.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/economic-classifications.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Economic classifications\"\nparent: global-patterns-of-economic-development\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/economic-classifications.html\n---\n\n![Picture](assets/economic-2_orig.png)\n"},"economic-development.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/economic-development.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Economic development\"\nparent: population-and-economic-development-patterns\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/economic-development.html\n---\n\nStarter: [**Hans**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1JaisAuU-p4J7IM9ya_Zt8qlMn5K0DlQp/view?usp=sharing) - [**Connect, extend, challenge.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_ouKqHH-aksXBZFW6_s0P3-tQt2xQ4Mg/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Task 1:** Core and Periphery\na) Draw your own diagram to represent the ideas expressed in class. Add as much detail as possible and remember to think about SCALE.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2019-08-13-at-6-57-55-am.png)\n\n[Extra support](https://youtu.be/oVGdECND9Fs)\n\nb) Check your understanding and improve your diagram [**here**](https://sites.google.com/site/chapter8development/core-periphery-theory). Include the ideas of spread and backwash effects. \nc) How might you adapt this concept while considering scale (local, national & international)? Offer examples.\n\nTask 2: Vietnam **-** Timeless Charm**.** Use the hard copy of the [**blank map of Vietnam**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/11ntlW9smOrT9RIOrCLUSw9pT4csWNoedujxoULeIsxQ/edit?usp=sharing).\na) Annotate the map with as much information as is possible to explain the reasons for the different wealth and population distribution within Vietnam using SEEP. \nb) [**Revisit the resource**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1PWOF_W61Zgao4eNqOfShUcQxLZ_Yazes?usp=sharing) and add more detail using research of your own.\n\nTask 3: Detailed example of [**South Africa**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/south-africa.html)\nUse the link to build your own detailed example of South Africa's economic development and population distribution.\n\n**Plenary:** Q&A\n"},"exploring-the-4ps.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/exploring-the-4ps.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Exploring the 4Ps\"\nparent: global-patterns-of-economic-development\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/exploring-the-4ps.html\n---\n\n**Starter:**[**Introducing the 4Ps.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1bXG_ol86IoDhlbcqdkaw0G-WTKwOwyKv/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Task 1**: Introducing the 4Ps in the media. \n[**Read your news article and then complete the fact file.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13D3I3jtxyQm7fWVMsJhRNSRWuxzWLst2B7KE5Smr3kI/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Task 2**: [**Use this document.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1kGUvo4a393sr_ef9Eg0EGQpbPQpUSHy0kX_KYK8Mqdw/edit?usp=sharing) \na) Complete the gap fill electronically \nb) Use the hard copies to complete the picture activity.\n\nExtension - Each group should now be charged with finding two images, ideally contrasting ones within a country or at different levels of economic development. You must study the images carefully and make notes privately on what you see including what the actual place is and what the processes, power and possibilities are.  However, unlike the previous activity, do not add any arrows. Print the sheets out in color and pass them to another group to complete the analysis. Each group should aim to add at least 8 evidence arrows and complete the 4P's grid on the back of each photo.\n\n**Plenary:** Test your [**understanding of the 4Ps.**](https://quizlet.com/306722117/ib-geography-concepts-flash-cards/%3C/string%3E%3C/dict%3E%3C/plist%3E)\n"},"extension.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/extension.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Extension\"\nparent: population-distribution\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/extension.html\n---\n\nPhysical Factors\n1. Relief and landforms\nLowland plains, flat river valleys and deltas and volcanic areas with fertile soil tend to have high population densities. Mountainous areas with steep slopes and poor quality soil tend to have low population densities.\n\n2. Weather and climate\nTemperate areas which experience few extremes of weather and climate tend to be more attracted than areas which experience extremes. Areas which are very dry, very cold or very wet tend to have sparse populations whereas areas which have a moderate climate with evenly distributed rainfall or with monsoon type climates have denser populations.\n\n3. Soil type and quality \nAreas which have rich, fertile soils allowing successful agriculture tend to have higher population densities than areas which have poor quality soils have sparse populations. Good quality soils may be found in low lying areas such as river flood plains and deltas where silt is deposited; in volcanic areas; in areas which have a high natural humus content. Poor quality soils may be found in areas with steep slopes; areas with very high rainfall throughout the year which tends to leach nutrients for the soil; cold areas of permafrost; areas experiencing soil degradation through human management e.g. over-grazing/deforestation.\n\n4. Water supply\nWater supply is essential for human survival and development and because of this areas which have sufficient water but to too much tend to have denser populations than areas which are dry or suffer from regular drought or areas which have excessive rainfall or which may be prone to flooding.\n\n5. Vegetation\nSome types of vegetation make the development of settlement more likely, e.g. grasslands. Areas with particularly dense rainforest, coniferous forests or those with little vegetation tend to have sparse populations.\n\n6. Raw materials/natural resources\nAreas with a wealth of natural resources such as oil, coal or minerals may have higher population densities than areas which do not. It is important to remember though that natural resources may be found in otherwise harsh environments and that they may be traded and exported/used  in areas other than where they are extracted.\n\n7. Natural threats\nThese may affect population density as people may try to avoid areas where pests, threatening animals and diseases are particular risks.\n\nHuman Factors\n1. Agriculture \nAreas with well-developed farming of crops or animals are often densely populated.\n\n2. Secondary industry\nThose areas in which manufacturing has developed tend to be densely populated. It is worth noting that even in old industrial areas in which manufacturing has declined or even closed, population densities may remain high.\n\n3. Accessibility\nAreas with well-developed transport infrastructure and links through road, rail, shipping, canals and air are likely to be more densely populated than areas which are poorly connected\n\n4. Political decisions\nGovernment policy can have a significant impact upon population densities. This can occur if governments decide to open up previously underdeveloped areas (e.g. Brasilia and the development of the Trans-Amazonian Highway in Brazil; the movement of Han Chinese to Tibet; the development of Abuja as a new capital city in Nigeria). If governments decide not to invest in an area it may also lose large numbers of people leading to reduced population density.\n\n5. Conflict\nWars and conflicts can lead to significant movements of population and a simultaneous decrease in density in some areas while others may increase.\n\nActivities: \nFor each of the countries/areas listed below, identify its population density and use the information above to explain why it is densely or sparsely populated.\nJava\nPapua\nEastern China\nTibet\nNetherlands\nCalifornia\nSiberia\nBangladesh\nSiberia\nThe Sahel\nStudy the world maps showing population density/distribution and comment on the effects of a) latitude b) altitude. Explain the patterns you identify.\n"},"forced-migration-idp.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/forced-migration-idp.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Forced Migration: IDP\"\nparent: changing-population-and-places\norder: 50\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/forced-migration-idp.html\n---\n\n[**The class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1y0eK4eCkrvkco9-s1ffXtYUVE0uZWmUJVu9UCycB13s/edit?usp=sharing) if you want to review it.\n\n**Starter:** Video activity: With a partner, watch [**one of the two 5-6 minute videos**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/11ShTH0qO5aeGmlkbLDQRe7ljaoVwvkBT?usp=sharing) each. \nShare what you learned between you.\n\n**Your next challenge:**\n\n[**Watch this**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-_6QPp9niPxOaiLvco3lW1sRUdSOAy_L/view?usp=sharing) on your phone independently.\n\nMuch was made of refugees having mobile phones. \nWhat thoughts does this video clip provoke?\n\n**A group challenge …**\nPlay the role of journalists to gather specific information on the Syria crisis as a case study of forced migration. \n[**Using this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1MYd13l0dg0uRbTPDCW0b5LXHsBM0R894?usp=sharing) - collaborate in groups of no more than three to investigate and record:\nWhy have people been forced to leave Syria in such high numbers over the last 10 years?\nWhat has been the impact on Syria, the country of origin?\nWhat has been the impact on Syria’s neighbouring countries and on countries in Europe?\n\n**Plenary:** Check that you can respond to the following: \nWhere is Syria? Can you locate it on a map?\nWhat is Syria's capital city? \nWhat is the UNHCR and what is its role?\nExplain a (political) **push** factor.\nExplain a **pull** factor. \n**Extension HW (optional)** - watch the 2019 award-winning documentary, [**\"For Sama\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/19aOsbFh2A3usY723G_MUEOq10IPChrLI/view?usp=sharing), made by a 26 year old Syrian woman, Waad al-Kateab, for her young daughter, over five years in the Syrian city, Aleppo. **Be warned/health warning - it is contains scenes that you might find disturbing. You might want to watch the trailer on** [**the documentary's webpage**](https://www.forsamafilm.com/) **before you watch it.**\n"},"gender-equality.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/gender-equality.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Gender Equality\"\nparent: challenges-and-opportunities\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/gender-equality.html\n---\n\nWatch the video below to:\na) Summarize the causes of gender inequality\nb) Ways in which gender gap can be reduced.\nc) Check out this [**Global Gender Gap Report '22**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1H55k1tZZMvBHx-UN5hqvaW4Rs5QTM4Xu/view?usp=sharing) to find out current trends in gender equality (rankings, key findings & country overviews).\n\n{{youtube 4viXOGvvu0Y}}\n\n**Today, we will revisit Rwanda, a country that has made real progress towards gender equality via the government's policies:****1.** [**Video:**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1mnKFmGMiEZrnRqOfyMOQIfmdPH8f8j_U/view?usp=sharing)The World Economic Forum on Rwanda's progress towards gender equality.\n**2.**[**Article:**](https://www.nationalgeographic.com/culture/2019/10/how-women-are-remaking-rwanda-feature/) How women are stepping up to remake Rwanda (National Geographic October 2019)\n**3.**[**Report:**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1iBMaws8IL-cRxsH2xuBJ7QXGOAIykQEY/view?usp=sharing) The United Nations Development Programme. \n**4.**[**Video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/19IDyTV95gjzS8kfFAYSSvV039Jb2sEdL/view?usp=sharing) on how gender equality in Rwanda works in practice.\n\n![Picture](assets/rwanda_orig.jpg)\n"},"global-patterns-of-economic-development.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/global-patterns-of-economic-development.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global patterns of economic development\"\nparent: population-and-economic-development-patterns\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-patterns-of-economic-development.html\n---\n\nStarter: [**Watch Hans**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Sm5xF-UYgdg) and play along. Are you smarter than the monkeys? What are the \"takeaways\" from the video? Share them as a group.\n\nTask 1: HIC, LICs & MICs based on GNI.\n\n![Picture](assets/global-gin_orig.png)\n\na) Unpick the map title by using the key and your existing knowledge.\nb) What do the various abbreviations mean? How is **GNI** calculated?\nc) Describe the global pattern of **economic development.** Is there a pattern you can identify? \nd) Write a paragraph describing the spatial distribution: your paragraph should reference each continent. Remember to identify and explain **anomalies/ outliers** as well as overall patterns and trends. Name countries and use specifics in your description.\ne) Read the infographics/info on this article, [**\"Ranked - the richest countries in the world\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1SGLoW5ko4UzaQVgPdlfUXns7QKRTRvx1/view?usp=sharing) (visualcapitalist.com May 2019) and add more to your notes.\n\nTask 2: **Dollar Street explained**\n\n{{youtube zyJpJLRTO4E}}\n\nUsing [gapminder.org](https://www.gapminder.org/dollar-street) and [PewGlobal](http://www.pewglobal.org/interactives/global-population-by-income/) \n1. In groups of four, create a table/ grid and collaborate to represent each of the levels of development. Look to contrast people from different continents living at similar levels of development. \nLow income countries (LICs)\nMiddle income countries (MICs)\nHigh income countries (HICs)\n2. For each group, use Dollar Street to investigate and describe each of the following:\nThe range of incomes at similar levels of development,\nQuality of life for people living in the country (to do this you will need to \"visit\" several families and examine their lives),\nProfession - what do people do for a living in different parts of the world, \nOther interesting facts: Possessions, how people cook, levels of education, diet etc.)\n3. How would you categorize your own home country? To what extent do you recognize the characteristics/agree with the classification? Justify your answer in some detail.\n\nPlenary: ﻿[Watch this](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1C4yzXacR4-nhp2mEUkamwDxd9lTOP1MU/view?usp=sharing)﻿ (why some countries are poor and others rich). Discuss whether or not the video supports your findings so far. If so, how?\n"},"home.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/home.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Changing Population\"\norder: 0\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/index.html\n---\n\n**GEOGRAPHY at ISHCMC**\n\nThe Diploma Programme geography course integrates both physical and human geography, and ensures that students acquire elements of both scientific and socio-economic methodologies.\n\nThe course examines key global issues, such as poverty, sustainability and climate change. It considers examples and detailed case studies at a variety of scales, from local to regional, national and international.\n\nThis site has been created for the students at ISHCMC in grades 11 and 12. Thanks to the contemporary nature of the course, it is updated almost every week!\n\nDownload the full IB DP guide for geography [**here**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/11TNMozXX2lPmJ3xCKtSzLrnnCPpMbS-r/view?usp=sharing).\n\n![Picture](assets/hodder-2_1_orig.jpg)\n"},"megacity-growth.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/megacity-growth.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Megacity Growth\"\nparent: changing-population-and-places\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/megacity-growth.html\n---\n\n**Prior knowledge and understanding:** \nHow do we define a \"megacity\"?\nCan you name five or more megacities today?\n\n**Introducing megacities**[**Article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1P2jXf-OshsEeUfp2Qr4u-FDq--I0Eg35/view?usp=sharing) - use this to create some notes on the consequences of megacities worldwide.\n[**Website**](http://www.visualcapitalist.com/animated-map-worlds-populous-cities-2100/) - an animated map of the world's 20 most populated megacities, 2010-2100. Check it out as an extra!\n[**Class presentation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rX3x-NqUVNpuXdJivQl6moq5uUr4E3Pd/view?usp=sharing) and a [**4 minute intro video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1PuEA36S7n2grr_oEqM078zNgnlVT7Q1z/view?usp=sharing) for you to review anytime.\n\n{{youtube _jnMivEZ8gc}}\n\n**Focus on HCMC: What are the consequences of the soon-to-be megacity status on individuals and the society of our home city?**\n\n**As a group, you are going to build a detailed case study using S.E.E.P. as a framework:****Social** - Housing, cultural heritage\n**Economic** - Migration, land values, as a regional hub, FDI\n**Environmental** - Air quality, Health-related issues\n**Political** - Government planning\n\nUse [**these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1M8Tqec4FzQ5z-M5GVasIjPRm1rW7gKwz?usp=sharing) to get you started.\n\n**Homework**\nWrite your answer to the title of this Ted Talk before watching it. Think carefully about possibilities (one of our 4Ps). After the video you can revisit your answer. Were you correct? What possibilities does Parag suggest?\n\n{{youtube U7y4GlmwPLQ}}\n\n**Plenary:** \nDo you agree with Parag? Why?\n\n﻿﻿\n"},"miniature-earth.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/miniature-earth.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Miniature earth\"\nparent: global-patterns-of-economic-development\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/miniature-earth.html\n---\n\n"},"old-age-dependency-ratio.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/old-age-dependency-ratio.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Old-Age Dependency Ratio\"\nparent: changing-population-and-places\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/old-age-dependency-ratio.html\n---\n\n**Spot the difference:** **Old Age Dependency Ratio**, **ODR**, is the **ratio** of **older** dependents (people **older** than 64) to the working-**age** population (those ages 15-64). \n**Dependency Ratio,** is the ratio of dependents aged zero to 14 and over the age of 65, compared with the total population aged 15 to 64.  **NB:** Data is shown as the proportion of dependents per 100 working-**age** population.\n\n**Starter**: What are the implications of a high ODR?\n\n{{youtube iVXXJDZpjWU}}\n\n**Task 1: Global patterns and trends of the ODR.** \nDescribe the global pattern of the ODR as shown on the figure below.\nComment on the change in % population over 65 between 2010 and 2040 as shown on the map.\nExplain the changes shown in one particular country on the map below. If possible, focus on a country you have looked at previously.\n\n![Picture](assets/odr-2_orig.jpg)\n\n**Task 2:** Create a Google doc named **Japan's ageing population** and use [**this page from a textbook.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Qi32ed8W5Xkj6noNgz5EcTPsXez_f31g/view?usp=sharing) \n1. Make notes (include relevant facts and figures). \n2. Answer the following: \nIn detail, explain the advantages of an ageing population.\nIn detail, explain the disadvantages of ageing populations. \nDescribe the changes in Japan's population pyramid, from figure 1.24. \nIn your opinion, is an ageing world an advantage or a disadvantage?\n\n**Task 3:**[**Ternary graph activity.**](https://docs.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/document/d/1qUV-pO6_fc2yJg6cit2gcQEhVca53g6JTT-0JMcA0Sw/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n[triangular-grid-graph-paper.pdf](assets/triangular-grid-graph-paper.pdf)\n\n**Extension:** One to watch at home to consolidate your understanding of Japan's situation: [**\"No sex please, we're Japanese\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WvISDPr8YNBR8MlfFNh581joTXVBA7vb/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary: The connection between the ageing population problem and the immigration debate**\nRead [**this article**](https://www.theguardian.com/world/ng-interactive/2025/feb/18/europes-population-crisis-see-how-your-country-compares-visualised) (The Guardian 2025) and play with the interactive map/graphs.\nDo you think it makes higher levels of immigration inevitable in Europe?\n"},"pop-pyramids.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/pop-pyramids.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Pop pyramids\"\nparent: changing-population-and-places\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/pop-pyramids.html\n---\n\n**Population pyramids**, also called age-gender pyramids, are graphical illustrations that show the distribution of age groups in a [population](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Population) at any particular time.\n\nStart by watching this short video, [**\"from a pyramid to a spinning top\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1cdIMWBwwwO2qwZ6uCJFeosvrHp8Bo-Tf/view?usp=sharing) to understand how population pyramids work.\n\n**How to read and interpret population pyramids**\n\na) Take a look at this graphic as an overview. If you want more help step-by-step, look at [**this presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1AWOCPOL8lR8R6t3r5a0qHrc9xOLhaqqf2KbhAVxNC3g/edit?usp=sharing)**.**\n\nb) Pick a country and use [**this site**](https://www.populationpyramid.net/) to see how its population pyramid has changed over time, and projections into the future.\n\n{{youtube RLmKfXwWQtE}}\n\n**Task 1:** \na) How are the Demographic Transition Model and population pyramids connected? \nb) Draw pyramids for stages 1-5 of the DTM. Justify the shape of each pyramid by discussing the changes in:\nBR,\nDR,\nchild mortality,\ntotal population,\ngender balance and\nlife expectancy. \n**Plenary: Q&A**\n\n**Extension:**[**What is population momentum**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1fzFk1ccCPKn2iLBB2QQFRzB0TEFIfqJK/view?usp=sharing)? \nWhen you start looking at the changes in population the concept of **population momentum** is important to understand. **Think -** How is it possible for a country's birth rate to decline but the population to continue to increase?\n"},"population-and-economic-development-patterns.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/population-and-economic-development-patterns.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Population and economic development patterns\"\nparent: home\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/population-and-economic-development-patterns.html\n---\n\n**For the first section of this unit, visit the following pages:**\n\n[Population Distribution](https://geogjon.weebly.com/population-distribution.html)\n\n[Global patterns of economic development](https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-patterns-of-economic-development.html)\n\n[Economic development - focus on Viet Nam and South Africa](https://geogjon.weebly.com/economic-development.html)\n"},"population-distribution.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/population-distribution.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Population distribution\"\nparent: population-and-economic-development-patterns\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/population-distribution.html\n---\n\nStarter: [**Define and discuss scale.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13IwdwVX_-gk0ej3pw44f3oqXOIDf0LaQ/view?usp=sharing)  (Create a glossary: Add Spatially & Temporally scale)\n\nTask 1: [**Slide**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1WvNdEcu1OAL5DCKCtdsCmSnJ2YZXf0-_XUE1eDRITDI/edit?usp=sharing)\n\nTask 2: Choropleth Map of Global Population Distribution-2015\n\n![Picture](assets/dis_orig.png)\n\na) Using the map above, describe the population distribution patterns at a global scale.  (You should only use the information on the map, including quantification to answer this.)\nb) Suggest why the population of the world is distributed in such a way (discuss physical and human reasons).\n\nTask 3:  In pairs: Each explain a couple/ few of the maps in the links below and then answer the accompanying questions.\n**Tip:** Aim to make connections between the maps in your explanation. \n[**Global GDP per capita Map**](assets/GDPpercapita_Demic.jpg)[**Natural Vegetation Map of the World**](https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Mohd_Maah/publication/221915805/figure/fig16/AS:305060178350081@1449743422075/Fig-19-Global-maps-of-land-vegetation-biomes-A-fair-number-of-global-vegetation-maps.png)[**World Religion Map**](http://www.age-of-the-sage.org/faith-vs-reason-debate-2.jpg)[**Global Precipitation Map**](http://www-das.uwyo.edu/~geerts/cwx/notes/chap10/global_precip.gif)[**Democracy Around the World**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/128tzastGa69kmxkE5ryb5VRT5enAUHyZ/view?usp=sharing) a) Using map evidence from above or your own research, explain the **physical geographic factors** that contribute to global population distribution.\nb) Using map evidence from above or your own research, explain the **human geographic factors** (social, economic, political, cultural) that contribute to global population distribution.\n\nPlenary: \nHow does population vary between places?\nWhat are the physical and human factors affecting population distribution at a global scale?\nDo you believe that physical or human factors have the greatest effect on population distribution? Justify your answer with reference to examples.\n"},"population-policies.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/population-policies.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Population policies\"\nparent: challenges-and-opportunities\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/population-policies.html\n---\n\n**Tuning in:** What's going on in Vietnam? Find out by reading [**this short article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1I9yVa5aBzXRfu5uczGtYaed4uVbvbDRo/view?usp=drive_link) (January 2025).\n\n**Investigation 1: Anti-natalist policies: Focus on China**\nCollaborating in groups, investigate the causes and impacts of China's anti-natalist policies. The One Child policy began in 1979 and changed to a 'two child' policy in 2016. \nTo shape your investigation, use [**the six thinking hats**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1EVCb0IocisC93GwpxZ5Hb0co_i1atTKW/view?usp=sharing)**.** \nUse the resources [**in this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1LW3oqG6ARYBKxr4dj_Dz3BFIDgkT_3-R?usp=sharing), and of course feel free to find your own, too!\n\n**Plenary:** 3, 2, 1\n**Homework:** [**China: Unnatural Selection**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l8MVc2yCJUM&feature=youtu.be) (Al Jazeera video 25 mins)\n\n**Investigation 2: Pro-Natalist Policies**\nStart with this overview ([**Guardian video, November 2024**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/11zXf4OPU69zTr3H2VCPgS4SRwWWzSlf3/view?usp=sharing)) on why pro-natalist policies are a hot topic politically today, especially among politicians/commentators on the right. According to the latter, why is pro-natalism in many places necessary economically and politically? Note down a few examples that are memorable to you.\n\n**Research to create a case study of how Japan is managing its ageing/potentially declining population.****Make sure you cover:**\nThe implications of the population situation today (only the **most important** pros and cons)—as you explored earlier\nCurrent and future plans for managing the population - pro-natalist and managing the elderly\nA critique of these policies.  **NB:** As with all case studies you will need illustrative material/facts & figures to demonstrate your understanding.\n\n**Resources to get you started:** [**Japan's ageing population poses urgent risk to society says PM**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/18JsWjTSxw2b9WLMsGjK1SGhdOF6HzVgl/view?usp=sharing) (The Guardian January 2023)\n[**Japan under pressure to accept more immigrants**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1thQefa1AYhyyQRya4YYkHKlcwHj2unFU/view?usp=sharing) (The Guardian November 2015)\n[**Robots help Japan's ageing population**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1kP2Wrrq3m2XJOHO-qPvLwaFn7SkKuxz-/view?usp=sharing) (AFP)\n[**Crisis forces Japanese out of retirement**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1T7qntHz4VOq03sPosbujrs6tAnZNydpL/view?usp=sharing) (Video short: Al Jazeera)\n[**Finding love in Japan**](http://www.aljazeera.com/programmes/101east/2016/08/finding-love-japan-160816080654099.html) (Video long: Al Jazeera August 2016)\nCheck out this resource at home: [**Who will look after Japan’s elderly?**](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-31901943)  (BBC March 2015)\n\n![Picture](assets/singapore-will-pay-citizens-to-have-a-baby-during-the-pandemic-cnn-oct-6-2020_orig.png)\n\n**Alternative - Focus on Singapore**\nArticle (CNN October 2020) [**Singapore will pay citizens to have a baby during the pandemic**](https://edition.cnn.com/2020/10/06/intl_business/singapore-childbirth-pandemic-bonus-scli-intl/index.html)[**Population**](https://youtu.be/Tf6GiqoUM7o) [policies in Singapore](https://youtu.be/Tf6GiqoUM7o) (3 mins overview) \n[**Slideshare**](https://www.slideshare.net/SitiMutiahAliUmar/population-policy-in-singapore) [on Singapore's population policies](https://www.slideshare.net/SitiMutiahAliUmar/population-policy-in-singapore)[BBC Bitesize on Singapore's population policies](http://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/ks3/geography/places/contrasts_within_continent/revision/6/)[**Mentos national night**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8jxU89x78ac&feature=youtu.be)(3 min video)\n"},"south-africa.md":{"path":"content/1-changing-population/south-africa.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"South Africa\"\nparent: economic-development\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/south-africa.html\n---\n\n**Why is South Africa's population unevenly distributed?**\n\n**Introduction:** \nThe fossil record indicates humans have inhabited South Africa since prehistoric times, and during the modern era the region was settled by Khoisan and Bantu peoples. Dutch traders landed at the southern tip of present-day South Africa in 1652 and established a stopover point on the spice route between the Netherlands and the Far East, founding the city of Cape Town.\n\nThe current population of South Africa is 58,135,722 as of Sunday, August 11, 2019, based on the latest United Nations estimates. South Africa population is equivalent to 0.75% of the [total world population](https://www.worldometers.info/world-population/). The population density in South Africa is 48 per Km2. This is a result of both human and physical factors which will be explored below.\n\n**Task:** Define  [**circular migration**](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circular_migration). Give examples of jobs that circular migrants do (domestically not Internationally).\n\n**Population density:**\nOn your blank copy of the map of South Africa, use the ﻿﻿[**maps provided**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1fsCUflRSWQx8dRDsk9TkKbylkaxSNokg?usp=sharing)﻿﻿ to create a fully annotated (detailed labels) map which shows:\nlarge urban areas: Cape Town, Port Elizabeth, Durban, Johannesburg and Bloemfontein (annotate each of these with their populations and why they are considered large urban areas).\nthe movement of people forced to migrate, homelands and influx control.   Now write a description of South Africa's population distribution. You should refer to data, province and city names and refer clearly to north/south/east/west of the country.\n\n**Human factors: Apartheid (political/ economic factors)**\nApartheid, (Afrikaans: “apartness”) policy that governed relations between [South Africa](https://www.britannica.com/place/South-Africa)’s white minority and nonwhite majority and sanctioned [racial segregation](https://www.britannica.com/topic/racial-segregation) and political and economic [discrimination](https://www.britannica.com/science/discrimination-psychology) against nonwhites. The implementation of [apartheid](https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/apartheid), often called “separate development” since the 1960s, was made possible through the [Population Registration Act](https://www.britannica.com/topic/Population-Registration-Act) of 1950, which classified all South Africans as either [Bantu](https://www.britannica.com/topic/Bantu-peoples) (all black Africans), \"coloured\" (those of mixed race), or white. A fourth category—Asian (Indian and Pakistani)—was later added.\n\na) Read and take notes on the [**impact of Apartheid**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1codYpZjYWoqVeC6rPO8ZTPOhMN_IJnTe/view?usp=sharing)**.** \nb) Using the map, describe the movement of people. Use the key and place names throughout your description. \nc) Discuss what is meant by [**\"influx control\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1yNWq0lPkFhYiwJ7ZUkGydQCrV7BhK0NE/view?usp=sharing) and link this to the idea of core/ periphery within South Africa.\nd) Give two examples of reasons for each type of migration below (tip: think Apartheid). \nforced migration\neconomic migration &\nvoluntary migration Extra Support: [**Video 1**](https://youtu.be/ec8IiTUX-lA)\n"}},"2-global-climate":{"atmospheric-system.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/atmospheric-system.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Atmospheric System\"\nparent: causes-of-global-climate-change\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/atmospheric-system.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Watch [**this 10 minute video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/19Ph1s2_ZLuj7ewYbXtorLsvT3oJkt4wU/view?usp=sharing) to tune into how the atmosphere works.\n**Extension:** If you need more information [**use this video.**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bKoFqXoLV0Y)\n\n**Task:** [**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/presentation/d/1konRJB4H0OyzlN481sHR38wOZLrmJ7NzCQbps4PnJRc/edit?usp=sharing)\nUnderstanding the atmosphere as a system is important to understanding the atmosphere and the causes of climate change. \na) Use this link to describe the atmospheric system in terms of [**energy balance**](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth%27s_energy_budget).\nb) Discuss solar radiation - long wave and short wave radiation.\nc) Discuss the role of clouds and gas [**(in both inputs (insolation) and outputs (re-radiation)**](https://d32ogoqmya1dw8.cloudfront.net/images/eslabs/weather/balance_diagram_simple_456.jpg)**.**\nd) What are the greenhouse gases and how do these impact the system (warm our atmosphere)?\n\n**Plenary:** Complete the necessary [**definitions in your glossary.**](https://docs.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/document/d/1FnaPqIdG7BxLCuvv2Mp4sNiiUM25XexCTbRwQEmDt_Q/edit?usp=sharing)\n"},"causes-of-global-climate-change.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/causes-of-global-climate-change.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Causes of global climate change\"\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/causes-of-global-climate-change.html\n---\n\nThis opening third of the unit has three parts:\n\n[**The Atmospheric System**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/atmospheric-system.html)[**Feedback loops and the albedo effect**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/feedback-loops--albedo.html)[**Greenhouse gases and their variations**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-greenhouse-effect--its-variations.html)\n"},"civil-societycorporate-strategies.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/civil-societycorporate-strategies.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Civil Society/Corporate strategies\"\nparent: responding-to-global-climate-change\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/civil-societycorporate-strategies.html\n---\n\n**What is a civil society?** \nAny organisation or movement that works in the area between the household, the private sector and the state to negotiate matters of public concern. Civil societies include non-governmental organisations (NGOs), community groups, trade unions, academic institutions and faith-based organisations.\n\n{{youtube iENQCuusv28}}\n\n**Greenpeace****is an international civil society.**\nVisit [**Greenpeace UK**](https://www.greenpeace.org.uk/about-greenpeace/how-we-create-change/)to find out how this organization uses different ways to influence the climate change debate.\nFind out about its \"Climate Justice\" campaign [**here**](https://www.greenpeace.org/international/story/19885/what-does-climate-change-have-to-do-with-human-rights/). What is its focus and why?\n\n**A debate in pairs**: Arguments [**for**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1hgA-Dq9fS1OFBzeltYPPe5rkfdMZ3-oy/view?usp=sharing) and [**against**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FSVdKR0VpIz8yEAx6FtRzz3Pugcu25XG/view?usp=sharing) civil societies.\n\n**Your own investigation @home: Focus on the role of combatting the effects of climate change in Bangladesh**\nUse [**this link**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Tr9Vu8eiKXCQKidoi5gaVEe6bUNWdxkn?usp=sharing) (and other sources) to investigate and write up a short case study of the response to climate change by the actions of \"non-government stakeholders\" in Bangladesh.  Divide up your notes to highlight different sectors - agriculture, fresh water, education and health.\n\n**Corporate responses to climate change** \nRead [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1X9aqrSPR7aleXZJvPKs31HDqOuy-184E/view?usp=sharing) about Exxon-Mobil's response to climate change - can you explain the corporation's position? \n**90 global corporations are responsible for two-thirds of global climate emissions. Meanwhile, some corporations are taking action to mitigate against climate change.**\n\n**Tesla** have been active in responding to climate change via their \"Gigafactories\". Read an article about them [**here**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1losGZZTpKIGLgjSNHNKx9XiMBVPvl0Vp/view?usp=sharing), and here's Elon Musk's meeting Leonardo DiCaprio in \"Before the Flood\" talking about them:\n\n{{youtube iZm_NohNm6I}}\n\n**IKEA** \nWhat does this corporation do to combat climate change? [**Read this page**](https://www.ikea.com/ms/en_JP/about_ikea/our_responsibility/climate_change/index.html) on their website.\n"},"climate-change-and-agriculture.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/climate-change-and-agriculture.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Climate change and agriculture\"\nparent: consequences-of-global-climate-change\norder: 50\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/climate-change-and-agriculture.html\n---\n\n[**Inquiry questions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1W4ZLLN4AGJFbwFy0HH4XsQNh-FYjHYYiJCBhgdvPC9g/edit?usp=sharing) **to guide your investigation.**\n\nWatch this short video to create a mind map of the key ways in which climate change affects agriculture. You can use the links/resources below to add more information on the different issues.\n\n{{youtube G0K9sD0vGus}}\n\nAdd to your mind map by using the information on [**these two infographics**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KqvGGTZlG3i2pEsmd7XLysHFHPig0ZoLRlGF0LfG3bA/edit?usp=sharing) that predict the consequences of climate change on agriculture by 2030 and by 2050.\n\nTake notes (and add them to your mind map) from these two articles to explain why agriculture in some places might **benefit** from climate change:  [**Article 1**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1bw1__5oVFx_JTVgsLwOWL7vMQPr8At5u/view?usp=sharing). [**Article 2**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1d8zCu7p4SSUdnxggsTFWhcJbJ00jW1Zr/view?usp=sharing).\n\n**How climate change affects farmers (and threatens food security) in poorer places**\nRead [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1zmfRZ7YfBrBiPw0iuPkNI54McUHk9fdM/view?usp=sharing) to explain why the poorest farmers suffer most. \n**Desertification** is a growing environmental problem in **semi-arid** areas like the **Sahel**, a belt of land across Africa south of the Sahara. Watch [**this video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wcCq5llmk0AwQ7Kx-wEX7RCCK-T-KmVH/view?usp=sharing) about one country affected, **Burkina Faso**, and how desertification is linked to both climate change and farming practices.\n\n**How climate change affects farmers in richer places (like the USA)**\nUse [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1u2jcnf5SpVw9yUrCzRAih3WhimGiDqBC/view?usp=sharing) to explain the uneven consequences of climate change on agriculture across the USA\n\n**Why soil erosion matters for climate change**\nWatch [**this video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wpBzwPk-lvh2y5RHbNe5rpgxleFvLtXr/view?usp=sharing) and record why soil is so important in terms of climate change thanks to its role in the carbon cycle as the largest terrestrial **carbon sink**. \nUse [**this video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13WCFXeMuvJTNNWSYc2DcnWUK4ZMnF5xE/view?usp=sharing) to explain why soil degradation and erosion is such a big issue today, and therefore indirectly linked to climate change as a result.\n"},"climate-change-and-the-atmosphere.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/climate-change-and-the-atmosphere.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Climate change and the atmosphere\"\nparent: consequences-of-global-climate-change\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/climate-change-and-the-atmosphere.html\n---\n\n[**Inquiry questions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1W4ZLLN4AGJFbwFy0HH4XsQNh-FYjHYYiJCBhgdvPC9g/edit?usp=sharing) **to guide your investigation.**\n\n**Starters:** \na) Watch the clip below from Jimmy Kimmel featuring Sarah Palin in 2016. \nAre you ready to present Sarah with counter-arguments?\n\n{{youtube 9UCdFbyL8y0}}\n\nb) Comment on the incidence (frequency) and severity (measures of strength) of extreme weather events by using [**this interactive page**](https://www.theguardian.com/environment/ng-interactive/2018/dec/21/deadly-weather-the-human-cost-of-2018s-climate-disasters-visual-guide) based on \"deadly weather\" events in 2018.\n\n**Are big storms or even hurricanes/cyclones/typhoons more likely as a consequence of climate change? YOU evaluate the evidence!**\n1. Read [**this article**](https://www.nationalgeographic.com/news/2015/1/150116-2014-hottest-year-global-warming-climate-science/) from National Geographic (2015) - what is happening to ocean temperatures? And so what?\n2. Watch [**this clip**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LslSdolex7DagxMJkc2vd2AGAV-uVJWa/view?usp=sharing) of David Attenborough from some years ago. What is his view presented here?\n2. Watch the BBC Newsnight clip (2017) below.\n\n{{youtube wsSxopt_Kn8}}\n\n3. What counter-arguments does the meteorologist present in [**this Fox News clip**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1bA0cjyoSSf09_sIqBaopSzvkUZBoClqG/view?usp=sharing) (2018)?\n\n**How is climate change linked to increasing drought risk?** \na) Explore [**drought risk**](http://www.eldoradocountyweather.com/climate/world-maps/world-drought-risk.html) and the potential knock-on effects but be sure to note down any variation of impacts according to economic development (LIC, MIC & HIC). \nb) Explore the following: [**drought linked to deforestation**](https://youtu.be/6rQmG-koEPI), [**impacts on people**](https://youtu.be/bm3CFAlB0fA) & [**drought case study in the Sahel**](https://youtu.be/c-MYQYKQXhI)**.**\n\n**Tying it all together**\nUse [**this website**](https://nca2014.globalchange.gov/highlights/report-findings/extreme-weather#intro-section-2), a US government-sponsored site prior to 2016, to add more substance to your findings about the potential consequences of climate change on the atmosphere/extreme weather.\n"},"climate-change-and-the-biosphere.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/climate-change-and-the-biosphere.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Climate change and the biosphere\"\nparent: consequences-of-global-climate-change\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/climate-change-and-the-biosphere.html\n---\n\n[**Inquiry questions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1W4ZLLN4AGJFbwFy0HH4XsQNh-FYjHYYiJCBhgdvPC9g/edit?usp=sharing) **to guide your investigation.**\n\n**Starter:** Think **scale** - What is the consequences of climate change on biodiversity (start on 6 minutes)?\n\n{{youtube XFmovUAWQUQ}}\n\n**a)  Impact of climate change on biodiversity**\nUse [**this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B1D3UOqNjNuuelFsLVFndnZPaTQ?usp=sharing)to find out more about the impact of climate change on biodiversity.\n\n**b) Impact of climate change on habitats and ecosystems:** \nRead[**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Ra9v7YgqWsgFS0FnZ6bbCu4A2npWSCFE/view?usp=sharing) to record the impact of California's forest fires (November 2018).\n\nUse these resources and your own research to discuss changes to specific habitats brought about by climate change. \n[**Map of**](https://ec.europa.eu/clima/policies/adaptation/how/territorial_en)[**Europe**](https://ec.europa.eu/clima/policies/adaptation/how/territorial_en)[**Guardian article**](https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2014/mar/31/ipcc-climate-report-wildlife-impact)\n\n**c)  The impact of climate change on specific species:**\nUse the resources and your own research to discuss the impact on a) species in general and b) on polar bears. \n[**Polar bear Guardian article**](https://www.theguardian.com/science/2015/jan/17/polar-bears-arctic-sea-ice)[**Polar bear video**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kv9v9ALV3yk)\n\n**d)  The impact of climate change on animal migration patterns:**\nDescribe the movements on the map and try to use prior knowledge to explain that movement in particular thinking about where the largest corridors of movement seem to be concentrated, the general direction of movement and linking in to the map showing shifting biomes. \n[**Average direction mammals, birds, and amphibians**](http://maps.tnc.org/migrations-in-motion/#4/19.00/-78.00)\n\n**Plenary:** [**Cringey but good.**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eRLJscAlk1M)\n"},"climate-change-and-the-hydrosphere.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/climate-change-and-the-hydrosphere.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Climate change and the hydrosphere\"\nparent: consequences-of-global-climate-change\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/climate-change-and-the-hydrosphere.html\n---\n\n[**Inquiry questions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1W4ZLLN4AGJFbwFy0HH4XsQNh-FYjHYYiJCBhgdvPC9g/edit?usp=sharing) **to guide your investigation.**\n\n**1. Oceans****a)** Watch this short video and explain how the oceans act as a **carbon sink.** Add this term to your glossary as well.\n\n{{youtube OoW2PlvMpZs}}\n\nb) Use these two videos to explain the impact of increased levels of carbon in oceans: \n[**Video 1**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SMWGV-DBnk&feature=youtu.be)**;** [**Video 2**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MgdlAt4CR-4&feature=youtu.be)**.** \nc)Use [**these links**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1gPEPzVxHGldX1yUHB1zRsCpyJbtnv4qQK-9u_y-reeU/edit?usp=sharing) to take notes on the consequences of rising sea levels, with a particular focus on Greenland.\nd) Check out any coastal location (including HCMC) on [**this website**](https://choices.climatecentral.org/#12/40.7116/-74.0010?compare=temperatures&carbon-end-yr=2100&scenario-a=warming-4&scenario-b=warming-2) - what are the predictions in terms of rising sea levels in increases of 2 degrees centigrade or 4 degrees centigrade scenarios? \nAnother website (National Geographic) - [**what the world would look like if all the ice melted**](https://www.nationalgeographic.com/magazine/2013/09/rising-seas-ice-melt-new-shoreline-maps/)\n\n{{youtube 8zZtSajKOig}}\n\n**2: On Land: Melting ice/ glaciers**\na) How does the melting of ice change the balance of carbon?\nWatch this video to find out:[**Researching the carbon impact of melting glaciers**](https://news.fsu.edu/news/science-technology/2015/01/20/study-melting-glaciers-big-carbon-impact/)\nb) [**How thawing permafrost could fuel climate warming**](https://graphics.reuters.com/CLIMATE-CHANGE/PERMAFROST/oakveelglvr/?fbclid=IwAR2ocV7Fj--YourgPsRpuoq1yrSuyQn0TqX5uvJYPXo4aAYG0_T2uBUMg0w) - use this interactive site (Reuters Oct 2020) to learn more.\nc) Use [**this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B1D3UOqNjNuuZEJ3aC1iRjY0dzQ?usp=sharing) to explore (and take notes!) on the consequences of melting ice, both freshwater glaciers and sea ice.\n\n{{youtube dzRvmjBGqp8}}\n\n**Plenary:** In your own words, explain:\nAcidification (of the oceans)\nCarbon sinks (and be able to name several!)\n"},"consequences-of-global-climate-change.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/consequences-of-global-climate-change.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Consequences of global climate change\"\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/consequences-of-global-climate-change.html\n---\n\n**Visit:**[The impacts of climate change on people and places](https://geogjon.weebly.com/impacts-of-climate-change-on-people-and-places.html)[Climate Change and the hydrosphere](https://geogjon.weebly.com/climate-change-and-the-hydrosphere.html)[Climate Change and the atmosphere](https://geogjon.weebly.com/climate-change-and-the-atmosphere.html)[Climate Change and the biosphere](https://geogjon.weebly.com/climate-change-and-the-biosphere.html)[Climate Change and agriculture](https://geogjon.weebly.com/climate-change-and-agriculture.html)[Forced migration - focus on Tuvalu, South Pacific](https://geogjon.weebly.com/forced-migration-environmental-refugees.html)\n\n**Add your mind maps that connect the different issues** [**in this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Bny7btUHdsR_7To9gquQ9qJjCw0N7CSZ?usp=sharing)**.**\n"},"cop-26-saigon.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/cop-26-saigon.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"COP 26 Saigon\"\nparent: responding-to-global-climate-change\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/cop-26-saigon.html\n---\n\n![Picture](assets/cop-ishcmc-logo.png)\n\n**Due to COVID-19 the scheduled COP 26 conference to be held in Glasgow, UK has been postponed until December 2021. In the meantime, COP 26 will take place in Ho Chi Minh City, hosted at ISHCMC.**\n\nYou will attend COP 26 as a member of one of the delegations listed below. As a delegation, read the information provided and research further information to support your position at the conference. Real data needs to be presented as part of your argument. During the conference one member of your group will scribe for the delegation to keep track of what is being said.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2020-11-29-at-4-22-59-pm.png)\n\n[**Australia**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1-6Uhixe95DWUE_fexvVw9wLjXRAVL7uY?usp=sharing)[**Brazil**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1qBGlreQedmaQEulvO0a0KS9jLKZVJjnr?usp=sharing)[**China**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1aS5z3OCzJJjbYyUeoWsG2qRuGE3s3vq1?usp=sharing)[**The EU**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1b2KTQb41Qbs5g9Wr4iKkrEuAD0cRj-66?usp=sharing)[**India**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1RxmfrvZTZqA0diHJui_Ggc0RPNgaFs_f?usp=sharing)[**Japan**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1NEWsRCzFSKVNqF13wQX7EF7KVmCsp8bK?usp=sharing)[**Kenya**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Qx5nwbiZHy41TgHM5R1BGf6IKn2TD7Ti?usp=sharing) [**Saudi Arabia**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1acpPy5Hv1Xf2rrIOriYpbGg9CF7MBFO_?usp=sharing)[**Small Island States**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1AzjQ_p_2GHCUElmPlXSVZYLtZLaOYkpJ?usp=sharing)[**The USA**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1h9QgSuEFateOPUVgiamo1bbE2pV61pgO?usp=sharing)[**Viet Nam**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1pENQHK5iY60M6f7dPlDh8W_MRqKsGkHw?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary:** [**Exit Ticket.**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1u0oNVCaP-Ev2Wi-lntTMpawfkFbekBOtCnRdmfeLMzU/edit?usp=sharing)\n"},"feedback-loops--albedo.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/feedback-loops--albedo.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Feedback loops & albedo\"\nparent: causes-of-global-climate-change\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/feedback-loops--albedo.html\n---\n\n**Refresher 1:** Complete this 'cloze' exercise. What new ideas are within the text? Can you explain them? \nThe atmosphere is an \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ system, receiving radiation from both the sun and the \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_. The energy of the earth is very \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ but it does have an effect in particular in urban climates. Incoming solar radiation is referred to as \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\n\nThe main energy that drives all of our weather systems and our climate comes from the sun. The vast majority of this energy is absorbed at the \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ whereas energy is generally lost in the \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ regions.  However, this energy is \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ to higher latitudes  by wind and air \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\n\n**Choose from this key word list:** circulations;    open;    insolation;    earth;    equator;    redistributed;    polar;    small\n\nStart by reviewing [**the class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1pHd6Wj50MJM82UMRXXxfoo7UG1OjtaZaU4cJ31XXkxM/edit?usp=sharing) that includes definitions of **albedo**, **feedback loops** and **turning points**. \na) What is [**albedo**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuuZXVneGtZWEVfRTg/view)? Why is it important? \nb) Discuss what [**this table**](https://docs.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/document/d/17wxZ1OcVz9ro6uNjS_izxJdQQk4VXCa0RmdiPvYNZqs/edit?usp=sharing) illustrates and explain changes with relation to the different surfaces. \nc) How might seasons impact albedo? \nd) What is [**methane**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ko4cUnzoPic&feature=youtu.be) and where does it come from? Why is it significant? \ne) [**How is the melting ice (due to global warming) and methane related**](https://drive.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuuZlZsUHNBTUJfTWc/view?usp=sharing)? What is the significance of this relationship?\nf) HOMEWORK visit [**this site on feedback loops**](https://feedbackloopsclimate.com/?fbclid=IwAR2fPdTkc_XoeN56aJmirGpCEsfEQBL5lSHgfyVwkGi9AqsRjDnaVO4hrUw) (2020). Watch the introduction video, and at least one of the videos on permafrost and albedo.\n\n**Turning points?** \nRead at least two of these recent articles that suggest we are nearer 'turning points' than previously thought: \n[**Risks of 'domino effect' of tipping points greater than thought**](https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2018/dec/20/risks-of-domino-effect-of-tipping-points-greater-than-thought-study-says) **(The Guardian Dec 2018)**[**Scientists warn nine climate tipping points now ‘active’**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/10ZkA5_nKdyWpeBEF97sPLKYcJMlMIGzV/view?usp=sharing) [(University of Exeter Nov 2019)](https://drive.google.com/file/d/10ZkA5_nKdyWpeBEF97sPLKYcJMlMIGzV/view?usp=sharing)[**Climate emergency - world may have crossed tipping points**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13TvIAvn8DhPRG1ZRT2SGYr_pFyk0qXRm/view?usp=sharing) (The Guardian Nov 2019)\n\n**Consolidation video animation**\nAn absolute must to summarise your learning on feedback loops and turning points:\n[**Wake up, freak out ... then get a grip**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vWashntCOyoIALfjim-71X5Xt_cJKRK0/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Extension:** [**Exam style question**](https://docs.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/document/d/14DyNAlRPtuf7Y-DZGG46PKqFPk5-aARQNl6PBjiSdxQ/edit?usp=sharing)\n"},"forced-migration-environmental-refugees.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/forced-migration-environmental-refugees.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Forced Migration: Environmental Refugees\"\nparent: consequences-of-global-climate-change\norder: 60\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/forced-migration-environmental-refugees.html\n---\n\n**Discuss:** How does the video below connect with what you already know about megacities and population distribution?\n**TOK**: What's in a name? How does the language related to migration influence the way some migrants are perceived by certain groups?\n\n{{youtube OSpDsP58udM}}\n\n**Why is the plight of environmental** [**refugees difficult when compared to other refugees**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DzOs8MNkS7XhyIq9NHBC9-MhU9QWoHuj/view?usp=sharing)**?** Read [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1umYhTfB3ZTj225Nie9K1ywFDyiB-aWs7/view?usp=sharing) to find out.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2020-10-24-at-12-08-37-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Focus on Tuvalu, South Pacific**\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2020-10-24-at-12-09-44-pm.png)\n\nUse [**these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Laql92TLXXxvPsbIIj4g6baTyk_SzPPM?usp=sharing) to create your own case study of (environmental) forced migration.\n[**Latest article/video on the plight of Tuvalu**](https://www.channelnewsasia.com/sustainability/tuvalu-pacific-islands-migration-mass-displacement-climate-change-4437421?fbclid=IwY2xjawES5y9leHRuA2FlbQIxMQABHcTbSkdd_gz5o0HFih8aMUunXS3xWH1zCad5SlrPfXWqqZ5fL1Q1SbJA2w_aem_HBALLGUt4raJPez5iMxVrw) - Channel Asia (July 2024)\nExtra: [**Climate adaptation measures in Tuvalu**](https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/jun/20/tuvalu-climate-crisis-australia-visa-ballot) - a longer read (The Guardian June 2025)\n**You can use this case study in both this unit and the 'Population Change' one!** \nCauses (SEEP)\nConsequences (SEEP) \nThe responses of other countries - Australia, China, etc.\n\n**Extension**: [**Bangladesh**](https://www.climatechangenews.com/2017/08/14/will-become-bangladeshs-climate-migrants/)(a superb website that focuses on environmental refugees here - more on Bangladesh soon!)\n\n**Plenary:** [**Complete this activity.**](https://docs.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/presentation/d/1QBLciSM1xVvagA-gZWnz_GfmewYeSM38mxaX6HuJmRE/edit?usp=sharing)\n"},"government-responses.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/government-responses.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Government responses\"\nparent: responding-to-global-climate-change\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/government-responses.html\n---\n\n**Mitigation vs Adaptation**[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1mM5xJQ9sy87qgnO4NGoJOxb7uZGnKmssNMekQhYNDTM/edit?usp=sharing)**:** Introducing **adaptation** versus **mitigation** as government-led responses to climate change risk. \n[**Activity:**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/14bB8aGPKs4dLpTYLid1hMBwM15RRIsZ1/view?usp=sharing) Download and respond to the questions to ensure you understand the difference between **adaptation** and **mitigation**.\n\n**Geo-engineering****Investigate the geo-engineering as an approach to mitigating against climate change using** [**this folder of resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B1D3UOqNjNuuRWljdTZIeEpyN0E?resourcekey=0-jStHR88EXXjB49RbIitr7w&usp=sharing).\nTake notes, including examples, to **describe** the different types of geo-engineering, and attempt to **evaluate** them as viable investments for governments.\n\n{{youtube pA6FSy6EKrM}}\n\n**Carbon Offsetting & Trading** \nGo online to explain:\na)  [**Carbon Offsetting**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kqRHHWj4I7c) \nb)  [**Carbon Trading**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nupdNvbaRAQ)\nc)  [**Carbon Tax**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FleM9upRPuM)\n\nUse thevideo to further your understanding and develop a critique of [**both offsetting and trading**](https://storyofstuff.org/movies/story-of-cap-and-trade/).\n\n**Plenary:**\nBetween offsetting and trading ... which is the best strategy?\n\n**Global Geopolitical Efforts**\nHow does the video below suggest that international agreement on climate change mitigation is difficult?\n\n{{youtube B11kASPfYxY}}\n\n**Inquiry: The Paris Accord and beyond**\n\nAs a pair divide up these two resources to record the key agreements in the Paris deal, who signed up, and why it is so significant:\nAl Jazeera (June 2017): [**Paris climate agreement - what you need to know**](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2017/06/paris-climate-agreement-170602102754827.html)[Video (Dec 2015 - 5 mins):](https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/0/paris-agreement-climate-change-everything-need-know/) [**The Paris climate deal**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qdqPoX9IV9A)[**What does COP mean?**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pb7iW_6b1NI) **COP 23, November 2017.**[**COP 26 - explained**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HaPF8zpWaa6KSIySTIV-DGdlDowxzOg9/view?usp=sharing)\nPM of Barbados, Nov 2022: [**Speech at COP 27**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1N9GJdmAtC8sDaE-7ziXIGFwM9ukXW1t4/view?usp=sharing) (edited)\n**Articles to read:**[**\"The climate villains are certainly making their presence felt\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1TUW6jKwjH-TJwo3aL9gZoxKWpBYjoTYJ/view?usp=sharing) (The Independent, Dec 2018)\n[**\"The one chance we have\" - COVID and the climate crisis**](https://edition.cnn.com/interactive/2020/09/world/climate-covid-money-intl/) (CNN Sept 2020)\n\n**Extension thinking:**\nDo you believe that the Paris Accord on Climate Change will have a positive result on mitigating climate change?\n\nWhat do mitigation strategies have to do with the [**Global Goals/ SDGs**](https://cop23.unfccc.int/achieving-sustainable-development-goals-through-climate-action)? (include the goal, target and strategy)\n\n{{youtube huX1bmfdkyA}}\n\n**HW: A further investigation/analysis at home: Watch a 30 minute edit of**  [**\"Truth to Power\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ynR_McsVagoHjjHGAPdAyy7n2QsSyfux/view?usp=sharing) **by Al Gore (2017).** Use the video and what you have learned already to write responses to the questions displayed at the beginning of the video.\n\n[**Add these terms to your glossary:**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pb7iW_6b1NI)[Adaptation](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pb7iW_6b1NI)--a response to climate change, that seeks to reduce the vulnerability (and therefore increase resilience) of social and biological systems to relatively sudden change. These actions focus on the EFFECTS of climate change.\n\n[Mitigation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_change_mitigation)--consists of actions to limit the magnitude or rate of climate change.  Climate change mitigation generally involves reductions in human emissions of greenhouse gases (GHGs).  Mitigation may also be achieved by increasing the capacity of carbon sinks, e.g., through reforestation. These actions therefore focus on the CAUSES of climate change.\n\n[Geo-Engineering](http://www.geoengineeringwatch.org/geoengineering-definition-of-terms/)-- **aims to mitigate the impacts of climate change through** artificial modification of the Earth's climate systems. Two main methods: Solar Radiation management (SRM) and Carbon Dioxide removal (CDR)\n\n[Carbon Offsetting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_offset)-- a mitigation method to reduce emissions of carbon dioxide in order to compensate for or to offset an emission made elsewhere.\n\n[Geopolitical](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geopolitics)--the political relationships between different places.\n"},"impacts-of-climate-change-on-people-and-places.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/impacts-of-climate-change-on-people-and-places.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Impacts of climate change on people and places\"\nparent: consequences-of-global-climate-change\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/impacts-of-climate-change-on-people-and-places.html\n---\n\n**Starter**: Mind map the direct and indirect impacts of climate change on health as you watch the video:\n\n{{youtube 6JgE0aTEFpQ}}\n\n![Picture](assets/climate-change-and-health-diagram_orig.png)\n\n**Part 1. Focus on health**\n\na) Add to your mind map with some detail from [**this WHO fact sheet**](https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/climate-change-and-health) and the graphic opposite.\n\nb) Watch [**this VICE video (2019)**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tSA5HNFBwnk&feature=emb_logo) about how the melting of the Arctic ice cap is reviving deadly germs.\n\nc) Check out [**this article (NPR 2019)**](https://www.npr.org/sections/goatsandsoda/2019/03/28/707604928/chart-where-disease-carrying-mosquitoes-will-go-in-the-future) - what does it suggest about changes in the distribution of mosquito-borne diseases like Dengue and Zika?\n\n**Part 2. Ocean Transport routes: The 'North West Passage' in the Arctic**\nUse these two resources to **evaluate** the impacts of this ocean shipping route. \nBBC article (November 2018): [**Is the Arctic set to become a major shipping route?**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FqtqYxtjuloMFcgZKK08N2oTalkky__x/view?usp=sharing) \nVideo (April 2017): [**Canada's new shipping shortcut**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZcDwtO4RWmo&feature=youtu.be)\n\n![Picture](assets/northwest-passage_orig.jpg)\n"},"responding-to-global-climate-change.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/responding-to-global-climate-change.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Responding to global climate change\"\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/responding-to-global-climate-change.html\n---\n\n**Visit:** [Vulnerability and risk](https://geogjon.weebly.com/vulnerability--risk.html) [Civil society and corporate strategies to combat Climate Change](https://geogjon.weebly.com/civil-societycorporate-strategies.html) [Government responses to Climate Change](https://geogjon.weebly.com/government-responses.html)\n"},"solar-radiation-variations.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/solar-radiation-variations.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Solar Radiation Variations\"\nparent: causes-of-global-climate-change\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/solar-radiation-variations.html\n---\n\nReview [**the class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1jjMivJiuvooqQYWKRtiys8jYchPUqBGQtZFOh1aBXjQ/edit?usp=sharing) on other natural factors (\"external forcings\") that influence the global energy balance. \n[**Video short**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/16fmzFMCoKmJqakR5RXasR6B6ToPB3c9W/view?usp=sharing)**:** (The Economist): How do volcanoes change the climate?\n\n**\"Global Dimming\" (natural causes)**\n\nThe eruption of Mount Pinatubo, The Philippines in June 1991 remains the best example of how a volcanic eruption can change the global energy balance and actually cool the planet.\n\nTake some notes from [**this short presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1SANe0sHM3jL1B_Z2vJ7Hs-Rj2K9yUDhoEfmp2KVswZk/edit?usp=sharing)to explain how the eruption led to climate change via \"global dimming\".\n\nAfterwards, watch [**this video clip**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1EE25MCd86bLWcY8dJLJkUJjwuP3EXGt8/view?usp=sharing) of the eruption and its aftermath.\n\n**\"Global Dimming\" (human aka anthropogenic causes)**\nRead [**this short article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/14_4Zo2fEbmbbMRFg2v9RTYXjZwnrIGsD/view?usp=sharing) to be able to explain why human activity can lead to \"global dimming\".\n"},"the-greenhouse-effect--its-variations.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/the-greenhouse-effect--its-variations.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"The greenhouse effect & its variations\"\nparent: causes-of-global-climate-change\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-greenhouse-effect--its-variations.html\n---\n\n**Starter:**\nUse [**the class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1A4KdntdfkjFA9vUTVHhAPDZWKQl-xDKxRIjpjyRrP0A/edit?usp=sharing) to record the main sources of the different greenhouse gases from human (anthropogenic) sources.\n\n**Task 1** - **Inquiry**: Who and what is to blame?\nWhere possible, quantify your responses, discuss specific sectors, countries and economic patterns at different scales. \na)[**Scroll down**](https://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2015-whats-warming-the-world/) to see how much both natural and anthropogenic factors contribute to climate change.\nb) The [**Carbon Map**](http://www.carbonmap.org/#intro) **-** include information from the background, responsibility and vulnerability sections.\nc) Pick out some highlights from this article (Nov 2023): [**\"Revealed: The huge impact of the middle classes\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LKz_CBCEXMieCOhoi5A2sen3_0TCnGT3/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Task 2** - **Explore emissions further -** emitters (historical and projected),sectors, energy, per capita. \n[**World's greenhouse gas emissions**](https://www.wri.org/blog/2017/04/interactive-chart-explains-worlds-top-10-emitters-and-how-theyve-changed)**,** [**All the world's carbon emissions in one chart**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1sGvrnkTMPNnCMI_3w9aAoipTNP2S7D2x/view?usp=sharing) & use these [**infographics**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Gm2YDd_HzcukntUtDBIbhqGGZQVwKnln?usp=sharing) too.\n\n**Exam style Q** - Evaluate the infographics (include pros AND cons), how could these resources be improved?\n\n**Homework:** [**Make a copy of this document in your Google Drive and complete the activities.**](https://docs.google.com/a/ishcmc.edu.vn/document/d/1qPUtZqmdAOYYfTEeGw1qqfMx-xZITz62oxKiTmS-i6c/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Extra extra:** [**Is globalization worsening the effects of climate change?**](http://www.cantechletter.com/2016/06/globalization-worsening-effects-climate-change-study-says/) [**The impact of trade on climate change**](https://www.wto.org/english/tratop_e/envir_e/climate_impact_e.htm)[**Globalization and the environment**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hWKcC9fvUmE)\n"},"vulnerability--risk.md":{"path":"content/2-global-climate/vulnerability--risk.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Vulnerability & Risk\"\nparent: responding-to-global-climate-change\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/vulnerability--risk.html\n---\n\n[**Class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1p-Hhi5epUZBrMgudHjVmeHE0UB4YlcbybTebEhrTCDc/edit?usp=sharing)to introduce the concepts of vulnerability and risk\ni) Revisit the interactive [**Carbon Map**](http://www.carbonmap.org/).  Play around with different shades like population growth and GDP per person.  While using different shades, distort the map to account for **various vulnerabilities.**\nii) Describe two patterns of vulnerability that you discovered from the maps.  Include snipped images of the maps to help your explanation.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2020-11-12-at-6-26-45-pm_orig.png)\n\n**In-class investigation: Why is Vietnam one of the countries most at risk from climate change?**\n1. The Mekong Delta: The risks posed by drought/rising sea levels/saltwater intrusion/coastal erosion.\n2. Central Vietnam: The risk of more frequent/more extreme tropical storms during the wet season.\n3. Ho Chi Minh City: The increased risk of floods. \nUse [**these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Uz728ggAanI-dvkvFxO-hFsZAAjedsL7?usp=sharing) to build your detailed example of climate change risk in each of these locations. \nWhich factors that cause vulnerability seem to most significant in Vietnam's case? Explain your answer with reference to **SCALE**.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2020-11-12-at-5-09-18-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Homework investigation: Focus on climate change and inequality in the USA****Read** [**these two articles**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1X5K6wSGbAS4q_fzQQhif_V6wfW0MU_cn?usp=sharing) **to respond to these questions:** \na) With named examples, describe the different consequences of climate change in different parts of the USA. \nb) Explain why the poorest populations of the United States are more vulnerable and face a greater risk from climate change than people in wealthier regions.\nc) What does the Economist article tell you about the importance of scale? \nd) Do the wealthy have a greater responsibility to rapidly decrease consumption of fossil fuels?  Explain your opinion.\n"}},"3-global-resource":{"alternative-energy.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/alternative-energy.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Alternative energy\"\nparent: global-trends\norder: 60\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/alternative-energy.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** The Story of a Spoon.\n\n{{youtube eg-E1FtjaxY}}\n\n**Market Place:** In [**groups**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13QHYOA9EsY0mHjkjWMnjqgRmicNmCI7UaaSyywD21OY/edit?usp=sharing) research and create a poster on a particular form of alternative energy.\n\nYou will have 40 minutes to research and create your poster.  You need to use that information to produce a poster containing **no more than 10 words** (drawings, diagrams, hand drawn maps, other visuals are OK). You will use this poster to teach others.\n\n**You must include:** \nHow your alternative energy works (the production and mechanisms) - add images,\nLocational map to show where this type of energy is prevalent (clearly state whether these places are LICs, MICs or HICs), \nInformation related to how this energy is used,\nAdvantages,\nDisadvantages/ criticism of the energy type &\nEvaluation of suitability for future mass use. \nAfter which 'marketplace' will take place in 3 minute rounds where you absorb and summarize the main ideas of other groups or teach. **Important Notice:** There will be a formative quiz after this activity where your scores will be logged.\n\n**Plenary:** 3, 2, 1.\n\n**Homework**: [**Watch  - Cowspiracy**](https://ishcmc.managebac.com/classes/10750699/events/19176542)\n"},"energy-peak-oil.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/energy-peak-oil.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Energy: Peak Oil\"\nparent: global-trends\norder: 50\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/energy-peak-oil.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Watch the video below.\na) In your own words explain [**Peak Oil**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Alq3Tcq6O3E9AhJ0wlHW9EDFDDUw7QEtf6__jpqpZGg/edit?usp=sharing) ( also known as the \"Hubbert curve\")\nb) What are some of the possible criticisms of Peak Oil as a concept?\n\n{{youtube z7WYYldIRb4}}\n\n**mind mapTask 1:** In groups of three answer the following questions using these sources: [**two page document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1zauv_CLoi_2lNa-Fo5OXik9oj0wfY12SGLjsuz8LYd0/edit?usp=sharing)this  [**infographic**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1l4zx3emHjtsHSmAVYMywOWEC1hKF16nDjdCGtROETSI/edit?usp=sharing), and [**this webpage of interactive graphs**](https://ourworldindata.org/fossil-fuels) (Our World In Data). \na) Compare and contrast the countries that are the world’s major oil producers (include years where possible).\nb) What does OPEC stand for? Why was it established and why has its level of **power** changed?\nc) Suggest reasons for the top ten producers and consumers. \nd) Discuss the patterns and trends in oil consumption.\n\n**Task 2:** \na) What is energy security?\nb) Thinking [**geopolitically**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KvfVrXkLzMoq5zE1Hh17MR05_uZnrKm8/view?usp=sharing), why might energy insecurity have grown?\nc) Use [**this website**](https://helpsavenature.com/fossil-fuels-advantages-disadvantages) to create a mind-map (on paper) to illustrate the pros and cons of using fossil fuels. \nd) The situation has changed since the Iran War in 2026. Take notes on how and why energy patterns are shifting - use [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1lc8Z2AvcJmX9jwN5ptZGj-zJU_YgiJp5/view?usp=sharing).\n\n**Plenary:** \nHow is demand for energy changing?\nWhy is **place** important when discussing patterns in energy production? \nHow do development **processes** have an impact on resource consumption? \nHow does the supply and demand for oil impact **power**?\nDiscuss **possibilities** in the energy mix for the future.\n"},"few-nexus.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/few-nexus.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"F.E.W Nexus\"\nparent: impacts-of-trends\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/few-nexus.html\n---\n\n**Starters:**[**Introductory presentation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1U2jai2XENpHcvF2UXaVtKiVX0Zv0kqro/view?usp=sharing)\nWatch the video and [**use this diagram**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1AMA7xgY9PLx8qu0ItSVYg_De83hHVXN8/view?usp=sharing) to explain what you understand by \"Nexus\". We will return to this definition at the end of the lesson and see what can be added.\n\n{{youtube CKW_ux2Xo_w}}\n\n\"Trade-offs between energy and water, or between energy and food, are increasingly being debated in terms of the 'Nexus' – a buzzword which has risen in prominence over the past few years as a way of thinking about interconnections between food, water, energy and the environment. These systems are inextricably linked, and integrated approaches are required, which move beyond sectoral, policy and disciplinary silos\". James Wilsdon and Rose Cairns June 2014\n\nDownload [**this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qKf1PrzW6DIwfuAntCfa2yjQJyfZ1cFSK2F8cs4ke3Q/edit?usp=sharing) where you will record how Nexus links food, energy and water issues together. \nUse [**these two resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1bGOu_T7VJowAkVPD_ZliCcpJsSIi3RYR?usp=sharing) to populate your table with ideas.\n\n**Plenary:**[**Watch this**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MaHVJu1qKab5uOtFYichMlfdUdRHKrGW/view?usp=sharing) and then link the remaining SDGs to FEW (2, 6 & 7)\n"},"flows-of-waste.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/flows-of-waste.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Flows of Waste\"\nparent: impacts-of-trends\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/flows-of-waste.html\n---\n\n![Picture](assets/story-of-stuff_orig.jpeg)\n\n{{youtube 5Wm__WNdOPw}}\n\n1. Use [**these four maps**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1-bdzbzp1LLs73gO2G7VAhwe-WJi-4is0R-0FQAv3RA4/edit?usp=sharing) to describe the patterns of international flows of waste. \n2. [**World Economic Forum January 2019**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13uO6QSztbZtNkMRUiUp39fV_kmwWjH-p/view?usp=sharing) - read this short article to add some memorable facts and figures to your notes.\n3. Use the interactive [**e-waste Republic**](https://interactive.aljazeera.com/aje/2015/ewaste/index.html) Al Jazeera site to develop an example of the impact of e-waste in Agbogbloshie, Ghana. \n4. What was China's response 2018 to international flows of waste? Watch [**this news clip**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NK20t11He14) (HBO Sept 2018) to find out. \n5. How have other countries been affected by China's change in policy since? Read [**this short article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i08fE2bfegUr3L1yaoMPDDvS4Uqtzs0a/view?usp=sharing) [from May 2019](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i08fE2bfegUr3L1yaoMPDDvS4Uqtzs0a/view?usp=sharing).\n\nHomework extra- [**Reggie Yates - A week in a toxic dump**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_RKLqhGtHiz9Y1OPX_skSbG2mQ0hcKck/view?usp=sharing) (Agbogbloshie)\n"},"global-resource.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/global-resource.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global resource\"\norder: 0\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-resource.html\n---\n\n**This final Core unit is in three parts. Click on the links below to access each:**\n\n[Global trends and patterns](https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-trends.html) [The impacts of trends in resource consumption - NEXUS](https://geogjon.weebly.com/impacts-of-trends.html)[Resource stewardship](https://geogjon.weebly.com/resource-stewardship.html)\n"},"global-trends.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/global-trends.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global Trends\"\nparent: global-resource\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-trends.html\n---\n\nThis first part of the unit focuses on patterns and trends in resource consumption at different scales - global, national and local.\n\n**Click on the links below:**\n\n[Measuring trends](https://geogjon.weebly.com/measuring-trends.html)[Growth of the new global middle class](https://geogjon.weebly.com/growth-of-the-ldquonew-global-middle-classrdquo.html)[Patterns and trends in the availability and consumption of water](https://geogjon.weebly.com/water-consumptionsecurity.html)[Patterns and trends in land use/food production, including changing diets](https://geogjon.weebly.com/land--food.html)[The changing importance of fossil fuels, and the concept of \"peak oil\"](https://geogjon.weebly.com/energy-peak-oil.html)[The changing importance of nuclear energy and renewable energy sources](https://geogjon.weebly.com/alternative-energy.html)\n"},"growth-of-the-ldquonew-global-middle-classrdquo.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/growth-of-the-ldquonew-global-middle-classrdquo.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Growth of the “new global middle class”\"\nparent: global-trends\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/growth-of-the-ldquonew-global-middle-classrdquo.html\n---\n\n![Picture](assets/middle-classes-reuters.png)\n\n**Activities:** \na) What do you think of the quote below and the infographic opposite? Why might H.I.C.s have seen a decline in the middle classes?\n\n**\"For the first time, a truly global middle class is emerging. By 2030, it will more than double in size, from 2 billion today to 4.9 billion. It is estimated that the European and American middle classes will shrink from 50% of the total to just 22%. Rapid growth in China, India, Indonesia, Vietnam, Thailand, and Malaysia will cause Asia’s share of the new middle to more than double from its current 30%. By 2030, Asia will host 64% of the global middle class and account for over 40% of global middle-class consumption\".** **David Rohde, Reuters.**\n\nb) Have a look at [**these graphics**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1UaUhazh5J6M0dzKGbeLkSrD8Rr7bQqPc?usp=sharing) that compare different perceptions of what it means to be middle class in different places. Why might perceptions vary from place to place?\n\nc)  What is the significance of an increase in the middle classes of Middle Income countries? [**Read this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1SyunyBDAYGtHXg690k56Y88K9dKn-Bes/view?usp=sharing) to explain the impact of the global middle class on resource consumption.\n\nd) Read [**this Bloomberg article**](https://www.bloomberg.com/features/2021-emerging-markets-middle-class/) (April 2021) to add a few lines to your notes on how Covid is changing the trends in the growing Global Middle Class.\n\n**Plenary:** Watch the legendary Hans Rosling explaining [**how to end poverty in 15 years**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1kdOfwON8klmU9CdBmLZfZ7u0JaRYZFOz/view?usp=sharing). As you watch the video consider the implications of how declining levels of poverty affects the size of the middle class.\n"},"impacts-of-trends.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/impacts-of-trends.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Impacts of trends\"\nparent: global-resource\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/impacts-of-trends.html\n---\n\nThe second part of this unit focuses mostly on NEXUS, connecting issues of consumption and security in food, water and energy.\n\n**Click on the links below:**\n\n[Introducing the food-energy-water NEXUS](https://geogjon.weebly.com/few-nexus.html)[NEXUS - the implications of climate change and applying NEXUS to two countries](https://geogjon.weebly.com/nexus-20.html)[Flows of waste - disposal and recycling](https://geogjon.weebly.com/flows-of-waste.html)\n"},"land--food.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/land--food.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Land & Food\"\nparent: global-trends\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/land--food.html\n---\n\n**1. Food production**\nFood intake (measured by calorie intake) has gone up dramatically in most regions of the world. This has been due to our ability to increase crop production. Enough food is produced globally for all of us to be full and healthy. \nStarter: [**Watch this video clip**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuucXpVMlVQLXZralk/view?usp=sharing) from \"Home\". Take notes to describe and explain increases in food production, and its impacts on the environment.\n\n**2. Land Grabs**\na) Watch [**this two minute animation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuuN3M3UWtIRGhSUW8/view?usp=sharing) to define \"**land grabs**\". \nb) Read through [**this infographic**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuuWU5FREtPdU5nX0E/view?usp=sharing) to pick out some memorable facts and figures. \nc) Read [**this short article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuuS1EwaVRYMU5fYnc/view?usp=sharing) to explain the impacts of land grabs on the food security of small-scale farmers.\n\n{{youtube 3cKUDIte2sk}}\n\n**3. Changing diets and lifestyles**\nObesity rates in the United States, the South Pacific and the Persian Gulf are among the highest in the world — more than one in four Americans is obese. But over the last 35 years, obesity, defined as having a body mass index over 30, has grown the fastest in countries throughout Latin America, Africa and Asia.\n\nUse the video above and [**this interactive article**](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2017/09/16/health/brazil-obesity-nestle.html) from the New York Times (Sept 2017). Make notes (including a handful of facts and figures) on the changing diets of M.I.Cs and Brazil in particular. Be sure to comment on:\n\nThe role of big business/TNCs (name and shame the brands too)\nThe impact on health of changing diets, particularly the problem of obesity. \nMention any \"underhand\" tactics employed by big business ('agribusinesses') to reach new emerging markets.\n\n**Plenary: 'Food for thought\"** (ha!) ... discuss the contents of this video.\n\n{{youtube nUnJQWO4YJY}}\n"},"measuring-trends.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/measuring-trends.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Measuring trends\"\nparent: global-trends\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/measuring-trends.html\n---\n\n[**Introductory presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1YW1FOYZmNEnt84wRPhMzxm20GQKqxaIOlHzRpA8anvE/edit?usp=sharing) \nRead [**this article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bwSRCcuLeKe4_tuYHgZCccowxjigLYbhWxmGJaJW6bc/edit?usp=sharing) (WWF 2021) to answer the following:\nWhat is meant by “overshoot day”? \nWhy does overshoot day vary on a national scale? Think of several reasons, quoting examples of countries.\nExplain the trend in the dates for overshoot day, including anomalies.\nExplain what is causing the world to overshoot, and what the consequences are.\n\nWatch this YouTube video and explain the concept of the \"ecological footprint\".\n\n{{youtube nP1nzCqoT9g}}\n\n**Activities:** \n1. \na) Describe the changes to the world's ecological footprint [**using this**](https://econation.co.nz/ecological-footprint/) (reference the graph's key & quantify your answers). \nb) Suggest how and why ecological footprints might vary according to levels of development. Comment on LICs, MICs and HICs.\nc) Suggest reasons for the differences you identify (use **SEEP** in your explanations). \nd) [**Comment on your home country**](assets/EcologicalFootprintMap.jpg). Are there any surprises?\n\n2. Use this [**website**](https://www.footprintcalculator.org/) to work out your own ecological footprint using your school email. \na) If everyone lived like you how many earths would we need?\nb) What date is your individual overshoot day? \nc) Re take the \"test\" and make changes to your lifestyle during the pandemic. Explain the three things you changed about your lifestyle during the pandemic and why you have become more sustainable.\n\n**Homework:** Make a copy of [**this glossary**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1XKgSEzAYZCcpeqIka6pXCm2RpC_Siyfif_LROPyh2Pk/edit?usp=sharing) for the unit. Start it by adding definitions for the key terms already in there.\n"},"nexus-20.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/nexus-20.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Nexus 2.0\"\nparent: impacts-of-trends\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/nexus-20.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Focus on the importance of climate in this video. Explain the implications of global climate change for the water–food–energy Nexus.\n\n{{youtube 83x8wvfqboo}}\n\nUsing [**these two textbook extracts**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1wEmbw9ZT9f6s0GUgv5ZSLTswXfh_8Eo2?usp=sharing)and[**this article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PY2P-xBw5rmZLHMxsA5OX_TibjBw9mevanajTxCa9q8/edit?usp=sharing)**:**\nc) Create a detailed mind map to explain the implications of global climate change and its impact on i) food ii) water iii) energy. Be sure to identify actual problems which exist in the world and include places as examples. \nd) How can the Nexus approach enable places to ADAPT to climate change? Include at least two examples for i) food production ii) water use and iii) energy production.\n\nIn pairs, you will explore India and Kenya as two countries with contrasting levels of resource security.\na) Use[**this guidance**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/17PPJixwPcaD20ZFIknQ4pwCaBBPK5F1EDXdUKSZgDMM/edit?usp=sharing)and [**this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1LJRrS-J-lKC0q3uAwxj4F73rPpM86M-p?usp=sharing) for the Indian example.\nb) Complete a detailed example of Kenya using the same template. This [**folder contains all the resources needed.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1uFMZ6mO32o_HDyHmpikX54wM278d2hxB?usp=sharing)\n"},"population-theories.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/population-theories.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Population theories\"\nparent: resource-stewardship\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/population-theories.html\n---\n\n**Starters:** To gain an overview of the two main perspectives, take notes on the ideas of [**Thomas Malthus and Ester Boserup**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1dsU78_u_vDseE797lsyWbwGj1oYJDjOA?usp=sharing). \nWatch this Crash Course video to get an understanding of the theory of Thomas Malthus and its historical context.\n\n{{youtube QAkW_i0bDpQ}}\n\n**Party people:** \nYou have been asked to host a party for the greatest population thinkers of our time (past and present). The problem is they are a boring bunch! You will need to be careful where they are seated so the party-poopers don't spoil the fun.\n\n**Task:**[**In groups**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1u33ju2BlY8OqNxtVQ3Yh3fj-PMlPDMFc2_fF5tEwfzI/edit?usp=sharing)\nFind out about who you are as a guest at the party to discuss issues regarding population growth versus resource consumption. \nCreate a slide to represent your views as a guest and [**add it here**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1WDuiH_QXIIlgBP724X55mJn373ST_Y8tIGMEtw09AVs/edit?usp=sharing) (max. of one slide per group) using [**these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B1D3UOqNjNuubFI1VHd3cmRvOXc?resourcekey=0-kPqgRcDDEk3_pDJrABLkxw&usp=sharing)**.**\nDiscuss/argue with the other guests!\n\n**Debrief:** \nWhat are the \"divergent ways of thinking\"? \nCompare and contrast Neo-Malthusian and Anti-Malthusian views on population and resources.\n\n**Plenary:** \nWhich of the views do you personally agree with? \nAre you an optimist or a pessimist? \nCan you use an example to support your views?\n\nGo to this next page, the final part of this Core unit, [**a review of how the UN SDGs link with all these resource consumption issues**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/sdgs.html).\n"},"resource-stewardship-circular-economy.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/resource-stewardship-circular-economy.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Resource stewardship: circular economy\"\nparent: resource-stewardship\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/resource-stewardship-circular-economy.html\n---\n\n**Watch the video below:** How are the indigenous and western systems different in the way that they manage resources?\n\n{{youtube RxPVrr44KHI}}\n\n1. The predominant **linear** systems approach of ‘take-make-use-waste’ also leads to the “tragedy of the commons”. [**Watch this video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1tllkpeh4rxmtsrg7hy9E-ArCytXcTbHS/view?usp=sharing) to be able to explain this theory. Think about how it could be fixed.\n\nThere is now divergent thinking that focuses on more sustainable approaches to resource management - to promote **resource stewardship**, where humans act as ‘caretakers’ of natural resources.\n\n2. The **circular economy** is a systems approach to **resource stewardship**. While watching [**these two videos**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1QtCxARu5aQzX7rKCB9m0vXLtOMNnceGj?usp=sharing):\na) Define the **circular economy**.\nb) In what ways is it different from the **linear economy**?\n\n**3. Applying the circular economy approach in practice**\nThere are many examples already evident. Watch a sample of [**these videos**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1MXyoDLCovAKfySC6NdqUb8_yMVCmsHbM?usp=sharing) at different scales and a couple of examples from [**this website**](https://www.ellenmacarthurfoundation.org/case-studies) to record some specific examples. \nWhich one(s) impressed you most? What challenges do these projects face?\n\n**4. Is the circular economy \"a magical fix\" to the way we manage resources?** \nRead [this](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1iKXNwxN5mIT-UPYvG3JrulqYCtAqlmjZ7TGd3qWqKLY/edit?usp=sharing) [**article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1iKXNwxN5mIT-UPYvG3JrulqYCtAqlmjZ7TGd3qWqKLY/edit?usp=sharing) to highlight some shortfalls of the **circular economy** approach.\n\n**Follow-up HW**\n1. Take more detailed notes on the **circular economy** from [**this Geography Review article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1VtDpOXK5NA7TttzD8L_w9n_KD7SM4vVR/view?usp=sharing) written by the Chief Examiner, Simon Oakes, especially the examples of the **circular economy** in action at different scales to add to yours.  \n2. Watch [**the TED**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZwikPFs_7eOWe1Gvlfo1_EDnS-Crt5wt/view?usp=sharing)[, \"Doughnut Economics\"](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZwikPFs_7eOWe1Gvlfo1_EDnS-Crt5wt/view?usp=sharing) by Kate Raworth. Summarise this alternative approach to economics that focuses on **resource stewardship**.\n\n{{youtube Ir-XjQhOyNQ}}\n\n**Further watching/ reading:**\n\n[**Ellen MacArthur foundation**](https://www.ellenmacarthurfoundation.org/)[**More from the MacArthur foundation.**](https://youtu.be/zCRKvDyyHmI) [**What I learnt sailing around the world solo (Ted).**](https://youtu.be/ooIxHVXgLbc)\n\nGo to the next part of the unit, [**a debate over population-resources theories**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/population-theories.html).\n"},"resource-stewardship.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/resource-stewardship.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Resource stewardship\"\nparent: global-resource\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/resource-stewardship.html\n---\n\nThe final part of this unit focuses on the POSSIBILITIES for the future with regards to global resource consumption and security.\n\n**Click on the links below:**\n\n[Different perspectives on resource consumption versus population theories](https://geogjon.weebly.com/population-theories.html)[The 'circular economy' as a systems approach](https://geogjon.weebly.com/resource-stewardship-circular-economy.html)[The role of the UN's Sustainable Development Goals](https://geogjon.weebly.com/sdgs.html)\n"},"sdgs.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/sdgs.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"SDGs\"\nparent: resource-stewardship\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/sdgs.html\n---\n\n{{youtube q5nFFTnUM4w}}\n\n**The Sustainable Development Goals are the blueprint to achieve a better and more sustainable future for all. They address the global challenges we face, including those related to poverty, inequality, climate, environmental degradation, prosperity, and peace and justice. The Goals interconnect and in order to leave no one behind, it is important that we achieve each Goal and target by 2030.**\n\n**Progress in the SDGs since 2015: You be the judge!**\n\nIn a group you will focus on one of these four SDGs that underpin this Core unit:\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-11-01-at-6-14-39-pm_orig.png)\n\nUse [**these extracts of the SDG report 20**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1snktFjZQSndcQHZHX7uM-8q0sF3cZbFY/view?usp=sharing)**21.****For your group's assigned goal:** \n1. Record 1-2 facts/figures that illustrate progress (or lack of it) since 2015. \n2. Comment on the regions where the most/least progress is being made, and give some explanation why. \n3. Using an IB score of 1-7, where do you judge progress in your goal so far? Be prepared to justify. \n**Also, in your group:** \nUse the last few pages of the handout to find out which stakeholders seem to be making the most difference - governments, private businesses, social entrepreneurs and NGOs, and which goals seem to be a priority today in different regions.\n\n**Plenary:** Watch the video below (Ola Rosling, son of Hans - October 2020) to consolidate your understanding.\n\n{{youtube v7WUpgPZzpI}}\n"},"water-consumptionsecurity.md":{"path":"content/3-global-resource/water-consumptionsecurity.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Water consumption/security\"\nparent: global-trends\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/water-consumptionsecurity.html\n---\n\n**Starter -** Watch the YouTube clip below and make notes using the [**Cornell method**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1qvaiC7uhqKIOHCdGPKQWHOD4gGZcybsI/view?usp=sharing). Along the left hand side you must include the following terminology:\nWater scarcity, demand, exponential increase, growing middle class, 1 kg of beef, 2030, innovated, agriculture, urbanization, industrialization, energy, desalinization & infrastructure.\n\n{{youtube VIaw5mCjHPI}}\n\n**Use these resources:** [**Water consumption graphs**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/11_DeRf4rU0Ex758r3aaWeS5VSLbAHlvJXfZ0gY11trs/edit?usp=sharing) **and** [**\"Global water use trends ever increasing\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oKMxPrI469cOQAF7rzdUxMAT6kg5qERt/view?usp=sharing) **(July 2019):**\nDescribe the differences in water use between HICs and LICs/MICs. Use the article to explain these differences.\n\n**\"Embedded\" (or virtual) water.** Use [**this web page**](https://waterfootprint.org/en/water-footprint/national-water-footprint/virtual-water-trade/) to find out: \na) What does \"embedded water\" mean? Give examples. \nb) Make notes on the sections \"Why is it important?\" & \"water saving by trade\". \nc) Figure B: - Compare and contrast the imports and exports of virtual water into Europe using the map.\n\n**Water security/stress**\nUse these resources to describe the places most at risk from water security, and give reasons why:\n[**Infographic on water security and SDG 6**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1SS5tCZDnikSRjSvQ8sIFJ87tZQIRAUxd/view?usp=sharing)- for the global overview\n[**Extreme water stress affects a quarter of the world's population**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eSOhA7-BvSTLZNBEqNAqczcw4ikLeH8v/view?usp=sharing) (The Guardian August 2019)\n[**Are we running out of water?**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1o-L8umeZwJOkMYH-_398PQVAddJpLPRG/view?usp=sharing) (The Guardian June 2018)\n[**Water scarcity**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuuNWVnWUNkRnNIems/view?usp=sharing) - video clip from \"Home\" (8 mins)\n[**Water stress and climate change**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1D3UOqNjNuuc0x1cWhBWmJDRVE/view?usp=sharing) (Infographic 2015)\n\n**Plenary:** If you haven't come across these terms, find out - \nWhat is the difference between **economic water scarcity** & **physical water scarcity**? Explain each term using place names as examples. \nWhat is meant by Nexus with (reference to water)? Find out!\n"}},"4-geographical-themes":{"building-sustainable-urban-systems.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/building-sustainable-urban-systems.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Building sustainable urban systems\"\nparent: urban-environments\norder: 50\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/building-sustainable-urban-systems.html\n---\n\n"},"case-study---infrastructure-growth--deindustrialization.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/case-study---infrastructure-growth--deindustrialization.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Case study - infrastructure growth & deindustrialization\"\nparent: changing-urban-systems\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/case-study---infrastructure-growth--deindustrialization.html\n---\n\n**Keywords:** Complete [**this activity**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1dT122p_3qzrft5kqEdT9i0aUHfG1r8Zw4KQGo66r7TE/edit?usp=sharing) as quickly as possible.\n\n**Urban systems growth** - is the increased need to provide access to clean water, sanitation and waste disposal as urban areas grow. If urban areas are to be successful they also need to expand the transport and telecommunications networks (think HCMC!!). Without these features it is difficult to attract new economic activities. Traffic jams, air pollution, unclean water and inadequate telecommunications reflect a failure by those in charge to match   population growth with infrastructure growth.\n\n**A changing urban environment - Case Study Shanghai**[**Andrew Marr - cities and transport**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LaLS1wP-aMdWINl0eQY-WyZinuCcM_88/view?usp=sharing)- extract all things \"Shanghai\". \nRead and make notes on these exerts [**from the textbook.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1jz_spiODKmZQa9s_JNXP4vWLuePkQ8Pk?usp=sharing) [Complete activities 6, 7 & 8.](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1jz_spiODKmZQa9s_JNXP4vWLuePkQ8Pk?usp=sharing)\n\n**Causes and consequences of deindustrialization in \"Motor City\" (Detroit, USA)** \nDeindustrialization is the long-term, absolute decline in employment in the manufacturing sectors of the economy. It refers to a lose of jobs rather than a decline in productivity. In many MICs and LICs the economies are rapidly growing as a result of outsourcing and & FDI.  In many traditional industrial towns and cities in HICs, urban deindustrialization has occurred. Sheffield in the UK and Detroit in the US are prime examples.\n\n{{youtube 7QNT01AadYg}}\n\n**The causes of urban deindustrialization (SEEP)**[**NY Times article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/14YBqjBUtRn0XJSGpVszoTzpKmbA0ugyo/view?usp=sharing)—Anatomy of Detroit's Decline \n[**Video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1YWvT92hXrE5BUXyQhQukvFNtN-kbzfBN/view?usp=sharing)**—The** Abandoned Skyscrapers of Detroit.\n\n**The consequences of deindustrialization (SEEP)**\n\n![Picture](assets/cycle-of-urban-decline_orig.png)\n\n**Plenary:** [**Video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Ei6kZ9sI9nE9CUrgp9JF9n8yFXsbSqCB/view?usp=sharing)  - How might these cities be renewed? Give reasons for growth.\n\n**Go to the next page:**[**Environmental stresses—microclimates, congestion, and air pollution**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/micro-climates-traffic--pollution.html)\n"},"case-study-2012-london-olympics.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/case-study-2012-london-olympics.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Case study: 2012 London Olympics\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-international-scale\norder: 50\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/case-study-2012-london-olympics.html\n---\n\n**Task 1 - Factors that influence hosting international sporting events**\nComplete[**this activity**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1GLQDJ0HauvmJxCLccYOmSeOgjzrd7peAiVibOfOXmEU/edit?usp=sharing)to consider what geographic factors (political, economic and cultural) influence the choice of hosts of major international sporting events like The Olympics. Be ready to justify your ideas! \n**Extra** - visit [**this web page**](http://geographical.co.uk/places/mapping/item/1845-cartogram-special-olympics) that displays world maps that indicate participation and success in The Olympics. How does POWER influences the patterns?\n\n**Task 2 - The legacy of London Olympics 2012**\nIntroducing the London Olympics - [**presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1c5tmGs_baY73VT1MzgBA0nT_FL1O_Bhu3lcJrUPoHm4/edit?usp=sharing)\nIn a small group, consider the costs and benefits for London/UK for hosting the Olympics in 2012, both in the **short-term and long-term**. [Sort these](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1_jYALha2eZht2if-ZE2nXLc19_ue8Ic_V_6E6gZuuzo/edit?usp=sharing) [**cards**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1_jYALha2eZht2if-ZE2nXLc19_ue8Ic_V_6E6gZuuzo/edit?usp=sharing) and think about the different impacts. \nAfterwards, watch [**this short video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13eLypd4vlrD5iE3FqR-zVi3YwHNUOJv7/view?usp=sharing), \"the legacy five years on\" to add to your notes.\n**In conclusion, was it worth it?**\n\n**Task 3: Paralympics - Inclusion** \na) Watch the video below and read one of the articles [**found here.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1yXvkfOaqn0wvFiJlg2bhZ32V_VYWPFuj?usp=sharing) \nb) Share with a partner to explain the main messages of these resources. \nc) Write a paragraph explaining the growing importance of the Paralympics, particularly since Olympics 2012.\n\n{{youtube bUs_mNTNwvE}}\n\n**Plenary:** \nWhat has **POWER** got to do with it when considering countries that host major international sports events like the Olympics?\nDo host countries ever win?  \nWould you want your home country to host an international sporting event? Why?\n"},"changing-leisure-patterns.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/changing-leisure-patterns.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Changing Leisure Patterns\"\nparent: leisure-sport-and-tourism\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/changing-leisure-patterns.html\n---\n\n**Categorizing leisure, sport and tourism:** \nWatch both videos below. Each one contains elements of **leisure, sport** and **tourism**. How should we define each?\n\n{{youtube KcjPHJ7EhBM}}\n\n{{youtube SwbP9WLX3fY}}\n\n**Sport:** Describe two types of sporting activities which vary greatly in terms of their cost, popularity, site. For example, football versus horse riding.\n\n**Leisure time ... who has the most?** \nTake a look at [**the top 30 countries with the most leisure time (and \"personal care\")**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1gv6NFU_hGfbaGw7GU2O_ry2WrUhr6QQgzMJxvCquFTw/edit?usp=sharing) (OECD 2017) - can you explain the pattern?\n\n**What causes changes in patterns of leisure, sport and tourism?**\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-01-24-at-5-53-49-pm_orig.png)\n\nIn a group, skim-read the resources below - **two minutes max on each** - in order to write a tweet/very short summary of each. \nCategorise them according to:\na) Leisure\nb) Sport\nc) Tourism\nand according to the underlying cause in each case: accessibility, changes in technology or affluence.\n\n[**Resource 1**](http://www.washingtonpost.com/news/wonkblog/wp/2015/07/29/rich-american-tourists-kill-hundreds-of-lions-each-year-and-its-all-legal/) [**Resource 2**](https://vietnamtourism.gov.vn/english/index.php/items/13782)[**Resource 3**](https://www.statista.com/chart/18983/time-spent-on-social-media/)[**Resource 4**](https://www.goal.com/en/news/who-are-the-richest-football-club-owners-in-the-world/15g2j9z9l37m91y0i5c8os7xgn) [**Resource 5**](https://www.pledgesports.org/2018/05/the-massive-growth-of-esports/)[**Resource 6**](https://phuketcondo.net/russian-tourism-phuket-thailand/)[**Resource 7**](https://wersm.com/youtube-vloggers-changing-travel-industry/)[**Resource 8**](http://www.theguardian.com/travel/2015/sep/17/first-plane-lands-st-helena-airport-atlantic-ocean-south-africa)[**Resource 9**](https://manictube.com/video/the-rise-of-airbnb-from-airbeds-to-billions/)[**Resource 10**](https://www.nytimes.com/2020/02/26/travel/antarctica-tourism-environment-safety.html)[**Resource 11**](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/jan/17/wheelchair-climber-hauls-himself-250-metres-up-hong-kong-skyscraper-for-charity)[**Resource 12**](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/09/26/space-tourism-how-spacex-virgin-galactic-blue-origin-axiom-compete.html)\n\n**Read this remixed textbook extract and take lots of notes!**[**Factors affecting participation in leisure and sports**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oLjk-FeRmPORpTmfR0VkGl9JHXkpNcq4/view?usp=sharing).\n\n**Plenary****- Exam-style question** \nDiscuss the difficulties in attempting to define leisure, tourism and sport.\n\n[**Go to the next page**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/china-economic-development--leisure-activities.html) to explore the recent changes in leisure patterns/trends in China\n"},"changing-urban-systems.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/changing-urban-systems.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Changing Urban Systems\"\nparent: urban-environments\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/changing-urban-systems.html\n---\n\n"},"characteristics-and-patterns.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/characteristics-and-patterns.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Characteristics and patterns\"\nparent: urban-environments\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/characteristics-and-patterns.html\n---\n\nStarter: [**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1ce14m8Z3X_Z0-px6_G17-Fnf06TEfaIxXqDzkymhMdo/edit?usp=sharing)\nUsing the maps on the presentation and the [**following**](https://www.economist.com/node/21642053), explain the pattern and trends in urbanization. \nWhy do you think so many settlements are located in some areas but not in others? \nIs there a pattern in terms of size of settlement?\nIs there a pattern in terms of spatial distribution?\n**Settlement site, situation and hierarchy**\nThe **site** of a settlement refers to the physical characteristics of its location. It is these factors that historically explain the location of a settlement. Today however, site characteristics include the social, economic and political characteristics of a settlement.\n\n![Picture](assets/siteandsituation.png)\n\nThe situation of a settlement explains the location of a settlement in relation to important physical features, communication routes as well as other settlements and so locates it within a regional context. \nTo understand the concept of a **settlement hierarchy** complete [**this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VNtoBHyeINW8jJm39Vw1tNDGaCPFjWma/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true)**.**\n\n**Models/Theories on how cities are organised (= urban \"morphology\")**\ni) Use [**this presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1G7IJ1ZCvlC3KG5sOnyVeK80p725nYPJNnL9otfi2N6w/edit?usp=sharing) to gain an understanding of models that represent the theoretical explanation of how the land use of cities is organised. \nii) Compare and contrast the different \"growth factors\" of [**each of the models.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1BiGsmY_QsvnLbwqPwOI3F4blzhR6AT9z?usp=sharing) \niii) Include the most significant limitation of each land use model.\n\nWhat is the relationship between the location of the CBD and the price of land? The theory of Bid Rent\nWhat does the [**Bid Rent video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1k-mdvygd4WCKTWYaM1vm-c_OpIkzAN43/view?usp=sharing)say about the following sections - \ncommerce (retail) rent, \nindustrial rent & \nresidential rent.  Peak Land Value Intersection (PLVI) and the declining CBD in High Income Cities - [**Read**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1wueIn6pb391LyVYaWpqgefpRJd3he-TSq4wNP7ZI6yc/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**The planned and spontaneous growth of urban areas: Megacities**\nYou've watched the video below. Re-watch it while making notes about the reasons for megacity growth and the problems which these settlements will face.\n\n{{youtube _jnMivEZ8gc}}\n\n[Website](http://www.visualcapitalist.com/animated-map-worlds-populous-cities-2100/) - an animated map of the world's 20 most populated megacities, 2010-2100.\n\n**What challenges do societies face with the rapid growth of megacities?** [Use this website](https://en.reset.org/knowledge/megacities-07152014) for an overview of the challenges of megacity growth. \nRead/take notes on [this textbook extract](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Vn59swir4-o_o_2lVEJHSuwbZxGiQIGg/view?usp=sharing) that focuses on the challenges faced by Shanghai. \n[**Check out these before/after images**](https://www.theatlantic.com/photo/2013/08/26-years-of-growth-shanghai-then-and-now/100569/)of Shanghai, 26 years apart.\n\n**The impact of megacities on individuals:** Download [this activity sheet](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13xQW8dP0bomR3Srg5YZuZUOf85RHmztCVIGeohVDHJ8/edit?usp=sharing) \nWhat is it like living in a megacity like Shanghai? \"Living in the shadows\" investigates the lives of three migrant worker families living there. To listen to the vodcast and evaluate the impacts on these individuals.\n\n**Exam-style questions (optional):** \nA megacity has a minimum of 10 million people. What's a millionaire city?\nComment on the location of megacities.\nSuggest reasons why people migrate to megacities? \nBriefly explain why high-value land can be found in a) central areas of cities and b) on the edges of cities. \nState four characteristics used to classify settlements. \nDefine and describe named and located examples of a settlement hierarchy. \nExplain how spheres of influence may vary in space and time. \nIdentify and locate the industrial functions of a city.\nBriefly explain the meaning of the term \"bid-rent.\" \nExplain the factors determining the location of the commercial zone in the city.\n\n**Extension:** Update your glossary with terminology from the Urban Environment unit so far.\n"},"china-economic-development--leisure-activities.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/china-economic-development--leisure-activities.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"China: Economic development & leisure activities.\"\nparent: changing-leisure-patterns\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/china-economic-development--leisure-activities.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Watch the video below:\nWhat is meant by \"the grey economy\"?\nExplain what types of leisure products and services are becoming more popular in China as a result.\n\n{{youtube 8Mozu3lX3Sg}}\n\n**To create a detailed example of recent changes in participation in leisure, sport and tourism in China:**\n\nMake a copy of [**this table**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1b7eMRl2nB7VWPhxdsPu6OkjO2-l5GM4AyKMri9X_3z4/edit?usp=sharing) to structure and record your findings on the changes, and their causes. \nUse [**these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Jk8mxxNSj2CHqLHNayvUM7JMG_iYWZaY?usp=sharing) (and any of your own!) to build a \"detailed example\" on China.\n\n[**Go to the next page**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/uk-economic-development--leisure-activities.html)to explore the changing patterns in leisure participation in the UK as a contrasting example.\n"},"contested-land-use--managing-urban-deprivation.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/contested-land-use--managing-urban-deprivation.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Contested land use & managing urban deprivation\"\nparent: urban-environment-and-social-stresses\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/contested-land-use--managing-urban-deprivation.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** What's it like in slum? Enjoy the video and then right your own definition. Where are these settlements like?\n\n{{youtube J9-Lwpgfd1E}}\n\n[**Slum Clearance in Rio/Contested Land**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/17_aq9iDOV107pSHEQDm0uPLr9hOiQ3X7SgzIEDw_gqs/edit?usp=sharing)[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/17_aq9iDOV107pSHEQDm0uPLr9hOiQ3X7SgzIEDw_gqs/edit?usp=sharing)\nFirst the World Cup and then the Olympics—watch, [**read, and make notes.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/14XKb_IGKVavvy7rHwHmAK-SeefNHpm52?usp=sharing) \nDiscuss how the space is contested. Who are the competing interest groups? Which groups have power?\nWhat interest groups have power and influence over space? Why\n\n[**Now study the images and watch the video made before the Olympics.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1AFOoR149eGMqPzv0TIwTI2UZDg0qn_Bp?usp=sharing) \nDescribe the location of Vila-Autodromo. What impact did this have on the neighbourhood of Vila-Autodromo?\nWhat impact did it have on the green space within Vila-Autodromo?\n\nThe problem or the solution? [**What do you think?**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Fizjv2ORrKHmZEaNfy8cOKZuXKKftqxX/view?usp=sharing)**Extra:**[**Article**](https://e.vnexpress.net/news/news/environment/ho-chi-minh-city-to-clear-slums-along-canals-in-district-8-relocate-15-000-families-4908031.html?fbclid=IwY2xjawLRPylleHRuA2FlbQIxMQBicmlkETFROVJlQ3dRb1YyZkNZb24yAR4momPTp7xGekeGvhyuegFVhNJzdOhqvbDS7wa0TLX1iIcUzXo4SbLJqMMi8A_aem_B43ttt3S456U3DTduoW_DA) on slum clearance programmes in Ho Chi Minh City, June 2025\n\n**Managing the impacts of urban social deprivation, including the cycle of deprivation and geographic patterns of crime.**\nStudy the [**diagrams**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1rWKuENpjRBxSc6l1w1BBuK0FNzjsSi_c?usp=sharing) and discuss what they show about the causes of social deprivation.\nConsider your list of social deprivation characteristics and identify the key areas for policy and management strategies to tackle  urban social deprivation.\n\n{{youtube VX45Oz7pzEw}}\n\n[**London's Burning.**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1omUqKJL12ZgCcw-XPUX-XCJzjiDaXWKlmA3v3lQgrnA/edit?usp=sharing) [**Strategies to manage social deprivation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Z9u6_QABZSKrhbjqrsUGlgvYvTKSgxsV/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary:**[**The future of policing?**](https://youtu.be/wLxZ36oOvOQ) [**Superb extra**](https://www.thinglink.com/scene/820543245133021186?buttonSource=viewLimits)\n\n**Go to the next page:** [**Urban growth and resilient city design**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/urban-growth--resilient-city-design.html)\n"},"eco--smart-city-design.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/eco--smart-city-design.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Eco & smart city design\"\nparent: building-sustainable-urban-systems\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/eco--smart-city-design.html\n---\n\n**What is an eco city?** Make connection with sustainability and the work completed on circular economies from the resource consumption unit.\n\n{{youtube IeTHVLm57aw}}\n\nWith a partner, complete two detailed examples ([**London**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1oUVDZ0YLrsTLR3D4XGLLedY-pBKyXCjN?usp=sharing)[, England](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1oUVDZ0YLrsTLR3D4XGLLedY-pBKyXCjN?usp=sharing) & [**Songdo**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1c_UmrW49dvL5xnBV_k-qD_nfbiv9lxm5?usp=sharing)[, Korea](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1c_UmrW49dvL5xnBV_k-qD_nfbiv9lxm5?usp=sharing)) to illustrate possible strategies to manage urban ecological footprints.\nInclude:\nLocation including a map, \nThe role of technology,\nWaste management, \nThe sustainable use of resources &\nCriticism of the schemes. \n**Plenary**: [**Listen carefully**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1PLIemgoGEfjj8SRqBictXYmmSvVnx_7V/view?usp=sharing).\n"},"factors-affecting-poverty.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/factors-affecting-poverty.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Factors affecting poverty\"\nparent: urban-environments\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/factors-affecting-poverty.html\n---\n\n[**Starter:**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1Stx3A_dqU0xW6u2E3ukbgJZESFKWzpW19IXPv-AaUI0/edit?usp=sharing) What is the message from Goldie?\n\n{{youtube i-P98B2skts}}\n\n**London - Urban poverty and deprivation** \nUse the slide from the intro from [**these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1r3zqs7QdA91WPwL9bB0C7IEBpAn1bgUC?usp=sharing)[.](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1r3zqs7QdA91WPwL9bB0C7IEBpAn1bgUC?usp=sharing) [We are going to](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Hx4fHcMHNeYjsX4WLR1RQuLQkQWNRfDa/view?usp=sharing) examine the pattern of deprivation in London. \nWe will use maps of different indicators (and other information) to create a detailed annotated map. Ask yourself:\nIs there a clear pattern? \nAre there any anomalies?\nWhich deprivation indicators are the most significant?  In your own words, explain what is meant by the \"cycle of deprivation.\" \nWith reference to London, what is the difference between ‘culture of poverty’ and the \"cycle of deprivation\"?\n\n**Informal activity in urban areas**\nInformal housing is another term for slum settlements. [**Organize the pros and cons of informal housing.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1v9pgcRRp_R79ufN1jPjqyiqNm554Ya8jY3SNpqlsuQ0/edit?usp=sharing) \nThe dual economy of the developing world city consists of the formal economy and informal economy. Give examples of each with a realistic location for each.\nThe informal sector can be divided into two - a traditional bazaar economy and a street economy. Give examples of each with a realistic location for each.\n[**Organize the characteristics of the formal and informal economic sectors.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1evCO1DCT_mOIAxso-AVg5MrJf4chNOIXV7L6yA5-bm8/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Exam-style questions**\nOutline the main characteristics of the \"cycle of deprivation.\" \nComment on the nature and characteristics of the informal sector. \nOutline the main characteristics of slum housing. Give a named example of a slum you have studied. \nBriefly explain why some modern retailing (in HICs) is attracted to out-of-town locations. \nExplain why informal activities are important in the economy of the developing world. \nDescribe the pattern of deprivation in London. \nWhat are two main reasons for clusters of high socio-economic status in the inner city (London)?\n\n**Extension:** Update your glossary with terminology from the Urban Environment unit so far.\n\n**Go to the next page:** [**Urbanization and urban systems—centripetal and centrifugal movements**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/urbanization--urban-systems.html)\n"},"famine-stricken-area---a-case-study.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/famine-stricken-area---a-case-study.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Famine-stricken area - a case study\"\nparent: food-and-health\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/famine-stricken-area---a-case-study.html\n---\n\n[**Land of Africa**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Xvp-LbUBy1dR4pwtjkU0wxGPSGurCBDY/view?usp=sharing) (a tune to tune in ...)\n\n{{youtube aNbGtWSAV_c}}\n\n**The Horn of Africa - a case study**\na) Make a copy of [**this document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1JLy9c2gGD2lXSTOz_GTj4bEqcfkHDdF3hN1a0gf_FN8/edit?usp=sharing) using the 4Ps to organise your notes. \nb) Read [**the following articles**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/16z9S92zZj6Kpo1WDezGjjjBvPBRPna2j?usp=sharing) to understand the many causes of this famine situation. Record according to the 4Ps. \nc) [**Infographic**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1emrA9xWYl1gNFlQBv3I7_AAvH4AU6-R4/view?usp=sharing)**:** Use it to suggest ways women have been impacted by the crisis in the Horn of Africa. \nd) Extra - [**\"Faultlines\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xCLACWTfzDNHo-A_RE25GR86wIf8U8tJ/view?usp=sharing), an edited (22 minutes) documentary made by Al Jazeera based on the previous famine in 2011.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2019-11-18-at-2-57-50-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Plenary:** Power, possibilities ... and a conclusion: should famine **ever** happen today?\n"},"food-and-health.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/food-and-health.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Food and health\"\nparent: geographical-themes\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/food-and-health.html\n---\n\n**This option unit divides up as follows:**\n\n1. [Measuring food](https://geogjon.weebly.com/measuring-food.html)\n2. [Food systems, processes, and the diffusion of agricultural innovations](https://geogjon.weebly.com/food-systems-processes-innovations.html)\n3. [Measuring health](https://geogjon.weebly.com/measuring-health.html)\n4. [The incidence and spread (diffusion) of diseases](https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-incidencespread-of-diseases.html)\n5. [Stakeholders in food and health](https://geogjon.weebly.com/stakeholders-in-food-and-health.html)\n6. [A case study of a famine-stricken area: The Horn of Africa](https://geogjon.weebly.com/famine-stricken-area---a-case-study.html)\n7. [Future security and sustainability in food & health](https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-food--health-security-and-sustainability.html)\n"},"food-systems-processes-innovations.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/food-systems-processes-innovations.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Food systems, processes, innovations\"\nparent: food-and-health\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/food-systems-processes-innovations.html\n---\n\n**1. Farms as systems**\nApplying the systems approach to subsistence rice farming in the northern Philippines - use [**this magazine double spread**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1gMooBo-VRuqBO47NsAXyRkkn_fDPy_vi/view?usp=sharing) for the information, and [**download this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1JtTIHBMO4optC7IanaQDqb5DQlvn-lCUbK8Or-FPRT4/edit?usp=sharing) for you to annotate.\n\n**How sustainable are modern farm systems?**\nWhen you read/watch these resources, think about **the energy efficiency ratio**, the added inputs of water, agrochemicals, fuel, land and the added outputs/consequences of pollution. For HL students, this is a revisit. \nWatch [**this five-minute clip of \"HOME\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1nYpq2GkE3DnTyLCGvFElKqlaSEEIXg4T/view?usp=sharing) about the impact of modern farm systems. \nRead [**this article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1P3n195JOUuQ1l5peBg0iv4mLt7UZR7Xwzd85H0Sl1m4/edit?usp=sharing) about agrochemicals and intensive farming.\nWatch [**this clip**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ff1_sWd8uLUD4nonXKmZP6pFHUmQ5YiV/view?usp=sharing)of Sir David Attenborough (\"Extinction\" 2020) on the impact of farming on **biodiversity**. \nIf you haven't seen it before, watch [**the hidden cost of hamburgers**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jconD5sb_4_NtejgMkK2SzewDwmyE20-/view?usp=sharing) - its water footprint and the inefficient/unsustainable production of beef.\n\n![Picture](assets/shutterstock-634025597.jpg)\n\n**2. Diffusion of innovations in agriculture** [**Presentation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1e94_pDGfFu7-7NMZ0cSXvp23Sxg5mSoG/view?usp=sharing) to introduce the diffusion of innovations in agriculture. \nBridges versus barriers to agricultural innovations - **use** [**this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/14hpIRr7sTVsH_CFAt57W6b5zrdxpEZkhGYSqmErsF1A/edit?usp=sharing) to distinguish between the two. Classify them according to physical, political and economic factors.\nInnovations in the Green Revolution - [**presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1odB8U9uilfhpnpmgPvm3JxG1jqghLJqB66h1h9Enqi8/edit?usp=sharing) and [**cards to explain the impact/rate of diffusion**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1XOBeksYbuAmlWNtjJsREM2ECbgmALosZ/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true)\n\n**3. Variations in food consumption**\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2019-10-15-at-8-34-19-pm_orig.png)\n\n{{youtube UPirDWfxfKc}}\n\na) **What physical and human processes cause variations in food consumption?** Use the images on [**these slides**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1YxoKX2b7rhpRBU1VGQlUfISBaQ5ehmFpyRddVmYttCw/edit?usp=sharing) to respond to this question. \nb) Download [**this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1IaR7pwOzvRkBz-oVsGcMtYBwqHCcd_TQYbR2Kaz5egM/edit?usp=sharing.) on how social systems/culture influence food consumption patterns and trends in three different societies.\n"},"future-food-security-and-sustainability.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/future-food-security-and-sustainability.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Future food security and sustainability\"\nparent: food-and-health\norder: 80\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-food-security-and-sustainability.html\n---\n\n[**Starter**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1IxViPIb3po1UB9UXO8zAmeCpaCf_Pecg_FMmmXLqDA8/edit?usp=sharing)**: Read through** [**this short overview**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1uloP6l_I0Ig8otVpQT57YhcGE8lzTDST/view?usp=sharing) **of sustainable agriculture.**\n\n**1. Case study of an attempt to tackle food insecurity: the Loess Plateau, NW China**\n\nWatch[**these two video clips**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1j3uz30Cass7SEGERKcbqUmxzG5TXassJ?usp=sharing)that show what actions were taken in the Loess Plateau as an example of tackling food insecurity through sustainable management of the environment and involvement of local people. \nDivide your notes into: a) HOW did they do it and b) What were the RESULTS?\n\n{{youtube BlwnUVjEhsU}}\n\n**2. Contemporary approaches to food production/achieving food security****In groups, you will investigate and present/share on one of these approaches, evaluating its advantages and disadvantages:**\na) Genetically modified food\nb) Vertical farming\nc) In-vitro meat\nd) Reducing food waste\nUse the resources [**in this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1-vdy-ioyZopdCD2AO3kM-xdbeNYOOGfX?usp=sharing) to prepare a presentation to share in a \"marketplace\" activity.\n\n![Picture](assets/in-vitro-meat-2_orig.jpg)\n\n**Plenary**: **TOK:** Artificial meat stops cruelty to animals, is better for the environment, and it could be safer and more efficient, and even healthier. Animal welfare groups are generally in favor of the production of in vitro meat because it does not have a nervous system and therefore cannot feel pain.\n\n**Q:** Is in vitro meat less objectionable than traditionally obtained meat - as it does not involve killing and reduces the risk of animal cruelty - or is it unnatural and are scientists interfering with nature?\n"},"future-resilience--adaptation.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/future-resilience--adaptation.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Future Resilience & Adaptation\"\nparent: geophysical-hazards\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-resilience--adaptation.html\n---\n\n**Global geophysical hazard/disaster trends and future projections**\nDownload a copy of [**this document**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BA0lkUEMbi75-DIvq4mn5w1RjIc9gns7/view?usp=sharing)with information, graphs and an infographic. Write responses to questions 1-4.\nExtension: Visit [**this website**](https://ourworldindata.org/natural-disasters) to find out more about the past and present trends in geophysical hazards/disasters. \nIn view of the past/present trends, what are the likely possibilities in the future in terms of:\na. The death toll and population exposure to geophysical hazards\nb. The estimated damage costs from geophysical hazards\nc. Worldwide distribution of a and b?\n\n**Geophysical hazard adaptation** - accept the hazard risk\nRead [**these three short articles**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1GcLEqbuXnWDuBrxODrK_HStBTdZCb0Sa?usp=sharing) that explain three different ways in which Californians are adapting to earthquakes.\n\n**Pre-event management strategies** - mitigate against the hazard risk\nUnlike adaptation, where people accept hazard risk and adjust accordingly, pre-event management involves new possibilities in technology and engineering/building design that mitigate the hazard risk. Remember adaptation versus mitigation strategies in response to climate change! \nMake a copy of [**this document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vvuv2z-Ej8ykwlRe6tx4KDBYrwT8lfe7iCR8CmZPkLM/edit?usp=sharing). Use the links to explain the different strategies used in different places.\nIn each, consider the feasibility in HICs versus LICs.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2020-02-01-at-16-46-43_orig.png)\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2020-02-01-at-16-46-24_orig.png)\n\n**Post-event management strategies:****The 3Rs: Relief (Rescue) Rehabilitation, Reconstruction**\nMake a copy of [**this document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1yLjIEGw5-ZadoOod88HcL_KYG4AFDv339cElHenUg-w/edit?usp=sharing) to see if you can distinguish between the 3Rs.\nAdd to your notes from [**this textbook page**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/159LxCNQLVDI_tvbwi1SJ4yCytOpZNEBK/view?usp=sharing). What factors are important in the reconstruction stage?\nWatch the video below that shows how technology has been used in the reconstruction effort in Haiti.\n\n{{youtube Oou32o-jR0M}}\n\n[**Geophysical Systems**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/geophysical-systems.html)[**Geophysical Hazard Risks**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-risks.html)[**Hazard Risk & Vulnerability: The Case Studies**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-case-studies.html)\n"},"future-tourism-sustainable-tourism.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/future-tourism-sustainable-tourism.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Future tourism, sustainable tourism\"\nparent: managing-tourism-and-sport-for-the-future\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-tourism-sustainable-tourism.html\n---\n\n**Sustainable Tourism and 'Ecotourism'**\n\n{{youtube 7X1u7OdwYyE}}\n\nWatch the two videos and [**use this link**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1iq6yPP3hwo0_KP-zPvA6BScoD56biBZ2/view?usp=sharing)to help you define sustainable tourism and ecotourism.\n\n{{youtube 1i4ioqIaXrE}}\n\n**Next:** Read the [**pros and cons**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MUVq0wR0ihNY5crcFnRYTz2tKGBG3n51/view?usp=sharing) of ecotourism and watch this - [**How green is ecotourism?**](https://youtu.be/uES2Ts0YhpA)\nUse this information to create a mind map to evaluate ecotourism as a strategy.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-05-09-at-11-38-23-am.png)\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-05-09-at-11-38-01-am.png)\n\n**Case study of sustainable tourism in a Low Income country:****An investigation into Kenya**[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1VsgUOO3GPVkDI0sGJ3snJYw7i1DF2RBS5clWKIWid_Y/edit?usp=sharing)[:](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1VsgUOO3GPVkDI0sGJ3snJYw7i1DF2RBS5clWKIWid_Y/edit?usp=sharing) Introducing tourism in Kenya (and the Maasai). \n[**Use these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1tDJHTrsz1N61DAF9SEeEr-pVTefjbvUC?usp=sharing) to evaluate the impact of sustainable/ecotourism on Kenya, with a particular focus on the country's safari tourism.\n**Consider:**\na) The environmental impact of safari tourism in Kenya\nb) The social and economic impact on local communities, particularly the Maasai\n\n**What are the challenges and POSSIBILITIES for tourism in the future?** \nMake a copy of [**this table**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1iM7S7aWekvxtJ6gxicxygVsdjve_2BO17-iv67-3Mvg/edit?usp=sharing)**.** Follow the links in each section to record the **possible** impact of social media, international security, and 'diasporas' on future tourism.\n\n**Homework practice: Plan a long response** - \"Using examples from Low Income countries, evaluate whether sustainable tourism is an effective strategy\". \nOne page plan or two pages in full. \nIntro – define key terms, introduce the main ideas, including definitions of sustainable tourism and ecotourism. \nMain body paragraphs – divide up into benefits/ costs for the environment and people. Use case studies and specific examples from Kenya. Think about people (especially the Maasai) and the environment (especially game parks). \nConclusion – on balance, is it an effective strategy?\n"},"geographical-themes.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/geographical-themes.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Geographical Themes\"\norder: 0\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/geographical-themes.html\n---\n\n"},"geophysical-hazards.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/geophysical-hazards.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Geophysical Hazards\"\nparent: geographical-themes\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/geophysical-hazards.html\n---\n\n**This option unit divides up as follows:**\n\n[**Geophysical Systems**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/geophysical-systems.html)[**Geophysical Hazard Risks**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-risks.html)[**Hazard Risk & Vulnerability: The Case Studies**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-case-studies.html)[**Future Resilience & Adaptation**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-resilience--adaptation.html)\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2019-12-10-at-6-22-13-pm_orig.png)\n"},"geophysical-systems.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/geophysical-systems.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Geophysical Systems\"\nparent: geophysical-hazards\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/geophysical-systems.html\n---\n\n**The mechanisms of plate movement**\nStarter: Visit [**this interactive site**](https://dinosaurpictures.org/ancient-earth#0) ... what did the world look like millions of years ago?\n[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1MSyI0qxdnH4_6As1ng21QsvGuyEpYlImoiewTJI-MnQ/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce you to the mechanisms of plate movement. Record definitions of all key words. \nMarketplace activity: [**Use this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1-vCepJ_UVlNdVCyyIt_mDYojIbzxjjKm?usp=sharing) to access information on what takes place at each type of plate margin. \n**Extension:** Visit [**this site**](https://qz.com/577842/scientists-have-used-groundbreaking-technology-to-figure-out-how-the-earth-looked-a-billion-years-ago/) as an extra of what scientists know today.\n\n**The characteristics of volcanoes formed by varying types of volcanic eruption, and their distribution.** \nThis is your homework investigation!\n1. Make a copy of [**this table**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1zdn7fO_zKfZbc2AjLHgadJaSvwUW14dJoBGz-ZdAPDQ/edit?usp=sharing) and the links built within to complete information on the three main types of volcano - Shield, Cinder Cone and Stratovolcano (AKA Composite volcano).\n2. On the second page, use the maps to describe and explain the distribution of active volcanoes worldwide. Refer to types of volcano in relation to types of plate margins and other locations where volcanoes occur.\n\n**The characteristics of earthquakes by varying types of plate margin movement and human triggers, and their distribution.**[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1ufnLXI4kMpGytB91ARIba1oNO9p_Y3bYHqInnGaj7To/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce the processes that cause earthquakes, and their distribution at different scales.\nVideo - [**\"the anatomy of an earthquake\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1zVjkfguCfsje2cSxt58mnfqN3CyHvtGt/view?usp=sharing) - 7 minutes - must watch!\nTake notes from [**this textbook extract**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1I-zl7ffB-H3HFV61q3JA71-nlJ5c1Fqh/view?usp=sharing) to supplement your notes. \nOnce you have read through the presentation/watched the video, download your own copy of [**this Word document**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Y1_ztblzELJKPyJzzgLV3SJPcKNT6ceL/view?usp=sharing) to complete a gap-fill activity on the characteristics of earthquakes.\n\n**The classification of mass movement types according to their physical and human causes, and their distribution.**[Presentation](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1rcavLKdkuYXP3RjNE9kGLqgyeFc9HBsFScsuc97fkOw/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce types of mass movement and the natural processes and human triggers that cause them.  \nMake a copy and take detailed notes from [this summary of types of mass movement](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1JuuIpWHSnMTuuPN-zwP77LMNRZZTaF8l3e-2xUp5NeU/edit?usp=sharing). \nMake a copy of [this document](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-SstikcF1PvnhTNXw9Zks-UAz3va0ZcjyUySUCPDmIg/edit?usp=sharing). Answer the questions to consolidate your understanding.\n\n[**Geophysical Hazard Risks**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-risks.html)[**Hazard Risk & Vulnerability: The Case Studies**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-case-studies.html)[**Future Resilience & Adaptation**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-resilience--adaptation.html)\n"},"glastonbury.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/glastonbury.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Glastonbury\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-local-and-national-scale\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/glastonbury.html\n---\n\n{{youtube bX4Anwyzhmw}}\n\nStart by reading through [**this presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1MAVUD16cXNKl_oRphnBDMh0SjE1duaLl-wgz4fuZO6Y/edit?usp=sharing) that introduces Glastonbury festival and its location.\n\nTake some notes!\n\n2. What is a [**temporary site of leisure**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vlesSnlY635wEAOpUJdEm2kym0B2BHQU9g8z47Bjnj0/edit?usp=sharing)? Why might some people consider a site of leisure to be permanent, while others consider it to be temporary? (Think about the nature of the activity and how long the site is used.)\n\n**3. Site and** [**situation**](https://www.rgs.org/schools/teaching-resources/mapping-festivals/the-geography-of-glastonbury/) **of Glastonbury festival**\nUse [**this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1srrpbqY15J9JTRhiSr1axarDXftVpnaxRGO-7a2n-5Y/edit?usp=sharing) to learn the definitions of site and situation, and which ones are significant for the location of Glastonbury festival. Why might the site and situation be **unsuitable** for a festival like Glastonbury?\n\n**4. Geographical impacts of the festival**[**Use this table**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1MxDpX-IQYRnmqO5dU2CR8uc9lF6JZ6InNeK32B_GjuU/edit?usp=sharing) to record the costs and benefits of Glastonbury on this rural location. As usual, think SEEP!\n\nSources for you to use: \nAn [**overview of costs and benefits**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13QrTxqmFzw6dUYuQtF0x2KmhbYkjWQSXi-nfii-BADQ/edit?usp=sharing)from Geographycasestudy.com \n[**Local benefits**](https://www.glastonburyfestivals.co.uk/worthy-causes/local-benefits/) (Glastonbury website) \n**G**[**reen initiatives at the festival**](https://www.glastonburyfestivals.co.uk/information/green-glastonbury/) [in 2019](https://www.glastonburyfestivals.co.uk/information/green-glastonbury/) from Michael Eavis, the organiser. \n[**Article**](https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/07/04/glastonbury-is-over-but-what-might-it-look-like-in-future-artists-proposing-sustainable-model?fbclid=IwY2xjawLVOO5leHRuA2FlbQIxMABicmlkETFROVJlQ3dRb1YyZkNZb24yAR6atrrH8KK4TN5S4ry51HfrD-b6ncoInqi2ydRtKFAzxF1xs2C096E5iTC7dQ_aem_ud_rkSuABqqncBHXzDEhrA) on sustainable models for the future of the festival (June 2025)\nVideo shorts: [**Festivals are an environmental disaster**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/17_ReSTiOmMMY8yPAGepTKT9ojpXbE8PB/view?usp=sharing) and [**Glastonbury is banning single use plastic bottles**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1mvePHmhBf6NsGyjqDgVHWUObFv31egXX/view?usp=sharing) (March 2019)\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-02-09-at-12-20-31-pm.png)\n\n**As you take notes from the different sources:**\nExplain the phrase, \"fallow\" years with reference to the Glastonbury site.  \nDescribe the potential conflicts between visitors and residents that result from Glastonbury festival.\nExplain what measures are being taken to manage the environmental impacts of the festival today.\n\n**5. The impact of COVID-19**\nRead [**this short article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eviIqnOD3P1e9FDTDAindBTp5e98Ra6-/view?usp=sharing) (BBC March 2020) as an update since Glastonbury has now been cancelled two years in a row. \nWhat is the economic impact of cancelling the festival, particularly for the local economy?\n\n**Extension:** Explain why charities/ civil societies such as Greenpeace have participated in the festival despite the environmental problems that it causes.\n"},"hazard-case-studies.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/hazard-case-studies.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Hazard Case Studies\"\nparent: geophysical-hazards\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-case-studies.html\n---\n\n**Starter**: Can you say [**Eyjafjallajökull**](https://youtu.be/7hhziJTtMiE)?What's the [**connection**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13AP3Yw1mvvsr1SxMxs7MQXYLFsbTfOU7_6gKRcgGrtM/edit?usp=sharing)[?](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13AP3Yw1mvvsr1SxMxs7MQXYLFsbTfOU7_6gKRcgGrtM/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n{{youtube wh9Sg6Ejwrc}}\n\n**Case Study:** Compare and contrast - Use the resource linked in the [**template**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bYPlZ1619_D2HmiEOwYJd4kyzEBYPUko3j0ngFajf3w/edit?usp=sharing) to create a case study of our two contrasting volcanic hazards. \na) [**Eyjafjallajökull, Iceland – 2010 (HIC)**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/10KTnZoh-0Kc2ESqC58WhusxR8I6bsmP4?usp=sharing)\nb) [**Merapi, Indonesia – 2010 (LIC)**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1mlBQSIg4Of759jZvTEkofHHSpDRSptA3?usp=sharing)\n\n**Starter:** What causes a tsunami?\n\n{{youtube oWzdgBNfhQU}}\n\n**2. Case Study:** Compare and contrast - Use the resources linked in the [**template**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1DSv0CZ5KEVodYO1B34Q02B8qJRuedr3f_KW7lCaw8p4/edit?usp=sharing) to create a case study of our two contrasting earthquake hazards. \na) [**Japan - 2011 (HIC)**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/18dITynRoZjxi8rTUWOes4GHypgoF7-tl?usp=sharing)\nb) [**Haiti - 2010 (LIC)**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1UP4kIthwehAYlfwGearEwFpevm11GOmf?usp=sharing)\n\n**3. Case Study:** Test yourself with [**Quizlet**](https://quizlet.com/_5bhdcd?x=1jqt&i=2nah3c) - Which did you get wrong? Make a note of the question and the correct answer.\nCompare and contrast - Use the resources linked in the [**template**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bp2kzwErMaoRAlWGaPfPSt2q0gTvre4f2v6c8mGTCsg/edit?usp=sharing) to create a case study of our two contrasting mass movement hazards. \na) [**Oso, Washington State, USA mudflow 2014 (HIC)**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1PZEqQ8S0lRZLz86Zbas6p1sswm2FwBpe?usp=sharing) \nb) [**Freetown, Sierra Leone landslide 2017 (LIC)**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1dSz__5LAobmW6hgIh9Wbgyx8MbpMlPIk?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary:** [**Compare and contrast**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1gC4xckpblEumEmmcxM0y6038vxdSQqtf4EOh1qPCjIM/edit?usp=sharing)**.** **Extra:** [**Revision quiz for GeoHazards.**](https://www.thinkib.net/geography/page/25897/revision-quizes-for-geophysical-hazards)\n\n**Next:** [**Future Resilience & Adaptation**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-resilience--adaptation.html)\n\n[**Geophysical Systems**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/geophysical-systems.html)[**Geophysical Hazard Risks**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-risks.html)\n"},"hazard-risks.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/hazard-risks.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Hazard Risks\"\nparent: geophysical-hazards\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-risks.html\n---\n\n**The relevance of hazard magnitude and frequency/recurrence of risk management.**\n\n**Volcanic eruptions: primary and secondary hazards**\nUse [this presentation](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1zPswIzuph4IVJo2VSqvhlrlGVqD-H5Oa8T-GHA_8lbY/edit?usp=sharing) to learn about volcanic hazards\n\n**Earthquakes: primary and secondary hazards**\nUse [this presentation](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1IeYvYu7OypHGpCIwkqAVuFsa-Zzv_5lQZAesCSVI3oE/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce you to the hazards caused by earthquakes.\n\n**Factors that affect geophysical hazard risk and vulnerability - economic, social, demographic and political**[Presentation](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1A2UDYdiREOAnhOmRryY7ewl4sY6Gs0P-i9KGLBcWO54/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce you to the concepts of hazard, risk and vulnerability. \nMake sure you fully understand the information, as it will frame our investigation into different case studies.\n\n{{youtube w4GDNGcLg3g}}\n\n[**Geophysical Systems**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/geophysical-systems.html)[**Hazard Risk & Vulnerability: The Case Studies**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/hazard-case-studies.html)[**Future Resilience & Adaptation**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-resilience--adaptation.html)\n"},"introducing-tourism.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/introducing-tourism.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Introducing tourism\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-international-scale\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/introducing-tourism.html\n---\n\n[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1BkP8iXiv4Nj2xbEAyxFNueSPofl5rIypY5sWLwtKULM/edit?usp=sharing) Trends in tourism worldwide\n\n![Picture](assets/categorizing-tourism-mind-map-1.png)\n\n**How can we classify tourism?**\na) Read through [**these definitions of types of tourism**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sWlrkYIdnGzupjurwg_dpdau2KNhrFFnUTGuPu0Ss6c/edit?usp=sharing). Add them to your glossary.\nb) Explain how two types of touristic activities (such as a skiing holiday and an organised city tour) vary in terms of their cost, duration and destination.\n\n**Demand and supply of tourism**\nComplete the tasks on [**this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/16j1rQbfU8-2Js8S-DOGNQbOiOCEFCb3inEcYM2i4m9A/edit?usp=sharing)that introduce you to **primary versus secondary tourist resources and allocentric versus psychocentric tourists.** \nUse [**this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qGqVyOz4wOHtV6E8PvOH9fWe9_U782gJUmhLF5baGCA/edit?usp=sharing) to explain how the family cycle of tourism influences the demand for tourism in specific places.\n\n**Plenary - Exam-style questions**\nDefine the term \"niche tourism\" and \"mass tourism\".\nExplain the difference between primary tourist/recreational resources and secondary tourist/recreational resources. \nWith reference to tourism, what is meant by \"the multiplier effect\"?\n"},"ko-phi-phi-a-rural-hotspot.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/ko-phi-phi-a-rural-hotspot.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Ko Phi Phi: A rural hotspot\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-local-and-national-scale\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/ko-phi-phi-a-rural-hotspot.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Use [**this website**](http://www.phiphi.phuket.com/top10/top10-phiphi-attractions.htm) to find out the **primary** and **secondary** tourist resources available on Ko Phi Phi, Thailand to explain its popularity.  \n[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1tl1fnLZfMXLLYagvIQdqTYnK3zLbJqc_SQG8pHyk6Iw/edit?usp=sharing)Introducing Ko Phi Phi and the issues concerning pressures on its carrying capacity.\n\n**An inquiry into the impact of tourism on this rural tourism hotspot: Ko Phi Phi as your case study**\nUse [**this folder of resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1qstlJUE_un0Pk89WBTpMJNggC7lmA_8j?usp=sharing) to record detailed evidence on the impact of tourism according to the different types of **carrying capacity** - **physical, environmental** and **perceptual.**\n\n**And since?** [**Read this update**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1gE0rqu_WQo1dAjpfvM3DTbxLLnAsaYQk/view?usp=sharing) (The Guardian May 2022) \n**Extra** [**Phi Phi islands' sustainable tourism renaissance - in pictures**](https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/gallery/2021/dec/20/phi-phi-islands-thailand-sustainable-tourism-renaissance?fbclid=IwAR2xUHY7y40B9fJiYyIFudFeEQZASYMl-P2Q3HOCsn2COv_YDdArbUj_-pk) (December 2021)\n\n**Plenary:** Discuss your choice(s) or new ideas to deal with the management of the islands. Be prepared to debate your own point of view.\n"},"leisure-sport-and-tourism.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/leisure-sport-and-tourism.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Leisure, sport and tourism\"\nparent: geographical-themes\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/leisure-sport-and-tourism.html\n---\n\n**Leisure, Sport & Tourism**\n\nAs more people join the “global middle class”, they have disposable incomes allowing participation in tourism, including international travel and different types of sport. Sport can also be an important use of leisure time for people on low incomes who cannot afford to participate in tourism.\n\nWhile tourism often has an urban focus, rural areas provide another important geographical setting for touristic activities, including walking, enjoying wilderness, doing extreme sports or visiting heritage sites. The uses made of places vary greatly, depending on physical geography, history and level of economic development.\n\n**Links to the sub-sections of the unit:**[**Changing leisure patterns**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/changing-leisure-patterns.html)[**Tourism and sport at the local and national scale**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/vung-tau-a-tourist-hotspot.html) [**Tourism and sport at an international scale**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/niche-national-tourism-strategies.html)[**Managing tourism and sport for the future**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/going-the-distance-kenyan-athletes.html)\n"},"managing-pandemics.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/managing-pandemics.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Managing pandemics\"\nparent: food-and-health\norder: 60\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/managing-pandemics.html\n---\n\n{{youtube UG8YbNbdaco}}\n\nAs background context for any pandemic, watch the TED short video above (2012), and read [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rL_yTZtMVhXjNXZWIec5W8FziIHLN2pJ/view?usp=sharing) written in 2016.\n**What do we know now that we didn't know then? What does this say about local and global awareness before COVID-19?**\n\n**COVID-19 - Managing the pandemic**[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1GtDY4iAG-0qAH-sVrFg_kc2iKLKvpn2b56faivoIxts/edit?usp=sharing) - the diffusion of the virus, the geographic impacts, management to create diffusion barriers, the role of the media. \nRead [**these two articles**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1FAcFY4XGIGQUw5d5pyW7bH6WG5etajQH?usp=sharing) and respond to the following:\n1. Explain why Low-Income countries have suffered greater challenges from COVID-19.\n2. Why has the international action in response to COVID-19 led to \"vaccine apartheid\" since 2021? What are the implications for the future management of COVID-19 as a result?\n**Evaluating the management of the pandemic - at an international and national scale**[**World Health Organization video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1d5StbXE04W51-BoLU5YwKUx8vOuxaIjA/view?usp=sharing) (5 mins). BBC article (May 2021): [**Serious failures in the WHO and global response**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZJqviP6hNU0_CFdRHFx-1MAk5d452ahs/view?usp=sharing). \nHow did Vietnam compare with other countries like the USA on a **national** scale? \nUse [**this folder of resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1L7IyYRj-bRczdbmm9BDOYdlt_t-gMLZf?usp=sharing) to investigate.\n\n**Managing pandemics/prevention versus management of disease: Focus on HIV-AIDS**\n\n[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/19w3vfldCjqWsXMfB3YsJFgeufxdvSB8Ax7r96Q2_vKg/edit?usp=sharing)focusing on managing HIV in Thailand \nTED Talk:[**How Mr. Condom made Thailand a better place**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/19OKnuxADeOoma6cJZV05AobSHdaLoJ43/view?usp=sharing) [**Articles to read as a follow-up**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/15i5TQDiiM64r9Tq6s9XnRdEC9GbyQ1B8?usp=sharing)\n"},"managing-tourism-and-sport-for-the-future.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/managing-tourism-and-sport-for-the-future.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Managing tourism and sport for the future\"\nparent: leisure-sport-and-tourism\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/managing-tourism-and-sport-for-the-future.html\n---\n\n**Use the links below:**\n\n[**Sustainable tourism and factors influencing future international tourism: Focus on one Low Income country**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/future-tourism-sustainable-tourism.html) **- Kenya**\n\n[**Political and cultural influences on sports participation, including a case study of Kenya's long distance runners**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/politicalcultural-influences-on-sport.html)\n"},"measuring-food.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/measuring-food.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Measuring Food\"\nparent: food-and-health\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/measuring-food.html\n---\n\n**Tuning in:** Read this article, [**\"have we reached peak food?\"**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1qEIV26gga0G0rPsVmUafXSGJASt0e4MQAPp6O9EHR2g/edit?usp=sharing) Summarise its message as a tweet.\n\n{{youtube QJG7HmQyYcY}}\n\n**1: Malnutrition and hunger (undernourishment)**\na) Define:\nmalnutrition \ncalorie intake  b) Complete this [**short 'top and tail' task**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1RXVf1qL5kLlH0bhOY8oHJGGXzMgmYhy78QUDteACPKY/edit?usp=sharing) to understand the types of malnutrition. \nc) Complete this [**map task**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/16S7XcA6uY_K3iiJUABFxMwSxXHhMqbD6aWh3FpDCdGk/edit?usp=sharing) that explores the global patterns of calorie intake and types of malnutrition.\nd) Suggest reasons why calories intake may not be a reliable indicator of nutrition.\n\n{{youtube L3abZswA5XY}}\n\n**2:**[**The Global Hunger Index**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ekqFMKR3kMMpsU3LqcIoKcg12kjA8yuU/view?usp=sharing)\n\na) Visit [**this site**](https://www.globalhungerindex.org/about/) to find out how the Global Hunger Index is calculated.\n\nb) Use the map below to describe the pattern of the Global Hunger Index in 2018.\n\nc) Suggest at least two reasons why some countries have been successful in improving their Global Hunger Index score.\n\nd) Read [**this article**](https://www.weforum.org/agenda/2020/07/global-hunger-rising-food-agriculture-organization-report/) by the World Economic Forum (2020). What reasons are given for why global hunger is increasing again?\n\n![Picture](assets/csm-2021-global-hunger-index-map-5238b2efdd_orig.png)\n\n**Food security** “exists when all people, at all times, have physical, social and economic access to sufficient, safe and nutritious food to meet their dietary needs and food preferences for an active and healthy life.\"\nSource: [**FAO**](http://www.fao.org/) **|** [**Food and Agriculture Organisation of United Nations**](http://www.fao.org/)\n\n**3: The food security index**\nThe Food Security Index is produced by The Economist, measuring the level of food security in 113 countries. It uses both qualitative and quantitative data based on food affordability, availability, and quality. Within these three components, 28 different indicators are used making the index an extremely thorough measure of the complex factors that cause food insecurity.\n\n![Picture](assets/components-of-food-security-index.png)\n\na) Complete this task to learn about the [**components of food security.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1eJKUYyiFc4MVdFg4OTPOT8H2NaUTAVjid5RV_7F65PQ/edit?usp=sharing) \nb) In what ways is the Global Hunger Index different from the Food Security Index?\nc) [**Visit**](https://foodsecurityindex.eiu.com/index)and click \"visual analysis\". \nd) Visit [**this webpage**](https://foodsecurityindex.eiu.com/Country) that shows an interactive map of the food security index today. Describe and attempt to explain the pattern.\n\nThe **nutrition transition** is the shift in dietary patterns that coincides with economic, demographic, and epidemiological (health) changes.\n\n**4: The nutrition transition**\n\n**Look at the diagram opposite.** How do the stages of 'nutrition transition' connect with the stages of the 'demographic transition' model?\n\n**Look at the graph below.** What is the relationship between increased nutritional energy supply and GDP per capita? How does this relationship confirm the 'nutrition transition'?\n\n![Picture](assets/nutrition-transition_orig.png)\n\n![Picture](assets/nutrition-energy-supply-vs-gdp_orig.png)\n\n**5: Focus on obesity, a consequence of the 'nutrition transition'**\n\n{{youtube jMqxTuoWqsQ}}\n\na) Watch the animation above and study the infographic. Describe the trends in obesity worldwide.\n\nb) Read [**this Guardian article about obesity**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1YxZ5GdL3GEsEHVHJxf-TGxKHg1Xx7b2z/view?usp=sharing) (Feb 2015).  \nWhat does it suggest about the causes of the rise in obesity, and how this trend needs to be managed?\n\nc) [**What are the fattest countries in the world?**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sRNtCdyZ8bM&feature=youtu.be) Watch this short video as an extra.\n\n![Picture](assets/infographic-obesity_orig.jpg)\n\n[**Plenary**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_AWGBl90YClQVAPq1uEB8TVdePt3JBVU/view?usp=sharing)**:** TOK - Are all forms of malnutrition equally serious? Which ones are self-inflicted and which do people have little control over? How long does temporary hunger last?\n"},"measuring-health.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/measuring-health.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Measuring Health\"\nparent: food-and-health\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/measuring-health.html\n---\n\n**Part 1: Health indicators**\na) Make a copy of [**this table**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1n020EKDIsvfnS93GsIm1Ba1eo8gReGwnbJrHyHBPBAU/edit?usp=sharing) to record information on these key measures of health - \nb) Write a paragraph response to the following statement: ‘The HALE index is a more effective measure of the health of a nation than average life expectancy’.\nc) Video extra: [**\"How will you die?\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Qb7cpC3PwiNFbkcYOgRMsQWFOWogYvCL/view?usp=sharing)\nd) Read [**this short article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1S7VJ1qrDqeI8xEbHY5UlXMom_WnS7Rkw/view?usp=sharing) (NPR, Dec 2020). In your own words, describe how the causes of death have changed worldwide, and regionally, since 2000. \ne) [**Global deaths from different causes since 2000**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jI68_d5ljQoSKE1GuOn9Js2wlVTB9HmY/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Part 2: Understanding the \"epidemiological transition\", how patterns of disease and mortality change over time.**\n\na) What are the three stages of the \"epidemiological transition\"? Watch [**this video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1L86ALvWk4nDjHaDN9UhkjfKWjDm8WsfR/view?usp=sharing) to find out.\n\nb) How do the stages of \"epidemiological transition\" link with the stages of nutrition transition and demographic transition? Use the diagram opposite to help you.\n\nc) Use [**this site**](https://vizhub.healthdata.org/gbd-compare/) to compare the causes of death (years of disability/HALE) in different countries over time by age/gender or overall population. What surprises you?\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2019-10-14-at-2-57-33-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Part 3: The implications of a global ageing population for disease.**\n\na) What is meant by the [**Global Burden of Disease (GBD)**](http://www.healthdata.org/gbd/about)?\nb) Read this article, [**\"China's hidden epidemic\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/18Sslcf1rz_5jPArUWaQvn5gzjWQKfc2m/view?usp=sharing) (The Guardian, Sept 2022)\ni) Explain the implications of changing diets and lifestyles/habits as China develops. \nii) What management challenges is China facing as its populations age and get richer?\nc) Extra - [**Read this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1aMJw6S9DanL-mF2Z8yWnikqV1Qd3YPy5/view?usp=sharing) (January 2022) that gives another example that illustrate the epidemiological transition: \"Global spread of autoimmune disease blamed on western diet\".\n\n**Plenary: TOK** - There is a theory known as Hart's Inverse Care Law that states that those who need health care the most, the poor, the homeless and the other vulnerable groups - have the least access to it. Those who need health care the least- the well off- have most access to health care. **To what extent is this true or fair?** What data from this unit so far supports this view?\n"},"micro-climates-traffic--pollution.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/micro-climates-traffic--pollution.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Micro climates, traffic & pollution\"\nparent: urban-environment-and-social-stresses\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/micro-climates-traffic--pollution.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** How does the Walkie Talkie building impact temperatures in the area?\n\n{{youtube eiK3tcjJXiA}}\n\n**Presentation: Urban microclimates—modification (including UHIE) and management**\nIn pairs, read the two resources below. Debrief each other once you are ready. \n[**Urban micro-climate reading**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/12YWEiJXzR83walnAlbypM5UU4IZPJyLK/view?usp=sharing)[**Urban heat island reading**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KQcR_85up_i1sQGGwshhZIFkn9ciOZIW/view?usp=sharing)**Quick task:** With the help of the presentation, complete the following tasks:\nWhat is the difference between microclimate & UHIE? \nSummarize the causes & effects of the UHIE . \nOutline the potential management strategies for UHIE.  [**Extension video**](https://youtu.be/4SihdPRRMPI)\n\n**Case study of air pollution and traffic congestion in London - the impact on people and management responses.** [**Presentation:**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1AruHLjZVyBP_h2zbgCvXpd-kFDyp6M3w_IreVjvcTQc/edit?usp=sharing) Watch the video below to begin to understand the cause, effects and solutions of London's traffic congestion and subsequent pollution problem (the worst in Western Europe).\n\n{{youtube LvHhagyIXm4}}\n\n**Pollution, traffic and congestion: patterns and trends**\nWhat is the pattern [**globally**](https://waqi.info/)? \nLondon's [**road use?**](https://www.theguardian.com/uk/datablog/2012/apr/30/lond-road-use-data) Describe the patterns locally.\n\n**Air pollution and its varying impacts on people**\nWhat are the different[**impacts on people?**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1jMdbh8nzBhyFJBVqtHRQDwARUUxEShWh?usp=sharing)Aim to include the following in your notes. \nAsthma\nLung conditions\nPremature deaths\nHeart disease\nAlzheimer’s & Parkinson’s. \n**Management responses to traffic congestion**\nWhat is being done to reduce this [**traffic problem?**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/13HiTHxVTKHNtLl7tJ1-JCAE-ZryV52ml?usp=sharing)Aim to include the following in your notes.\nAn ultra low emissions zone.\nA congestion charge zone. \nScrappage schemes.\nClean Air Act\nTraffic control\nCycling and walking infrastructure. \n**Go to the next page:**[**Social stresses - contested land use, managing the impacts of urban social deprivation**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/contested-land-use--managing-urban-deprivation.html)\n"},"national-tourism-strategy-viet-nam.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/national-tourism-strategy-viet-nam.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"National Tourism Strategy: Viet Nam\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-international-scale\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/national-tourism-strategy-viet-nam.html\n---\n\n[**Đổi Mới (New Thinking)**](https://www.weforum.org/agenda/2018/09/how-vietnam-became-an-economic-miracle/): How long has Viet Nam been open to tourism?\nWatch [**this video**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=04Kf_0kppPM), a virtual tour.\n\nStarter [**'heads & tails' activity**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1dzE9i2bTYVI2vKd194fypcXo4Lk9yeP-sERdSW203Cc/edit?usp=sharing) - why has tourism grown so quickly in Viet Nam? \n[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1bH36Aw8wEquD5E8FXfyVq8JkfrOaNLf-WH0D46BBYPs/edit?usp=sharing) for an overview on the types of impacts of tourism\n\n[**Activity**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1DA6e0gSlvu42PMU3Kl2KNEYdRaQ9VnSiIivDE2j5lzg/edit?usp=sharing) - categorising the impacts of tourism in Viet Nam according to SEEP\n\n**An overview of tourism as a national development strategy** - the rationale and the potential impacts - [**website**](https://geographyfieldwork.com/TourismProsCons.htm) and a [**document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1BuNpGrdM4BwyvycUy7lvdL8YXODQmRirCdqU6ue798w/edit?usp=sharing).\n\n{{youtube 6bX6iah_4Ec}}\n\n**Tourism as a development strategy for Viet Nam**\na) Read [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1I9myx4juUhBWFlm4zzAY-vFnyJuim7ag/view?usp=sharing) that focuses on the benefits and drawbacks of tourism as a development strategy for the country, including the challenges/goals faced by the Viet Nam government today. Turn it into your own notes, categorising the issues into SEEP and including specific examples. \nb) Find 1-2 more articles to explain what has happened to Viet Nam's tourism sector since COVID. To what extent has the government's development strategy been affected, at least in the short-term?\n\n**Be ready to use the information in this case study to write a summative long response!**\n\n**Light relief:**[**Jack Whitehall travels with his father**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1VGS93RNwyHbt9oLc9_qs9Cl-F9kjsfwm/view?usp=sharing) in Hoi An\n\n**NEXT** - [**an exploration of 'niche' tourism with a focus on New Zealand and another location of your choice**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/niche-tourism---nz.html)\n"},"niche-tourism---nz.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/niche-tourism---nz.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Niche Tourism - NZ\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-international-scale\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/niche-tourism---nz.html\n---\n\nWhy the fuss?\n\n{{youtube HynsTvRVLiI}}\n\n‘Niche’ tourism - \"special interest tourism that caters for relatively small numbers of tourists\".\n\n[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1oed40juYt9yRfNRgwG8SKuRo-3b-eUh7_cAGWBZUhzs/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce NZ tourism.\n\n**1. 'Niche' Tourism in New Zealand**\nWatch [**this video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Cwv-LcMNNSNsf0nKDGQN1TPEvn_-xouC/view?usp=sharing)[.](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Cwv-LcMNNSNsf0nKDGQN1TPEvn_-xouC/view?usp=sharing) Record different examples of 'niche' tourism in New Zealand. Can you categorise them?\n\n**a) Adventure tourism**[**Use this source**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1b_rnshh9XAeLaGeAH1PftqVEpqq91dlk/view?usp=sharing) to define 'adventure tourism' and to explain its **patterns** in New Zealand - include types of activities (adventure vs extreme adventure), patterns in age groups, and tourists' origin to explain the **global sphere of influence** of NZ.\n\n**b) Movie location tourism**[**Use these sources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/13LVFy86arQsyk68mtwJMdeVr2j_0Rfbd?usp=sharing) to complete the 5Ws plus H on movie location tourism in New Zealand.\nHow have \"Lord of the Rings\" and \"The Hobbit\" benefited New Zealand tourism?\n\n**2. Your inquiry into a 'heritage' tourism site** \nPick a known heritage site of your choice (for example Hue, Angkor Wat, Halong Bay or Hoi An).\na) Define [**Heritage tourism**](http://culturalheritagetourism.org/what-is-heritage-tourism/).  \nb) Outline the attractions and geographical features of the World Heritage Site.\nc) Compile a SEE(P) analysis of the costs and benefits of heritage tourism on the site, to the locals and the surrounding area.\n\n**Plenary:** \nHow might regulations protect 'heritage' sites that are under pressure from visitor numbers?\n\n**NEXT** - [**to find out how transnational companies (TNCs) have a significant influence on international tourism**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-role-of-tncs-in-tourism.html)[.](https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-role-of-tncs-in-tourism.html)\n"},"politicalcultural-influences-on-sport.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/politicalcultural-influences-on-sport.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Political/Cultural influences on sport\"\nparent: managing-tourism-and-sport-for-the-future\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/politicalcultural-influences-on-sport.html\n---\n\n**Part 1 starter**: Discussion: What does the video suggest is at the heart of Kenyan long-distance dominance?\n\n{{youtube DWnsIvmUPDo}}\n\n**Case Study - Focus on Kenya.** \na) [**Quick research task**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1TvpT7awBDOeQuIVOJbzdRBqNiv6U3ee841HervyMuWY/edit?usp=sharing) \nb) Make a copy of this [**google doc**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13dZTUWbMq8L-H0LmHlaCD-TFoXa-XHyIaVvWTOWOkDw/edit?usp=sharing) so that you are able to collect your thoughts. Add your ideas from the starter video. \nc) [**Explore this**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1GSOUsI6vZ-PhO3uIVFuQAaRdXYUp63J0fePiK8rW0bU/edit?usp=sharing) and add ideas to your table. \nd) [**Read the two articles and watch ONE video.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1bX-UoP-gfG6oeof2lLxANBGaWvGG8h9H?usp=sharing)\n\n![Picture](assets/screenshot-2026-03-27-at-5-14-30-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Part 2:** In groups, you will investigate some of these key issues around participation in sport:\n1. **Cultural:** Barriers to participation as a result of gender, race, and religion and initiatives to break down these barriers.\nUse the resources [**in this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Zd2lRf5e2FXWJusdMKHN1xSO7yzb0gib?usp=sharing) to investigate. \n2. **Political:** Sources of corruption (in international sports bodies like FIFA, plus doping and match fixing) and how to tackle the problems. Use the resources [**in this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1RBrTC9SYR9psVOrg9RPtK_hz3i5gsbIQ?usp=sharing) to investigate.\n\nExtra light relief: Watch [**\"Bend it like Beckham\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Ywlz4sw6bQlPl_pdTft7G-oQSV-hEN8b/view?usp=sharing) at home!\n\n**Plenary:** Add these terms to your glossary.\n\nInclusion\nEthnicity\nLegacy\n"},"sphere-of-influence.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/sphere-of-influence.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Sphere of Influence\"\nparent: changing-leisure-patterns\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/sphere-of-influence.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** What happens in each zone/ area of the Burgess model?\n\n{{youtube MRP2WQZT80o}}\n\n**Extension:**[**How is this model different?**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O9bcFIxJuLA)\n\nMake a copy of [**the class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1HoFt1R_lIht81TG7bDUJ6LXiOqhka_G3Nf7xrC55lLU/edit?usp=sharing) (and add to your own folder) and complete the following:\n\nMake a copy of [**this activity sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1nXuXo8Visjik0IifkeAKUGnGGZTePJrX4Eb9jb4QMeM/edit?usp=sharing)**.**[a) Classify the leisure facilities according to the size of the settlement.](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1HoFt1R_lIht81TG7bDUJ6LXiOqhka_G3Nf7xrC55lLU/edit?usp=sharing) \nb) As you walk from the CBD outwards, what would you expect to find and why? \nc) Give at least three reasons why it is important for city planners to ensure that there are sufficient open spaces for urban residents.\nd) **Independently** draw an annotated sketch map of the leisure facilities in and around HCMC (see [**this example**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WlywXETat9e2pwShHONqtQJMi3KHbO2W/view?usp=sharing) of one based on Bangkok). **Tip:** Start by sketching the river!\n\n**Plenary:** \nDefine \"range\" in the context of sporting activities. \nExplain how and why the sphere of influence of different sporting facilities varies, quoting contrasting examples of sports.\nExplain the leisure facilities likely to be found in the centre of a city with those likely to be found on the edge of the city.\n\n**Ongoing homework for the unit:** Add all new or unfamiliar terms to your ongoing LST glossary. \n[**Support**](http://greenfieldgeography.wikispaces.com/The+leisure+hierarchy+and+intra-urban+patterns)\n"},"stakeholders-in-food-and-health.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/stakeholders-in-food-and-health.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Stakeholders in food and health\"\nparent: food-and-health\norder: 70\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/stakeholders-in-food-and-health.html\n---\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2019-11-18-at-12-48-51-pm.png)\n\n**1. The role of international organizations, governments and NGOs in food and health**\nIn groups, make a copy of [**this summary table**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/19P0o5updLu3iOqsXUN_ZcSjekuGI7cHE4R32TtEw1Kc/edit?usp=sharing) to record the roles of each organization, and a brief evaluation of the benefits and limitations of each.\n\n**2. The role of gender in food and health**\na) Study the infographic below. Use the information to write a paragraph on the importance of women's empowerment as a means of achieving food security.\n\n![Picture](assets/infographic-gender-roles-and-food.png)\n\n{{youtube WIvmE4_KMNw}}\n\nb) Use the diagram above to explain the causes of food insecurity faced by women in some places.\n\nc) The [**role of women's empowerment**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ydVRp2lfvOllA07N1ajiYlpzYftvsbWH/view?usp=sharing)in improving health.\nd) Prepare a long response on the following question:\n**“To what extent is women’s role in food production and food security affected by their lack of power as stakeholders?”** \\[10 marks\\]  \nTo answer this question, use [**this folder of resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1N3sYhBF-ysrcVixN5cFms6l2TznuzmSg?usp=sharing) (and sources of your own). Use the resources on Rwanda as a counter-argument for how empowerment of women can lead to significant improvements in food production and security.\n\n**3. The role of TNCs in shaping food consumption habits** \na) Revisit your learning on [**land and food**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/land--food.html) in the Global Resource Consumption unit. What type of tactics have been employed by TNCs in Brazil to encourage a move away from healthier traditional foods? Make the connection with the idea of a 'nutritional transition'.\n**Read**: [**Trade and Food Security**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xU-sZyO_YuDIdyY4fJ1y6WlYV7mym1Vs/view?usp=sharing) \nb) Why are the \"big food\" multinational companies (or TNCs) and globalization synonymous with ultra-processed food, energy dense convenience food and drink? \nc) How and why have rising incomes, urbanization and increased female labor force participation in LICs and MICs led to changes in eating habits? \nd) Outline the role of supermarkets in the modernization of food systems.\n\n**Plenary:** Q&A\n[**Recommended viewing - The Corporation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/19fM4wFvzFbWe4Xza4BG2u5hwMnLp37qY/view?usp=sharing)\n"},"the-english-football-league.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/the-english-football-league.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"The English Football League\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-local-and-national-scale\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-english-football-league.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Where everyone plays?\n\n{{youtube 8_XBxcIoZy0}}\n\n**A National Sports League:** [**English football**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1q9amk9wWQ1Ml-tVp7ZeJTscnkjw6GphsrogleVycPmU/edit?usp=sharing)\n\nDefine new key words - league system, sports division, relegation and promotion. \na) **Analysis of the league's hierarchy:**  [**Explain the location of teams.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bUpXRdQo_FEdawpPueM9wqr66OzvMb4GUnM2EjLGQCo/edit?usp=sharing) \nIs there any relationship between the size of the host city and the position of the club in the Premier League hierarchy?\nWhat are the reasons why London dominates as a location for Premier League clubs?\nOn average, what population size of a city is necessary to have a club in the Premier League?\nTo what extent does stadium capacity influence the league hierarchy?\nAre there any anomalies?  [**Is there a link between how far supporters are prepared to travel (the size of the sphere of influence) to attend a match and where teams are in the hierarchy of the Premier League? Are there any anomalies that stand out?**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1xKRm48SpmZBswM6FTO-8Th716ugdHt6W5fQ6k8U3MnE/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Extra**: Visit [**this interactive site**](https://interactive.twitter.com/premierleague/#?mode=team&teamId=all) that shows via Twitter data how the support/sphere of influence of some clubs is truly global.\n\nb) What OTHER factors now influence a club’s position in the English football league hierarchy? How do the top clubs keep their position at the top of the ‘hierarchy’?\nIn pairs:[**Use all the articles in this link that explore factors like:**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1qTWqYOqoYUZjQE_UrQbx3c78PlQEMAxt?usp=sharing)  \nSponsorship & shirt deals\nTV broadcasting rights and global TV audiences\nChanges in club ownership/FDI\nPre-season tours\nThe origin of star players **As usual, ensure that you include some detail - statistics etc - that are memorable to you!**\n\n**Plenary:** \nExplain why and how sphere of influence of different sporting facilities varies. \nBriefly explain the term \"range\" and \"threshold population\" in the context of sporting activities and facilities.\n\n**Homework**: Exam Style Question: Plan this extended response. Use the template (1 page max) and structure found in [**resources.**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/extended-response-writing.html)\n\n\"For **one named** national sports league, examine the relationship between the location of its teams and the places where supporters live\". (10 marks)\n"},"the-incidencespread-of-diseases.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/the-incidencespread-of-diseases.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"The incidence/spread of diseases\"\nparent: food-and-health\norder: 50\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-incidencespread-of-diseases.html\n---\n\n**The diffusion of diseases**\na) Download [**this activity sheet**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1kyfO6e4UAzLPcyI7VcyBwczpUyHdXAi7/view?usp=sharing) (as a Word doc) to work out the different types of diffusion involved with diseases. \nb) [**Vector-borne**](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/vector-borne-diseases) vs water-borne: What is the difference between the two?\n\n**Focus on malaria - a HW investigation**\n\nMalaria is a life-threatening disease caused by the parasite transmitted by the bite of the female Anopheles mosquito.   \na) Using the map, describe the pattern of malaria (malarial belt) and the likely climate.\n\n![Picture](assets/malaria-medication-travel-map_orig.png)\n\n**Use** [**this folder of resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/19inxGKptCs7DNYNW5WcSHSTPp47HnoSA?usp=sharing) **to take detailed notes on:**\n\n1. The natural and human **processes** responsible for the **diffusion** and pattern of **incidence** of malaria, including **barriers** to diffusion. .\n\n2. The **geographic impacts** of the disease - economic impacts, demographic impacts.\n\n3. Challenges of **preventing** malaria. What are the **barriers** to diffusion?\n**Extra:** [**A game**](https://www.y8.com/games/little_mosquito)**!**\n\n{{youtube 2OIqzAwu9Vk}}\n\n**Focus on cholera**\nCholera is a disease of the small intestine caused by the bacterium Vibrio Cholerae. It is a water-borne disease although it can also be transmitted by contaminated food. For cholera outbreaks to occur, there must be two conditions: Significant breaches of water sanitation leading to contamination by the bacterium Vibrio Cholerae. Cholera must be present in the population.\n\n**Starter:** Causes, consequences and solutions (as SEEP): \n[**The same video in Vietnamese**](https://youtu.be/3LdFxFOeFLI)[.](https://youtu.be/3LdFxFOeFLI)\n\n{{youtube jG1VNSCsP5Q}}\n\n**The \"F-diagram\"** shows pathways of fecal–oral disease transmission. The vertical blue lines show barriers: [toilets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toilet), [safe water](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_supply), [hygiene](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hygiene) and [hand-washing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handwashing).\n\na) Why is the diagram above called the \"**F diagram**\"?\n\nb) Explain what the diagram means by a \"**barrier**\".\n\nc) Briefly explain how to [**prevent cholera and how to treat it.**](http://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/cholera)\n\n**Summary: Focus on geographic factors that affect the rate of diffusion**\nWhat are the main types of **disease diffusion** associated with each disease? \nWhat are the important **human and physical geographic factors** (including barriers) in the **rate of diffusion** for each disease?\n"},"the-role-of-tncs-in-tourism.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/the-role-of-tncs-in-tourism.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"The role of TNCs in tourism\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-international-scale\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-role-of-tncs-in-tourism.html\n---\n\n**Starter**[:](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1G0M-GNwEBGX4VafGg3eqSWS_Lfa9nMWEvYidN79onw8/edit?usp=sharing) What evidence/clues from the video tell you that the Hilton Group is a transnational corporation (TNC)? Use [**this presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1G0M-GNwEBGX4VafGg3eqSWS_Lfa9nMWEvYidN79onw8/edit?usp=sharing) to help you.\n\n{{youtube Q3HZeUF5W5Y}}\n\n**The role of TNCs** - **U**[**sing this doc.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1fuNjtI-rl1iO4HODS6-5bYX_YeP6OXnsckYSHaATGbI/edit?usp=sharing) \na) Name three different **kinds** of TNC involved in tourism. \nb) Explain why TNCs can offer cheaper tourism opportunities.\nc) What is meant by: \nEconomies of scale?\nMultiplier effect? \nEconomic leakage? \n[**Stakeholder**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1A9SBKU2IuKaHbnFxRKI55TOgMbNO3-UomLWiM6PFO5s/edit?usp=sharing)? **Tip:** Add these key words to your glossary. \nd) What are some of the[**cost/ benefits for stakeholders**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1fuNjtI-rl1iO4HODS6-5bYX_YeP6OXnsckYSHaATGbI/edit?usp=sharing)(local residents, employees of tourist-related companies, tourists and people interested in development and environmental issues)? Sort these using SEEP. \ne) Distinguish between horizontal integration and vertical integration in the travel industry. Explain how TNCs are horizontally or vertically integrated, and the benefit of the links they have to other travel companies. Give real-life examples (you may need to do a quick search).\n\n**Exam-style questions:** \nDefine ‘economy (economies) of scale’ in the context of tourism. \\[2\\]\nOutline the difference between horizontal integration and vertical integration in the tourism industry. \\[2\\]\nExplain why TNCs can offer cheaper tourism opportunities. \\[4\\]\n"},"tourism-and-sport-at-the-international-scale.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/tourism-and-sport-at-the-international-scale.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Tourism and sport at the international scale\"\nparent: leisure-sport-and-tourism\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/tourism-and-sport-at-the-international-scale.html\n---\n\n**Use the links below:**\n\n[**Introducing tourism as a global industry - its supply and demand**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/introducing-tourism.html)[**Tourism as a national development strategy: Focus on Viet Nam**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/national-tourism-strategy-viet-nam.html)[**Niche tourism: Focus on New Zealand**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/niche-tourism---nz.html)[**The role of TNCs in expanding tourism destinations**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-role-of-tncs-in-tourism.html)[**Factors affecting the hosting of international sporting events: Focus on the London Olympics 2012**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/case-study-2012-london-olympics.html)\n"},"tourism-and-sport-at-the-local-and-national-scale.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/tourism-and-sport-at-the-local-and-national-scale.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Tourism and sport at the local and national scale\"\nparent: leisure-sport-and-tourism\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/tourism-and-sport-at-the-local-and-national-scale.html\n---\n\n**Use the links below:**\n\n[**An urban tourism hotspot: Focus on Vung Ta**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/vung-tau-a-tourist-hotspot.html)**u**[**A rural tourism hotspot: Focus on Ko Phi Phi, Thailand**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/ko-phi-phi-a-rural-hotspot.html)[**Case study of one national sports league: Focus on the English Football League**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/the-english-football-league.html)[**Large-scale festivals as temporary sites of leisure: Focus on Glastonbury festival, SW England**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/glastonbury.html)\n"},"uk-economic-development--leisure-activities.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/uk-economic-development--leisure-activities.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"UK: Economic development & leisure activities.\"\nparent: changing-leisure-patterns\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/uk-economic-development--leisure-activities.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** What does this video (from 2014) suggest about female participation in sport? Why might that be? \nExtra: [**2017**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCdrJS1ojD8)\n\n{{youtube ilFN1FXsQ8Y}}\n\n**Task 1: Adult participation in sports in the UK** \nUse [**this document**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13THs0I59ZUzlercsNez_MTvYt4aiapuz/view?usp=sharing) to: \na) Comment on sports participation by gender. \nb) Describe the pattern of sport and active recreation by age group.  \nc) Describe the pattern of sports participation by socio-economic status.\n\n**Read the case study material edited from the** [**Geography Course Companion.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1fgM7k1_un2sZZPDMDw9Y53hs0n9ILME-3uE0Okokay8/edit?usp=sharing)\nd) Explain three reasons why leisure time in HICs like the UK has increased. \ne) Identify the social characteristics of people who are excluded from participation in sport in the UK. How does this differ from [**Chin**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/china-economic-development--leisure-activities.html)**a**? \nf) How do the **type** of leisure activities differ from those in [**Chin**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/china-economic-development--leisure-activities.html)**a**? \ng) Which sports are most popular among both sexes?\nh) Why do you think men participate in a broader spectrum of sports? \ni) What are the most common reasons for people in HICs for not participating in sport? \nj) Comment on the popularity of sports by gender and age.\n\n**Plenary:** Watch [**this TED talk**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RjyxIsE3V2g) to add to your notes on changing leisure patterns in HICs (although bear in mind it is from 2012!)\n\n**Extension question:** Explain how gender influences leisure time in societies at different stages of development.\n"},"urban-environment-and-social-stresses.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/urban-environment-and-social-stresses.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Urban environment and social stresses\"\nparent: urban-environments\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/urban-environment-and-social-stresses.html\n---\n\n"},"urban-environments.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/urban-environments.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Urban Environments\"\nparent: geographical-themes\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/urban-environments.html\n---\n\n**This optional unit divides into the following sections:**\n\n[**Characteristics and patterns**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/characteristics-and-patterns.html)[**Factors affecting poverty in urban areas**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/factors-affecting-poverty.html)[**Urbanization and urban systems - centripetal and centrifugal movements**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/urbanization--urban-systems.html)[**Case studies and infrastructure growth and deindustrialization**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/case-study---infrastructure-growth--deindustrialization.html)[**Environmental stresses—microclimates, congestion, and air pollution**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/micro-climates-traffic--pollution.html)[**Social stresses - contested land use, managing the impacts of urban social deprivation**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/contested-land-use--managing-urban-deprivation.html)[**Urban growth and resilient city design**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/urban-growth--resilient-city-design.html)[**Environmental strategies to manage a city's ecological footprint**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/eco--smart-city-design.html)\n\n**HL students, make the connection between this unit and our investigation of** [**\"Londonistan\", the impact of cultural change in London**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/londonistan-cultural-changes-in-a-built-environment.html) **as part of Human Development & Diversity/Changing Identities & Cultures.**\n"},"urban-growth--resilient-city-design.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/urban-growth--resilient-city-design.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Urban growth & resilient city design\"\nparent: building-sustainable-urban-systems\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/urban-growth--resilient-city-design.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** What is the future of population growth? What are the patterns? Name the movers and shakers, both urban and nations. \nExplain the patterns using the video and your prior knowledge.\n\n{{youtube YJjz7LVVl8c}}\n\nListen to Robert Muggah (the guy behind Earth-Time) and make notes using SEEP.\n\n{{youtube Sv5QitqbxJw}}\n\n[**Explore the issues**](https://earthtime.org/stories/urban_fragility) facing our cities due climatic and geopolitical problems (fragility). \nStudy the map and describe the pattern of fragile cities (some cities are more fragile than others, as represented by the larger, darker circles). A city's fragility score is made of 11 variables, which range from city population growth, unemployment, and inequality to pollution, climate risks, homicide, and exposure to terrorism.  **Extension:** Explore the Earth-time Story and follow the slide show (look at, for example, sea level rise).\n\n**Resilient City Design—There** are lots of characteristics of cities that make them important, related to the high concentration of people and the type of economic activities they contain.\n**Resilient cities** are economically productive (so they need cheap, effective, integrated public transport networks), socially inclusive (which needs to allow for social mobility and lots of green space) and environmentally friendly (this might mean adapting to the risk of flooding).\n\nWith a partner, use this [**Resilience Report from the Financial Times**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1o0jL6GexHzHd4BGqdKcVtn7aja-ZjJtz/view?usp=sharing) to write up **two detailed examples** to illustrate possible strategies to manage escalating climatic and geopolitical risks to urban areas\n\n**Plenary:** Explore this [**infographic**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FutASo2JqjHnaxgcDjPmsf4Q5pniDiOm/view?usp=sharing) to see the impact of climate change on cities & how cities create efforts to mitigate.\n\n**Go to the next page:**[**Environmental strategies to manage a city's ecological footprint**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/eco--smart-city-design.html)\n"},"urbanization--urban-systems.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/urbanization--urban-systems.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Urbanization & urban systems\"\nparent: changing-urban-systems\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/urbanization--urban-systems.html\n---\n\nUrbanization can be defined as the process whereby populations move **from rural to urban areas**, enabling cities and towns to grow. It also refers to the **increase in the number of people living in urban areas** in comparison to rural areas. It can be measured at the global, regional and national **scales**. Urbanization in many industrializing countries is driven by centripetal population movement. Centripetal population movement involves the migration of people from rural areas to urban. Geographers often explain this migration in terms of push and pull factors. Push factors are negative conditions in rural areas that drive people to migrate away. Pull factors are positive conditions in urban areas that attract people.\n\n**Starter:** Discuss the ideas from the video.\n\n{{youtube fKnAJCSGSdk}}\n\n[**Introductory presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/11rOOx3Joid7LAN9u5W1WHu_Py98Jdk-s1brr3WeVQ0k/edit?usp=sharing) **on movements in urban environments**\n\n**Centripetal Movement: rural to urban migration in China** \nUse the resources in [**this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1aljt3F3nOKAyNYC6XxOriytZYLvG5kIN?usp=sharing) **to find out:** \nThe role of natural increase (give specific reasons regarding place)\nRural-urban migration push factors\nRural-urban migration pull factors\nThe government's plans to increase urbanization and its rationale\nThe changing role of the Hukou system\nThe winners and losers of gentrification in China.\nMake connections with the [**economic development**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/china.html) page from Unit 1.\n\n**Centripetal movements (inward movement) & centrifugal movements (outward movement)—in London**\nRead and make notes from these resources to highlight the trends of movement and their impact. Think about causality (causes and effects in terms of SEEP) and time scale.\n[**Timescale**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1u7COgcmUq_7-A21wIpGggBbeZ5iRk8qG3UJsf2gX7ks/edit?usp=sharing) [**Counter-urbanization**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/15oWPekd5SyIi8_hIbRYjogfYrg-4xY3p/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) **in London**[**Trouble in Metroland**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1SYu-Esh0bYxQQUN63LZQ9jwVl-4w8wjB/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true)**: the impact of re-urbanization in London**[**Gentrification in Brixton, South London**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQjrOqZ1iJ4-spTXEt8RJaKWxdoH_TZC/view?usp=sharing)**: its impacts (4-minute video)**\n\n**Plenary:**[**Match the key terms to the right definitions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1dT122p_3qzrft5kqEdT9i0aUHfG1r8Zw4KQGo66r7TE/edit?usp=sharing) for all the terminology you have learned during this investigation.\n\n**Go to the next page:**[**Case studies and infrastructure growth and deindustrialization**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/case-study---infrastructure-growth--deindustrialization.html)\n"},"vung-tau-a-tourist-hotspot.md":{"path":"content/4-geographical-themes/vung-tau-a-tourist-hotspot.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Vung Tau: a tourist 'hotspot'\"\nparent: tourism-and-sport-at-the-local-and-national-scale\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/vung-tau-a-tourist-hotspot.html\n---\n\n**Welcome to Vung Tau!** \nSkip through the video below to get a feel for Vung Tau and the types of **primary and secondary touristic resources** there.\n\n{{youtube Fmp5Ke-A0mY}}\n\n**Focusing on Vung Tau:** Research with a partner and complete this case study of an urban tourism hotspot. \na) The original factors that led Vung Tau to become a tourist hotspot.\n                                       i) Think primary tourist resources. \n                                       ii) Can you find a map from the past and present to compare development of the hotspot? \nb) The more recent factors that continue to make it a tourist hotspot today.\n                                       i) Think secondary tourist resources.\n                                       ii) What data can you find out about numbers of visitors?\nc) Think about how Vung Tau benefits the local community (SEEP).\nd) Think about the negative consequences of excessive tourism (SEEP).\n\n[**Articles for you to read**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1prt6LztSxAGbIsmTqnZ2F5-SNiACLV6P?usp=sharing)to learn about the attractions and impact of tourism in Vung Tau BEFORE we go there!\n\n![Picture](assets/butler-model.jpg)\n\n**Becoming familiar with Butler's model of tourism development**\n\nStart by reading through [**this slide presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1P--aSWg-b4KCQw-iLaV0D0UjpLRaEyR-xixDhis_PMg/edit?usp=sharing) and [**this document**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xqAO3Eu_N-p_vabftWPKdkI_qZ71_NcG/view?usp=sharing) (differentiated version [**here**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rq-PRRgHnQsIoByI3kIhQIfhWxWVPAKh/view?usp=sharing)) to familiarise yourself with Butler's model.\n\nDraw the Butler's model onto paper and summarise the stages of the model in your own words. Think about the link between Butler's stages and the different kinds of **carrying capacity**.\n\nExtension: [**Slide Share**](https://www.slideshare.net/geographyalltheway/ib-geography-globalization-tourism-resort-life-cycle-model)\n\n**Plenary:** What sort of things do you need to test or collect to identify the stages a place might be on Butler's model?\n\n[**Link to Internal Assessment Support page**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/internal-assessment.html)\n"}},"5-hl-only-global-interactions":{"aid-loans-debt-relief.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/aid-loans-debt-relief.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Aid, Loans, Debt Relief\"\nparent: global-networks-and-flows\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/aid-loans-debt-relief.html\n---\n\n[**Introductory presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/16NYbd9NvcEjAasiaQo3JlaipTbZ5ATohBN8tOVj_WYI/edit?usp=sharing) on aid, loans and debt relief.\n\n{{youtube 0tCPl0veqvc}}\n\n**Starter:**  Approaches to aid for poorer countries essentially follow two approaches to development: 'top-down' and a 'bottom-up', grassroots approach. \nDefine [**top-down & bottom-up**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hl_VYhtRCNZWOztozo8GQ-D4qQqBwrst6p6M7Xr70OI/edit?usp=sharing) & add the ideas/ sentences into the right columns.\n\n**Heavily Indebted Poor Countries (HIPC) & Debt Relief**\nSkim-read [**this IMF**](https://www.imf.org/en/About/Factsheets/Sheets/2016/08/01/16/11/Debt-Relief-Under-the-Heavily-Indebted-Poor-Countries-Initiative) **web page** on the HIPC to answer:\na) How many countries benefit from debt relief from this initiative today, and in which parts of the world (see table at the bottom)?\nb) What criteria do these countries need to meet to receive debt relief?\nc) In what ways does debt relief help these countries?\n\nDo you think the HIPC initiative to cancel the debt of the world's poorest countries is right?\nSome people disagree. Countries receive debt relief and are able to use the money saved to improve living conditions, for example, by building schools. However, only the poorest of the poor countries benefit from the HIPC.\n\n[**Update on the debt situation, October 2024**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1E5cvRJBM5SOzugJxqOlaDfRF-M4AT5um/view?usp=sharing). Use this summary of two articles to explain why debt servicing (repayment) is actually increasing for the poorest countries and what the consequences of this are.\n\n**You would think that these financial flows (aid, loans, debt relief) from the core to the periphery would make a huge difference. In fact, more money (capital) flows in the opposite direction.** \nRead [**this one-page article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1uKOLnj48pJXuLtUGk8znLsGea5zs4HGe/view?usp=sharing)to find out why (updated for 2025).\n\n{{youtube xbqA6o8_WC0}}\n"},"changing-identities-and-cultures.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/changing-identities-and-cultures.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Changing identities and cultures\"\nparent: human-development--diversity\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/changing-identities-and-cultures.html\n---\n\nThe second part of this HL unit focuses on 'changing identities and cultures'.\n\n**Click on the links below for the activities/resources:**\n\n[**Diffusion of culture**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/diffusion-of-culture.html) **- how global interactions affect cultural diversity, the concept of 'cultural imperialism'**[**Glocalisation**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/glocalization.html) **- how globalised commodities are hybridised/adapted**[**Global diaspora**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-diaspora.html) **- how diasporas influence cultural diversity and identity**[**\"Londonistan\"**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/londonistan-cultural-changes-in-a-built-environment.html) **- a case study of how global interactions change the cultural landscape of a built environment**\n"},"civil-society-action.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/civil-society-action.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Civil society action\"\nparent: local-and-global-resilience\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/civil-society-action.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** International Civil Society Organizations often play an important role in finding solutions to environmental and social risks associated with global interactions. \nRead[**this World Economic Forum article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Girk8U_rotoyRtJbZFXiGxt17VnFgpSV/view?usp=sharing) to summarise why civil societies are so important in the world today.\n\nRemember this video? What was Greenpeace (an environmental civil society) campaigning about?\nComment on the effectiveness of [these posters](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1RF9BtPs-gsf-mZy861fIVYD496XkiLgQ?usp=sharing) which accompanied the TV ad.\n\n{{youtube qhbliUq0_r4}}\n\n**Detailed example - Civil Societies and the environment: Greenpeace.** \nExplore the following [**resources.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1AIAg716vj03TbQ5deYV7Iz3c-LEbnDP2?usp=sharing)\nOutline the issue and attempted solutions. \nEvaluate the campaign (for good and bad).  **Extension:** Using the following site of the [**Greenpeace Arctic Campaign**](http://www.savethearctic.org/en) identify the ways Greenpeace campaign to protect the Arctic. Evaluate the solutions it proposes and comment on the challenges the campaign faces.\n\n**Detailed example - Civil Societies and the society: ActionAid.** \nExplore the following[**resources.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1IOyOfWlh9TWTQX6e6inZZAKiYGyaeZrv?usp=sharing)\nOutline the issue and attempted solutions. \nEvaluate the campaign.\n"},"correlation-between-globalization-and-nationalism.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/correlation-between-globalization-and-nationalism.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Correlation between globalization and nationalism\"\nparent: geopolitical--economic-risks\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/correlation-between-globalization-and-nationalism.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Watch this video and label this [**\"elephant\" chart**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1uhwykHiUWOP8_VBDJGRQAbSjsYoikl0U/view?usp=sharing)**.** Tip: Be sure to use annotations, arrows, examples and place names.\n\n{{youtube bjh-vt6xtfo}}\n\n**Tribalization** - \"The rise of \"us and them\" political movements, which are often opposed to globalization or Westernization\".\n**Populism** - \"The idea that every political decision in a democracy should reflect what the majority of citizens believe, not what the majority of the politicians believe\".\n\n**1. Nationalism & Populism- What and why?** \nIn pairs - think , pair, share: the ideas of [**nationalism**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1iwbdmzixpjeGiYa5P5XiaJTMPmHwplSB/view?usp=sharing) and [**populism**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1u1IlAYVz53XwFD36ONfKup8MsNF87GgZ/view?usp=sharing). \nRead this article, [**\"The end of globalisation: Tribalization\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rbDXH8_p3ZaW0OoSGT-6LebQs-adsLDQ/view?usp=sharing) (Medium.com Sept 2015) - including a refresher on [**the KOF index**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/globalizationsuperpowers.html)!\nWatch [**this 3 minute clip**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1f5MYGLelrbuxqCXi08Fa8sN6v8qilPM1/view?usp=sharing) from \"The Social Dilemma\". How is social media contributing to the rise of tribalism?\n\n**2. What examples might illustrate geopolitical tension/ conflict?****USA versus China - The Trade War:** [**Folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1B_BB_RFmtvhoVb3jBMzAtRx46Xr36yZf?usp=sharing) Explain how globalization and free trade have contributed to escalating tensions between the USA and China.\n**Make the link between this issue and**[**what you learned about superpowers**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/globalizationsuperpowers.html)**in the 'Power, Places and Networks' unit.**\n\n**Brexit, the EU and Globalization: Link back to** [**your inquiry into Brexit**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/political-factors-multi-governmental-organisations.html) **as part of the 'Power, Places and Networks' unit.**\n\n**Extension:** Exam Style Q:Examine the extent to which globalization is responsible for increasing nationalism. (16 marks)\n\n**Plenary:** \nIn  your own words, briefly explain why globalization has led to increased nationalism.  **OR****4Ps -** Think about (and make notes) how anti-globalization movements may have developed in different places as a reaction against the same two particular processes: 1) the spread of global culture and 2) the persistence of global inequality.\n"},"development-opportunities.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/development-opportunities.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Development opportunities\"\nparent: human-development--diversity\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/development-opportunities.html\n---\n\nThe first part of this HL unit focuses on 'development opportunities'.\n\n**Click on the links below for the activities/resources:**\n\n[The multi-dimensional process of development](https://geogjon.weebly.com/multidimensional-process-of-human-development.html) - the SDGs, the 'development cable' model\n[Empowerment and affirmative action](https://geogjon.weebly.com/empowerment--affirmative-action.html) - empowering women and indigenous groups to close the development gap\n[Social entrepreneurship](https://geogjon.weebly.com/social-entrepreneurship-fair-trade--csr.html) - corporate social responsibility/fair trade/micro-finance\n"},"diffusion-of-culture.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/diffusion-of-culture.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Diffusion of culture\"\nparent: changing-identities-and-cultures\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/diffusion-of-culture.html\n---\n\n![Picture](assets/giphy-1-orig.gif)\n\n**Global culture** is a shared sense of belonging at a worldwide scale demonstrated through common ways of communicating, consuming media and food, dressing, or behaving, including shared social norms such as a commitment to upholding human rights.\n\n[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1Yknrx-mDA3k76qls5Cvs1AdS9IZ8jFKZjVR4E-cvT1E/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce **cultural diffusion** and **cultural imperialism**.\n\n**Starter:** Read through [**these different scenarios**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ynaxGgzvyygiQHuPGk6_dmGYpi9HtQDc/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true):\nWhat aspects of culture do they highlight?\nBased on these scenarios, to what extent is cultural diffusion a positive process?\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2020-01-03-at-3-25-20-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Examples of cultural diffusion**\nRead/take notes from [**these articles**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1AKxXrlwD-_3IA-N9lXBg2sQtNebU4uoI?usp=sharing), each one with an example of cultural diffusion today, including a mini-case study of a media TNC, News Corp.\nFor each, think about:\n**How** is culture being diffused?\nWho holds the power in the process of cultural diffusion?\nTo what extent is it widening or narrowing (\"homogenising\") cultural diversity as a result? Explain … \nHow do receiving cultures respond?\n\n**Further investigation at home:****1. Cultural diffusion, imperialism and the loss of languages**\nAs another example, the variations in POWER of different languages leads to cultural imperialism in another way and at different SCALES. Read [**this document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sOUabhNwXSl20tydjcKY92wTsqhwRsQrdTLMh7pqYEs/edit?usp=sharing) to find out more, and listen to [**this CNN audio interactive**](https://edition.cnn.com/interactive/2021/04/us/losing-languages/) about disappearing languages.\n\n**2. The varying responses to cultural imperialism**\nRead [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UB9KNxusA-5O6YXM3i71dEsn_PX2srf7/view?usp=sharing) about the global reach of American culture.\nAccording to the article, what factors lead to the **adoption** or **resistance** of American **cultural imperialism**?\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-11-30-at-3-02-30-pm_orig.png)\n\n**3.** [**Cultural Imperialism and 'McDonaldization'**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jE0N51ERi8mQwD8rirx3PbAheJ2MHVeh/view?usp=sharing) **Questions to frame your notes from this article:**\n1. What is \"communicated\" by products and companies (in the cultural common market) like McDonald's, Netflix, Nike, and Starbucks? Explain. \n2. In your own words, define 'McDonaldization'.\n3. As claimed in the article, if McDonald's isn't just selling fast food, what are they selling? \n4. How have some nation-states **resisted** this form of cultural imperialism? Include an example.\n\n![Picture](assets/tenor_orig.gif)\n\n[**Link to the next page**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/glocalization.html) on Glocalisation with more on McDonald's.\n"},"economic-migration--migration-control.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/economic-migration--migration-control.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Economic Migration & Migration Control\"\nparent: human--physical-influences\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/economic-migration--migration-control.html\n---\n\n**Economic migration is a complex issue. The time scales, push and pull factors, volume of flow, and legalities of these movements all vary. As a 'financial flow,' remittances from migrants are very significant.** \n1. Read this one-page article, [**\"Migrants' billions put aid in the shade\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ESzh-mNJA_dAAEARAV7FxMwQPxRyWihi/view?usp=sharing) to take some notes on the importance of remittances.\n2. Watch the short video below and access this presentation for an overview of the importance of remittances.\n\n{{youtube f66WjetTjdg}}\n\n**Resistance to global interactions:****The rise of anti-immigration movements**\n\n**Starter activity:**\nMake a list of reasons for the recent widespread rise in anti-immigration movements.\nUse your graphic organiser on the loss of sovereignty/rise of nationalism to help you.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-01-06-at-6-00-17-pm.png)\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-01-06-at-5-58-44-pm_orig.png)\n\nWith a partner, discuss the pattern displayed on this graph, read [**this short article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1WPfl8r1SYWC9YmKs0BqBKT60-B4yi2N6/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) about \"the immigration backlash\" in Europe (US News, August 2023) and watch [this](https://drive.google.com/file/d/17KdgpWUnBQf4vbs_Psey6X4bqeg1AlJU/view?usp=sharing) [**three-minute video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/17KdgpWUnBQf4vbs_Psey6X4bqeg1AlJU/view?usp=sharing) (2024). Why is anti-immigration feeling on the rise?\n\nUse [**this article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vJ2fTu-yBWHMc2alSTeVCubqI2_DkyJo/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) to explain how anti-immigration movements around the world represent **local resistance to global interactions**.\n\n**Migration control****Starter:** For an overview, read and take notes from [this article, \"the globalisation of migration control\"](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1NJzQyz4LgvDg2ec-v6BgT4NL2ekcHX34XJYBx7ReZqI/edit?usp=sharing) in terms of issues on a regional and global scale.\n\n**For you to evaluate ways of controlling migration today, take your pick between the USA and the UK (post-Brexit).**\n\n**The UK**\nTake[**this test**](https://lifeintheuktests.co.uk/life-in-the-uk-test/?test=15)**,** a requirement for migrants who want to stay in the UK (as well as an English language test). Would they let you in?\n\n**From January 2021, Brexit meant that the UK gave up the EU's Free Movement Directive that governs labour mobility. Instead, it introduced a 'points-based' migration control system, similar to the policies used in the USA and Australia.**\n\n1. Use [**this BBC Newsnight video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1RqK5ysahbgo20d9e7LPEt30Pp_fd7fts/view?usp=sharing)**,**[**this ITV News interactive page**](https://www.itv.com/news/2020-02-19/government-under-fire-over-changes-to-immigration-rules) (2020) and [**this Guardian article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1mJivovo_A5Cp1j102EfPz1NuJtA0QNWD/view?usp=sharing) (2023) to explain:\na) How does the UK's 'points-based' system of migration control work?\nb) What is the impact on EU citizens who live and work in the UK?\nc) What are its advantages and disadvantages?\n\n2. More light relief from [**Charlie Brooker on EU migration control**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/16JrSc8JSTxIlfMd0IddhkY97dSFpn3ki/view?usp=sharing).\n\n**The USA**\nWatch the second half of the video below, which focuses on the issues surrounding migration control in the USA. Respond to the following questions as you do:\na) What is the pattern of migration? Are there any core areas people are moving to or away from?\nb) What have been the main changes over time?\nc) Suggest reasons why a nation-state like the USA would want to limit immigration.\n\n{{youtube 4XQXiCLzyAw}}\n\nIn the past, US economic prosperity was associated with the country's ability to attract labour - both skilled and unskilled. Due to their ageing population, the immigrant population used to account for more than 50% of the growth in the labour market. Today, many people in the US, including the current president, want strict immigration control.\n\n**Using your own sources:** \nDefine illegalimmigration.\nFind out about the punishment for illegal migrants including fines, imprisonment and deportation. \nWhat are the current levels of immigration - both illegal and legal in the US? What are the origins of most migrants in the US? \nFind out what the US define as a \"visa overstayer\". \nWhat are the main migrant jobs compared to American passport holders? Make sure your data is as up-to-date as possible.\n"},"empowerment--affirmative-action.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/empowerment--affirmative-action.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Empowerment & affirmative action\"\nparent: development-opportunities\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/empowerment--affirmative-action.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** What do they \"really really want?\"[**Source and background**](https://www.vox.com/2016/7/5/12099730/spice-girls-wannabe-girls-feminist-whatireallyreallywant)**.**\n\n{{youtube sZQ2RUFd54o}}\n\nDisparities exist between people within countries -  in wealth, education and political freedom. This affects women, indigenous people and minority groups disproportionately.\n\n**Empowerment and Affirmative Action:**[**What do these terms mean?**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/17xtkOh6ZguNdwpXYPWrMG9GYQMib6sSlL5YqvUawOjs/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Task 1: How are women affected by disparities?****a)** Which SDG focuses on women's empowerment and why? Read [**this**](https://www.unwomen.org/en/news/in-focus/women-and-the-sdgs/sdg-5-gender-equality) to find out. \n**b)** Why is women's empowerment essential for sustainable development?\nRead [**this**](https://www.iied.org/why-womens-empowerment-essential-for-sustainable-development), then summarise the three main reasons in bullet points, using these 'topic sentences': \ni) Gender is a moral imperative.\nii) Women are key managers of natural resources and agents of change.\niii) In most societies, women's unpaid work is not properly valued. \n**c)** How does [**this infographic**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1F4Kmxyl8nWJUAeHR1-PfhuWeRgYJ1Z14/view?usp=sharing) suggest that these issues are even more important since COVID-19? \n**d)**[**The latest data for the gender inequality index**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_xQ5YnWsTU3u1HSbUr-6NC8FQ9JYhvdG/view?usp=sharing) - what patterns do you see between countries of different levels of development (high-income vs middle-income vs low-income)? Are there any anomalies?\n\n**Task 2: Affirmative action in support of women**\nUse your notes on [**Rwanda's gender equality policies**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/gender-equality.html) as your example of affirmative action for women.\n[**Textbook extract**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_3JTFHKFqeetPq_gr7QIflC2tGHAaG0B/view?usp=sharing) on affirmative action to support women's right to education (focus on Malala/Pakistan)\nRead [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZtmWLFwypIAVQWma_IBFYxFYvABBKrtf/view?usp=sharing) as an extra on policies in Viet Nam (September 2020).\n\n**Task 3. Focus on the marginalization of indigenous groups, and affirmative action to support them**\na) [**Introductory presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1CxWltR2ye91UyaeYllgjNA4gy0-pS5BMMTBcOosHZ2k/edit?usp=sharing)\nb) Read [**this textbook extract**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1RkfEQ-I2qIg6_ckpa3pJiGIR_boKLwBr/view?usp=sharing) on indigenous land rights and Survival International's short report, [\"](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZR5vqGlUUnQrKM8mrBJFnv35GC1K0XQq/view?usp=sharing)[**Progress can kill\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZR5vqGlUUnQrKM8mrBJFnv35GC1K0XQq/view?usp=sharing). Focus on one of the indigenous groups to explain how/why land rights are so important. Take notes on your chosen example. \nc) [**Watch this video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Jz3IXztWJuGu-8FkHfdZXeYuiRClW2kB/view?usp=sharing) and write a short summary on how community radio is a medium for affirmative action/community empowerment for indigenous groups in Guatemala.\n\n**Plenary**: **Affirmative action - what processes lead to change?** \nConsider these four **processes**. Can you think of a recent example that illustrates each? [**See these images for some clues**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1hQ3pJqY2jCH7NEgnUeP32la3H0KfpP4I/view?usp=sharing).\nInterconnectivity through social media. \nKey influencers (individuals) that turn local action into global action. \nKey events that trigger change. \nThe action of powerful governments that have international influence.\n"},"environmental-risks.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/environmental-risks.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Environmental risks\"\nparent: global-risks-and-resilience\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/environmental-risks.html\n---\n\n**This theme of \"Global Risks & Resilience\" is subdivided into:**\n\n[Global trade and carbon footprints - global shift of industry/global agriculture](https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-tradecarbon-footprints.html)[Transboundary pollution: The causes, consequences and possible solutions](https://geogjon.weebly.com/transboundary-pollution.html) - focus on the Great Pacific Garbage Patch\n"},"fdi--tncs.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/fdi--tncs.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"FDI & TNCs\"\nparent: global-networks-and-flows\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/fdi--tncs.html\n---\n\n\"FDI is the investment by a company into the structures, equipment, or organisations of a foreign country. It does not include investment in shares of companies of other countries\". \nRead through this [**presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1rds0i2T7IpYS9zUrZYMEyu7lXYRJeoC87FvWLoQAPdw/edit?usp=sharing)**.** Take notes on:\nThe definitions of key terms \nThe characteristics of FDI\nThe economic power of TNCs\nWhy TNCs choose to outsource\n**Activity:** TNCs: \"Companies that operate in several countries are called multinational corporations (MNCs) or transnational corporations (TNCs)\". Yet their operations affect places, depending on whether they are in the CORE, SEMI-PERIPHERY or PERIPHERY of the global economy.\n\nAs an overview, think during [**this \"tuning in\" activity**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1fOedNqVLDLQ_wvSb6AAXTaA2fd1dQkx2h2CMRcgms1k/edit?usp=sharing) to categorise the different impacts of TNCs in different places.\n\n**Two detailed examples of TNCs - their global strategies** \nAs a general rule, a \"detailed example\" should take you 30 minutes to complete.\nMake a copy of [**this graphic organiser**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1TRpCk_76SJ_Su5b-du9q6PxNfGlmO5ICHla9EVKWLsg/edit?usp=sharing) to record your notes on Tata and Apple as contrasting examples of TNCs today: \n**The Tata Group**\nRead [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1GWK3nbkUOnFKck_iv_8OTs0gVwtmCPM4/view?usp=sharing) on The Tata Group.\n**Apple**\nFollow the instructions in [this presentation](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1HGleI3tundAWRZjcaCXTtAlw35p5-b_nanYWIUDxw3w/edit?usp=sharing) to investigate Apple Inc. \nCompare/contrast the different strategies of Apple and the TATA Group. Of the two, which do you think is the most successful? Why?\n\n![Picture](assets/screenshot-2025-10-12-at-6-02-09-pm.png)\n\n**(a BIG overlap with** [**Geopolitical & Economic Risks**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/geopolitical--economic-risks.html)**)**\n\n**1. How do global supply chains work?**\nStart with [**this**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1M6EkzWvXB9rCDAuK3V4M7KVvi-2AtzrB/view?usp=sharing) **short video** that explains it.\nBe able to define \"global supply chain,\" \"outsourcing,\" \"offshoring,\" and \"nearshoring.\" Refer back to [**the class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1rds0i2T7IpYS9zUrZYMEyu7lXYRJeoC87FvWLoQAPdw/edit?usp=sharing) if you need to. \n**2. Why have some places (like parts of Vietnam) become international outsourcing hubs?**\nUsing [**this article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mvlED9fOJYk7f3dxZTzbJhkxboa0RbHK8YMYIToJIK8/edit?usp=sharing), build a detailed mind map to explain why TNCs choose Vietnam. Quote memorable facts & figures!\n**3. What different types of risk do global supply chains face today, and what are their impacts on different places?**\nRead this article, [**\"Are global supply chains still a source of strength?\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rjwJwWQKzrsGIHuDzYdF8SewUfUhd_wy/view?usp=sharing) and answer these questions **in detail:** \na) Describe the different kinds of risk to supply chains, including at least one example for each. \nb) Explain the latest threats to supply chains, again quoting examples. \nc) Discuss the impacts of these from both optimistic and pessimistic perspectives.\n\nExtra, but worth it ... a well-explained [**BBC News report (four minutes)**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KXlW6UYDrN0hnMT7z52_Ff45OhXZEfNL/view?usp=sharing) on the risk to supply chains during the pandemic, 2021.\n\n**Illegal Flows**\n\nThis inquiry focuses on some of the most important (and profitable!) flows of goods that are illegal - counterfeit goods, narcotics (drug trafficking), and the illegal wildlife trade. Please make your own copy of [**this activity sheet**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DMt1gOGsNCkNxmSvZaJeTRCKHg1Jd1IV/view?usp=sharing) and follow the instructions.\n\n![Picture](assets/screenshot-2025-10-25-at-1-53-25-pm.png)\n\n**Plenary:**\nHow do global power and influence vary spatially? How do different places become interconnected by global interactions?\n"},"geopolitical--economic-risks.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/geopolitical--economic-risks.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Geopolitical & economic risks\"\nparent: global-risks-and-resilience\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/geopolitical--economic-risks.html\n---\n\nThis part of the unit is taught in Grade 12. It sub-divides into three parts:\n\n[Threats to individuals and businesses](https://geogjon.weebly.com/threats-to-individuals-and-business.html) - hacking, surveillance, disruptive global supply chains \n[New and emerging threats](https://geogjon.weebly.com/new--emerging-threats.html) - tax avoidance, disruptive technological innovations \n[Renewed nationalism/tribalism in response to globalisation](https://geogjon.weebly.com/correlation-between-globalization-and-nationalism.html) - examples of economic and geopolitical tension today\n"},"geopolitical-constraints-on-gi.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/geopolitical-constraints-on-gi.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Geopolitical Constraints on GI\"\nparent: local-responses-to-global-interactions\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/geopolitical-constraints-on-gi.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Pick out 2 \"ideas\" from the video. Make connections with aspects of the Geo syllabus. Be prepared to share.\n\n{{youtube 9cVJr3eQfXc}}\n\n**1.** [**Government and militia controls on personal freedoms**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1B0ZjlIKEQtag3VP8OOmXZu-9JfRQ2mPGfTXAPqnhHRU/edit?usp=sharing)\n\nRead - How and [why do](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1W9yVXI9hNdQByGj29n0uJfl_AREvSS-h/view?usp=sharing) [**governments control of information online**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1W9yVXI9hNdQByGj29n0uJfl_AREvSS-h/view?usp=sharing)? Other than China, which governments are involved and why? \n[**Zoom in**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1GW-DNq1ri0aBb4m8fsz1NqT4tYJWf7A6/view?usp=sharing) and explore which places censor your internet activity. \n[**Make notes**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/15b-k1wR8tJ-A7RW6Io4kGfxoiaNIRdMXccVAq_OTF1I/edit?usp=sharing) on the role of militia groups in controlling personal freedoms.\n\n**Extension:** This [**YouTube**](https://youtu.be/j3LZP_ZZWjY) or a longer \"What is [**Chinafile?**](http://www.chinafile.com/document-9-chinafile-translation)**\"**\n\n**2. National Trade Restrictions:** \"When state government erect barriers to foreign trade and investment such as import taxes. The aim is to protect their own industries from competition\".\n\nRead and make notes - [**Protectionism.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1s4a2nF_zVJZ6b0t_adSxeM1vLnWX2szsk3hbei-F0wo/edit?usp=sharing)\nWhy are **Brexit** and **Trumpism** both examples of protectionism? \nUsing Fig 1: State 2 examples in which protectionism has increased. State 2 examples of countries where this has decreased.\n\n![Picture](assets/changes-in-protectionist-policies-of-oecd-countries-2009-16_orig.png)\n\nUsing figure 2: Which regions have the greater number of protectionist policies? Identify 2 economic sectors with the most protectionist policies.\n\n![Picture](assets/protectionism-by-economic-sector_orig.png)\n\nWhy have some of the world's biggest economies imposed restrictive trade policies since 2008?\n\n**3.** [**Resource Nationalism**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vnzhBZ0uNzSNtkw4HT0Npxt-o37nmBuQBR7frTNvqyI/edit?usp=sharing): \"When state governments restrict exports to other countries in order to give their own domestic industries and consumers priority access to national resources found within their borders\".\n\nBriefly explain the pros and cons of resource nationalism for countries. \nExplain why **Debswana** is considered to be an ideal mining venture.\n\n[**Extension reading**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/14qx9_zWsfK2qf75CnYWsolK2Ohyg7LXK/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary:** Q&A \n[**More reading**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1O8imv2ila1YfCEbnv5YM-YVGiayuxGSa?usp=sharing)\n"},"global-diaspora.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/global-diaspora.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global diaspora\"\nparent: changing-identities-and-cultures\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-diaspora.html\n---\n\nDiaspora = a scattered population with a common origin that has managed to retain its cultural traits and traditions, and sometimes maintained strong political ties with its origin. Cultural traits are preserved in a modified form.\n\nRead through [**this introductory presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1Yrn2XI-CqxF1B1GqkJH1gAowbx3Xkj20p9jYjFgXOS4/edit?usp=sharing) on the concept of diaspora.\n\nThere are approximately 40 million people of Chinese origin living in 20 countries. **Describe** the pattern of the Indian and Chinese diaspora on the map below. The numbers represent populations in millions. Can you **explain** the pattern?\n\n![Picture](assets/chinese-diaspora_orig.gif)\n\n**Read** [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1qi97sSOKUQOSjLXg99tipXYO_nVSdsQv/view?usp=sharing) **about the causes and impacts of diaspora growth, using the examples of both the Indian and the Chinese diaspora.** \nUse these questions to guide your detailed understanding of the concept of diaspora:\n\n**1.** Define diaspora and explain how it operates on both spatial and temporal scales. \n**2.** Use the case study of the Indian diaspora, explain its economic, cultural, and physical impacts on both the country of origin and destination countries. As an extension, watch this four-minute video, [**\"why is India's diaspora so powerful?\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1zZ2-xs1nITf0i7H1aBW4uLz9JbL9BLHV/view?usp=sharing) and add the key takeaways to your case study of India. \n**3.** As a contrast, explain the impact of diaspora on China, including reference to how the 'shrinking world' is significant.\n"},"global-interactions-and-global-power.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/global-interactions-and-global-power.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global interactions and global power\"\nparent: power-places-and-networks\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-interactions-and-global-power.html\n---\n\nThe first part of this HL unit focuses on 'Global Interactions and Global Power'.\n\n**Click on the links below for the activities/resources:**[Powerful organisations](https://geogjon.weebly.com/powerful-organizations.html) - evaluating international organisations and global groups\n[Global Superpowers](https://geogjon.weebly.com/globalizationsuperpowers.html)- measuring globalisation using indices/what makes a superpower\n"},"global-networks-and-flows.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/global-networks-and-flows.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global Networks and flows\"\nparent: power-places-and-networks\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-networks-and-flows.html\n---\n\nThe third and final part of this HL unit focuses on 'global networks and flows'.\n\n**Click on the links below for the activities/resources:**\n\n[**The role of TNCs**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/fdi--tncs.html) **- FDI, outsourcing, and illegal flows of goods and people**[**Global Trade**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-trade.html) **- changes in international trade in manufactured goods and services**[**Aid, loans & debt relief**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/aid-loans-debt-relief.html) **- relative movements of money via aid, loans, debt relief and remittances by migrants**\n"},"global-risks-and-resilience.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/global-risks-and-resilience.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global risks and resilience\"\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-risks-and-resilience.html\n---\n\n**This HL unit is split into three parts:**\n\n[Environmental risks](https://geogjon.weebly.com/environmental-risks.html)[Geopolitical and economic risks](https://geogjon.weebly.com/geopolitical--economic-risks.html)[Local and global resilience](https://geogjon.weebly.com/local-and-global-resilience.html)\n"},"global-trade.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/global-trade.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global Trade\"\nparent: global-networks-and-flows\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-trade.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** \nWatch [**this video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1N22aEA_3Sb-qx4_8J3i6mu3nEV3CzGDF/view?usp=sharing) (2.5 minutes) that records the TRENDS in changes in exports worldwide since 1970.\n\n![Picture](assets/screenshot-2025-10-23-at-5-30-23-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Main activity:**[**Summarise the main issues surrounding global trade**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1TiWBPMGGmPGxoSbJAClvi7kvp3tyIum2_LcuJUpGERA/edit?usp=sharing)[,](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1TiWBPMGGmPGxoSbJAClvi7kvp3tyIum2_LcuJUpGERA/edit?usp=sharing) including the US-China trade war, the subsequent \"Liberation Day\" (April 2, 2025), and the USA's move away from free trade.\nThink back to what you learned previously on [**the changing balance of power between the world's superpowers**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/globalizationsuperpowers.html).\n\n![Picture](assets/trade-wars_orig.png)\n\n![Picture](assets/trump.png)\n\n**Trump and \"Liberation Day,\" April 2, 2025: What are the implications for global trade?**[**The latest tariffs by country**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1xwIl7WUXocym4Ur2pL7fmb0j3kKZAUP3/view?usp=sharing)(August 2025)\n\n**Quick reads:**[**The story of Nike and the potential impact of Trump's tariffs on the company**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/167s7UaHFa9awHLIENIMHD3GrTzNLB5RD/view?usp=sharing)**—respond** to the questions at the end!\n[**Trump announces trade pact with Vietnam**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1S-Bf1rIpB8TMZmvAtT7Ww_5LH_f3ypC6/view?usp=sharing) (Reuters, July 2025)\n\n![Picture](assets/screenshot-2025-10-23-at-6-23-21-pm_orig.png)\n\n**EXTENSION**\nIt's now old, but [**this BBC documentary**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/12PdV6eQudjQM0APTT1wmAbjKLohnnH0_/view?usp=sharing) really helps you to understand how global trade is connected and the relationship between China and the traditional 'Core' countries. Save it!\n\n**NEXT PAGE -** [**FDI and TNC CASE STUDIES**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/fdi--tncs.html)\n"},"global-tradecarbon-footprints.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/global-tradecarbon-footprints.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Global trade/carbon footprints\"\nparent: environmental-risks\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-tradecarbon-footprints.html\n---\n\n[**Introductory presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1daB93syrOO2tJS1o3P88puOs3Nv8FWVN0G2HehzLI80/edit?usp=sharing) \n1. ‘The global shift’: Why do transnational companies decide to relocate?\nRead through these \"[**reasons for relocating**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OeLOzscHaBF2NIw1_dzHFXmrugF-zgRRN7uZLVErSvw/edit?usp=sharing)\" cards. Categorize them according to the types of reasons why transnational companies decide to relocate to countries like China and Vietnam. \nWhat are the 'global interactions' involved?\n\n**How can pollution be moved offshore? And what are the consequences on human health and the environment?**  \nRead [**this article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1JqGavj157NX3xwsJH511D_BWHhCY8Taw0tWb51qm19o/edit?usp=sharing) from The Conversation and watch [**this short news clip**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WD5nRvfJrXSiGhXD556ddyBLdPadwqcn/view?usp=sharing) to respond to these questions. \n**Extension article:** [**CO2 emissions are being 'outsourced' by rich countries to rising economies**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1kM4brLLy7vL1ZAXjRXhFCkdN3SmU3_MORlihJlnj5Y4/edit?usp=sharing) - What are the implications of this in global negotiations over greenhouse gas emissions at COP 26?\n\n![Picture](assets/the-global-shift-of-industry_orig.png)\n\n**2. The carbon footprint of global trade** \na) Starter: Look at read through [**this interactive**](https://www.businessinsider.com/interactive-map-world-global-trade-flows-2017-1?amp). What does it tell you about the global flows of goods/commodities today? \nCan you identify core areas versus the periphery (and semi-periphery)?\n\nb) Rearrange the [**environmental impacts**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1B5t4JK0fqnil6b3GXh-Q8tCLDe-RnJoRi6dPrEd_VyE/edit?usp=sharing) to match the correct example of global flows.\n\n**Carbon footprint** - \"the amount of carbon dioxide used by an individual, organization or country as they go about their everyday lives and operations. It is usually measured in terms of the volume of carbon dioxide emitted into the atmosphere as a result of fossil fuel use per unit of time or per activity\". \nc) Read [**this page**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/15WT5dAiAChlMjIcQS7tyBBDckZYtJjr9/view?usp=sharing) from the Oakes textbook. Take some notes on how carbon footprints can be measured at different **scales**. \nd) Study this[**image**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ekiIjMlWmeGOOeMu_Rpi8BVa9BF4DQk9/view?usp=sharing)**.** Describe the pattern of carbon emissions based on sectors.\n\ne) What do these two graphics below tell you about the **patterns** and **trends** in carbon footprints as a result of global trade? **Describe** the **patterns** on the map, including any anomalies, and the **trends** on the graph. Can you e**xplain** them?\n\n![Picture](assets/co2-emissions-in-trade-map.png)\n\n![Picture](assets/c02-emissions-in-trade-graph.png)\n\nf) Read [**\"The Carbon Footprint of Global Trade\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1IVusL4KFW4zxtNABQL984jI8vCH0XEm_/view?usp=sharing) about the issues behind the impact of global trade on the carbon footprints of different places at different scales to add to your ideas above.\n\n![Picture](assets/bell-curve-of-carbon-footprints-worldwide_orig.png)\n\n**3. The global shift in food production systems - how, why, and so what?**\n\n{{youtube hJe4By2yurA}}\n\nStart with **this s**[**lide presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/18llamKwp-af0YYTsF47O_WlYoH7YIHOFDMmKctAM6rc/edit?usp=sharing)\nWith a partner, investigate these environmental risks caused by modern food production using the resources in [**this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Ss3XIL3bx6DMo3_j354GDuTCzEInky19?usp=sharing)**:** \nFossil fuel dependence\nGreenhouse gas emissions\nOver-use of freshwater\nPollution of freshwater (including eutrophication/algal blooms)\nUse of agrochemicals (pesticides and fertilizers)\nDeforestation/land degradation\nLoss of biodiversity\n\nExtra on the TNC/Agribusiness, Monsanto: [**a half century of scandals**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1LgdY_EQO3vCw0DCbRvsQ8tH5OZCNbYWvYhVbt1GPu9I/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Link what you have learned on this page with what you learned about** [**the environmental impact of shipping and cruise ships**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/our-shrinking-world.html) **at the end of Grade 11 ...**\n\n**Plenary:** \nShould a place/society that consumes goods take responsibility for the carbon emissions or the place/society that produces them? \nIs the relocation of polluting industries the main cause of pollution in Asia? \nWhat is meant by \"food miles\"?\n"},"globalizationsuperpowers.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/globalizationsuperpowers.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Globalization/Superpowers\"\nparent: global-interactions-and-global-power\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/globalizationsuperpowers.html\n---\n\n{{youtube yV-jAjjrv14}}\n\n**Superpowers**\n\n**What makes a 'nation-state' a \"superpower\"?** \nVideo starter, [**Top 10 countries by GDP up to 2022**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LCSVXo8ZUiA) - [how has the wealth of countries changed in the last 50 years?](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1E24roroyqpauIu482mIz5BYyGHZBp-x0/view?usp=sharing)[**Use the images/infographics in this class presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/15vXSl1PLUllcP0QpIKpXAWSsfQIDOupk-O7MyzaIYTo/edit?usp=sharing) to understand the different types of power (hard vs soft) and to explain the criteria for becoming a superpower.\n\n**The USA as a superpower****Build a detailed example of why the USA remains a superpower today using these resources and these guiding questions to organise your notes:**\n\n**1. Briefly explain how/why the USA has become a superpower since WWII.** **2. Describe the characteristics of the USA's superpower status with details on the different types of power (hard vs soft).** **3. Evaluate the arguments for and against the USA's superpower status today.** \nUse [**this 5-minute video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XZ6VDBmdLKBST77ky3dlILbnICEyoA3f/view?usp=sharing) (Vox 2016) for the historical context.\n[**The USA as a superpower**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1-rJ7za1cqa8mmK6yGwZpKds6HXGiXy1B/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) (Geography Review presentation written by the Geo Chief Examiner)\n[**America’s undying empire: Why the decline of US power has been greatly exaggerated**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1b3JC4KXu51WQ92jK_LC__HwpCADPceTu/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) (Guardian Nov 2023)\n[**Shifting soft power: The USA vs. China**](https://www.theguardian.com/world/ng-interactive/2026/feb/13/these-charts-show-how-trump-is-isolating-the-us-on-the-world-stage?fbclid=IwY2xjawQjrnRleHRuA2FlbQIxMABicmlkETF0clJ5YjZuZTFaMnkwOXpSc3J0YwZhcHBfaWQQMjIyMDM5MTc4ODIwMDg5MgABHpf1PVYOrXF8btscGL753fhc03EX_IGO8LQQFkAjBPZl3k5MYbCaCzKoUNtu_aem_MseaeMg4zWXWSFa81LS6GQ) interactive webpage (Guardian Feb 2026)\n\n**China: The next superpower?****What evidence is there of China's potential as a superpower? In what ways in terms of types of power?****Use these resources (and your own!) to present the evidence, for and against:**[**China & the World**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WHQDXTIbrrSfhK0VMu3Y5kICQwacQBCd/view?usp=sharing) (CBC 4 mins)\n[**Can China surpass the USA as the next world superpower?**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1K5bUB9RYftbCdAP2cvaiqPrExu4hvTVz/view?usp=sharing)(LSE blog, March 2023)\n[**China - a superpower's slump**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1NoxKdO2tTa_LqK2IlX8QOqxhZOqdYOux/view?usp=sharing) (The Week, September 2023)\n[**Top five reasons why China is a superpower**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5TASuCayo-Q&feature=youtu.be) (5 min video 2017)\n[**China is rehearsing for when it overtakes America**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1F1Vs-w2RfHUPRyoSQjcwZGOWM3VJqc5L/view?usp=sharing) (CNN January 2021)\n\n**Homework**\n\nWatch this 15 minute TED talk from April 2023: [**\"The next global superpower isn't who you think.\"**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uiUPD-z9DTg) \nWhat counterarguments to the focus on the USA and China as superpowers does the presenter suggest? \nPERFECT for an HL response! Take some notes.\n"},"glocalization.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/glocalization.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Glocalization\"\nparent: changing-identities-and-cultures\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/glocalization.html\n---\n\n[**Starter:**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/160D5FvxpSjnp6T814bBVxuNFbrOn4aRm/view?usp=sharing) Why does The Economist use the [**\"Big Mac index\"**](https://www.thebalance.com/what-is-the-big-mac-index-1978992) to measure globalisation? (**Hint:** Switzerland is top and India last)\n\n**Glocalisation of commodities: The example of McDonald's**\nComment on the [**franchise openings**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-dGr-mbh49mbzhRytO7FuT1xjRGN72fn/view?usp=sharing) of McDonald's to illustrate **adoption** of its commodities over time. Use specific place names! \nRead through [**this slideshow**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1VDKvV0R2e_ZvPQjL0xD0lszEbbQRz6xGKpYbhEbg0xg/edit?usp=sharing) to understand the meaning of **glocalisation**, and how the concept can be applied to McDonald's. \nWatch [**this short video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1h91um6oTkBu8mC7eX1nhAnVZFxWjYDFT/view?usp=sharing). Record some memorable examples of how McDonald's has **glocalised (adapted)** its products. \nRead [**this extract**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1X5iGSC_3palvj1kCYtBEECmW7_JaPxbk/view?usp=sharing) from the Simon Oakes textbook. Take notes!\n\n**Resistance of globalised commodities at a local scale: Examples from Saigon**\nRead/take notes on [**these three articles**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1G8sHfOQv4JZhYlSNxcrrDNe1FcOSK2_W?usp=sharing) (and the short video) related to **resistance** to fast food chains, including McDonald's. \nWhy might Vietnamese consumers respond differently to the way consumers respond in other societies? \nExtra (June 2026): [**South Korea temporarily shuts down all Starbucks outlets for a history lesson**](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jun/16/starbucks-korea-shut-all-stores-tank-day-promotion)\n\n**A case study in cultural hybridity:**[**Hip Hop**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1t0YKa1EbnAbfqt6CNzDuFnArtx8dJnfozSTtqqH5YL8/edit?usp=sharing) **and Saggin'**\n\nStart by defining the keywords and ideas in [**this presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1t0YKa1EbnAbfqt6CNzDuFnArtx8dJnfozSTtqqH5YL8/edit?usp=sharing)[.](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1t0YKa1EbnAbfqt6CNzDuFnArtx8dJnfozSTtqqH5YL8/edit?usp=sharing)\n\nWith a partner, read and take notes from an article each: \n[**Cultural hybridity**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1aBoBKKs7nmV3-qxEvd2fCPivPnNp4srj/view?usp=sharing) or cultural appropriation? Why did \"saggin''\" go mainstream, and why is making a comeback today? \n[**Glocalization**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1V9ZJktVTynmIjFwhIykyaBc_VV6aOGf8/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) of Hip Hop or a poor imitation? What is the message behind this article?\n"},"human--physical-influences.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/human--physical-influences.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Human & Physical Influences\"\nparent: power-places-and-networks\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/human--physical-influences.html\n---\n\nThe second part of this HL unit focuses on 'human and physical influences' on Global Interactions.\n\n**Click on the links below for the activities/resources:**\n\n[**'Our shrinking world' -**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/our-shrinking-world.html) **focus on changes in transport**[**Patterns and trends in communications infrastructure**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/patterns-and-trends-in-communication-and-infrastructure-use.html) **- increased data flows vs China's \"Great Firewall\"**[**The physical environment and Global Interactions**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/physical-environment-on-global-interactions.html) **- natural resource availability, the effect of physical isolation**[**Political factors - multi-government organisations**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/political-factors-multi-governmental-organisations.html) **- focus on the EU**[**Economic migration and migration control**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/economic-migration--migration-control.html)\n"},"human-development--diversity.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/human-development--diversity.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Human development & diversity\"\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/human-development--diversity.html\n---\n\n1. [Development opportunities](https://geogjon.weebly.com/development-opportunities.html)\n2. [Changing identities and cultures](https://geogjon.weebly.com/changing-identities-and-cultures.html)\n3. [Local responses to Global Interactions](https://geogjon.weebly.com/local-responses-to-global-interactions.html)\n"},"international-mindedness.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/international-mindedness.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"International Mindedness\"\nparent: local-responses-to-global-interactions\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/international-mindedness.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** How can Social Media be weaponized?\n\n{{youtube q1Hl9bRzwEs}}\n\nFor your glossaries...\n\nThe role of **civil societies** - \"A civil society is composed of all the civic and social organizations or movements that form the basis of a functionary society, and work in the area between the household, the private sector and the state to negotiate matters of public concern. Civic societies include non-governmental organizations (NGOs), community groups, trade unions, academic institutions and faith based organisations\".\n\n**International Mindedness** - \"A way of thinking that is receptive to ideas from different countries and recognizes that all people belong to a networked international community that is pluralistic, culturally diverse and meritocratic. It also involves an appreciation of the complexity of our world and our interactions with one another\".\n\n**Civil Societies: In pairs -** look at these organizations - [**PGA website**](https://www.nadir.org/nadir/initiativ/agp/en/) & [**Avaaz website**](https://www.avaaz.org/page/en/). \nState the mission of each,\nWhat issues the organization  is currently involved in\nInvestigate one or more campaigns that the organizations have been involved in.  \nRestricted Freedoms:\n**Detailed example 1**: [**Myanmar & Asia's new boat people.**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/16UeKmN-96yszDsY9iEKmMeBXf5uHSFDr?usp=sharing)  a) Who are the [**Rohingya?**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i3PG3lb8mRE)\nb) Read the article together and discuss [**Rohingyas' restricted freedom**](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-32740637)\nc) Watch the [**video**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=04axDDRVy_o)[.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=04axDDRVy_o) What has happened?\n\nd) Read the sources provided and carry out your own research about how freedoms have been restricted in Myanmar. Write your response below;\ne) Read the [**text**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1wF9jekeLBPnkDy7nVGL8EYx5Hf3MB_jtotJyXQNwIm8/edit) adapted from a report on the [**Law Library.**](http://www.loc.gov/law/foreign-news/article/burma-four-race-and-religion-protection-laws-adopted/)\nTo what extent do these laws attack the personal freedom of individuals and religious groups in Myanmar society?\n**Ext:** Improve your notes on the [**Rohingyas' restricted freedom**](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-32740637)**.**\n\n**Detailed example 2:**[**the Arab Spring & challenging restrictive freedoms**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1JFOaod-OOZqvHTTAtAA6wsJw85K4gbgh?usp=sharing) (folder). The Arab Spring was a series of pro-democracy uprisings within several largely Muslim countries, including Tunisia, Morocco, Syria, Libya, Egypt and Bahrain. The events in these nations generally began in the spring of 2011, which led to the name. The political and social impact of these popular uprisings remains significant today. \nSuggest how the Arab spring has led to greater freedom for some but not for others.\n\n1. Watch the [**video**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fgcd5ZcxDys)[.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fgcd5ZcxDys) What happened? Write a brief explanation. \n2. Watch the [**video**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EAuxNXmAbyY&feature=youtu.be) and answer the questions on how social media was used as the main driver for regime change in Tunisia.How was Twitter and Facebook leading the media reporting of Tunisian problems?\n3. What was the criticism with mainstream media?\n4. How did the group Anonymous get involved?\n5. What was the cyber war survival guide?\n6. How did the Tunisian government respond?\n7. How was Twitter being used to document protests?\n8. What was the regional impact of the Tunisian uprising?\n9. How did Egyptian activists organise themselves to bring down the government\n10. Read the articles in the folder above and make notes on restricted freedoms in the Middle East. \n11. How the [**Arab Spring**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lGGDfmhKoyk) changed Europe: Discuss\n12. Suggest how the Arab spring has led to greater freedom for some but not for others.\n\n**Extension:** Read more about \"weaponization\" according to the [**inventor of the world wide web, TimBL.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1yQ4P0Y7g1hC19tO9E52zuQ1w-0OEiCeW/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary:** Qs - \nExplain why the Rohingyas have become stateless people. \nHow can social media be \"weaponized\"? How can social media be used to bring about political change? Give examples. \nComment on how political changes in different places have brought about benefits for some but not for others.\n"},"introducing-global-interactions.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/introducing-global-interactions.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Introducing Global Interactions\"\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/introducing-global-interactions.html\n---\n\n**Globalisation Indices**[**The class presentation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZK1HQWvHb0aoG7qXT6J1Qs2pp2ALPs0C/view?usp=sharing) to introduce globalisation, global interactions, and the two globalisation indices.\n\n**The KOF Index**[**Activity**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ocsdXj7zlKZf4Yq280IdXSixi3vemnX18wCtgBY2bdg/edit?usp=sharing) - sort the KOF Index variables into economic, social and political forms of globalisation. \nVisit [**the interactive map on this webpage**](https://kof.ethz.ch/en/forecasts-and-indicators/indicators/kof-globalisation-index.html) of the KOF Index, 1970-2021. Can you **explain** the **trends** over time? \n[**Country rankings 2023**](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1WyznSMKJ3wAAddS87tknFogk0qyFXomu/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) - can you **explain** the **pattern**?\n\n**The DHL Global Connectedness Index**[**A short video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Wu6y0yJYhlCbF2twuj0uykoQhcr0Uxq9/view?usp=sharing) to explain the DHL Global Connectedness Index. \n[**Country rankings 2024**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/155FEA4gZZprC4mTEYEkrklc9gAbmuIDM/view?usp=sharing) - compare and contrast with the KOF 2023 ones. Can you explain any differences?\n\n**NEXT PAGE:**[**The role of powerful organisations and global groups**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/powerful-organizations.html)\n"},"local-and-global-resilience.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/local-and-global-resilience.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Local and global resilience\"\nparent: global-risks-and-resilience\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/local-and-global-resilience.html\n---\n\nThe final part of this unit focuses on:\n\n[Civil Society action](https://geogjon.weebly.com/civil-society-action.html) - focus on Greenpeace and Action Aid\n[Strategies to build resilience](https://geogjon.weebly.com/strategies-to-build-resilience.html) - \"re-shoring\" by TNCs, crowdsourcing, new technologies\n"},"local-responses-to-global-interactions.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/local-responses-to-global-interactions.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Local Responses to global interactions\"\nparent: human-development--diversity\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/local-responses-to-global-interactions.html\n---\n\nThe third and final part of this HL unit focuses on local responses to global interactions.\n\n**Click on the links below for the activities/resources:**\n\n[**Resistance to Global Interactions**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/resistance-to-gi.html) **- the rise of anti-migration movements, and rejection of globalised production**[**Political constraints on Global Interactions**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/geopolitical-constraints-on-gi.html) **- control over personal freedom, protectionism, resource nationalism**[**Civil societies and international mindedness**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/international-mindedness.html) **- challenging restricted freedoms, including the internet/social media**\n"},"londonistan-cultural-changes-in-a-built-environment.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/londonistan-cultural-changes-in-a-built-environment.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Londonistan: Cultural changes in a built environment\"\nparent: changing-identities-and-cultures\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/londonistan-cultural-changes-in-a-built-environment.html\n---\n\n**This has a strong crossover with your** [**Urban Environments unit**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/characteristics-and-patterns.html) **...**\n\n**Changing landscapes in the built environment**[**A presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1OuYKVBNWJerVlTR0rKU48U3FJvVNMIilSwkyIEZilS0/edit?usp=sharing) [to introduce cultural landscapes of cities today—homogenous or diverse?](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1OuYKVBNWJerVlTR0rKU48U3FJvVNMIilSwkyIEZilS0/edit?usp=sharing) \nRead/take notes from [**this article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1DxdDoA7srKsvKiOrzUdTxHT6dtpiQqbz/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) to explain the arguments why many of the world's cities are becoming culturally uniform. Use the example of Ho Chi Minh City here.\n\n**London: A \"melting pot\" of cultural diversity** **Melting Pot—A** cultural process that involves different communities mixing over time to form a more uniform culture which combines traits drawn from the traditions of each of the original communities.\n[**A presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/12TC05px4FEJnufdHRUvTdGOdPgJ38-gBwvyRBi4pyf8/edit?usp=sharing) to introduce London as a case study of cultural diversity. \nRead this [**o**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1y3VT1hcBxcCI8oc1zowKXwnMYsnYjtat/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true)**ne-page article**. Take notes to explain how the development of **ethnoscapes** within London has led to INCREASED cultural diversity at this scale, and why TIME is important.\n\n{{youtube mEB1C59hCvs}}\n\n**\"Londonistan\" and the 'ghettoization' of neighbourhoods?** **Ghetto: A** part of a city in which members of a minority group make up the majority of residents, typically as a result of social, legal, or economic pressure. The term may have been used originally in Venice to describe the part of the city in which Jews were restricted and segregated. \nWhat does [**Katie Hopkins**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1t-vowYdK7QAHLa47itRQ0J4ZaoSZ6t4r/view?usp=sharing) think? What do you make of her opinion? Bitter, or the truth?\n\n**Extension:**\nWatch this [**four-minute video on \"Multicultural London English\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/11DQhhnWHS9uJR9T_x9julKaYdIZuNlrn/view?usp=sharing) featuring Little Simz, Stormzy, and Emile Smith-Rowe. \nLike \"Singlish\", think about the **glocalisation** of language ... another quick example for you!\n\n**Next lesson, go back to our 'Glocalisation' page to explore:** [**A case study in cultural hybridity: Hip Hop and Saggin'**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/glocalization.html)\n"},"multidimensional-process-of-human-development.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/multidimensional-process-of-human-development.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Multidimensional process of human development\"\nparent: development-opportunities\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/multidimensional-process-of-human-development.html\n---\n\nDownload [**this document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1JB9-s-9i91_rpvu-R2CBqoHzgYICVlpB718S7Hv-8IA/edit?usp=sharing) to record your learning this lesson (or use it as a guide to create sections in your handwritten notes).\n\n**The 'Development Cable' model**\n\nThe model illustrates the multidimensional way human development can be measured ...\n\nThe core of the cable is the electric current of economic growth, technology and enterprise of development.\n\nThe outer layer represents the many different factors that provide the ‘power’ for development cable. They can also be the results of development, too.\n\n![Picture](assets/hdi.png)\n\n**The Human Development Index (HDI)** \nCompare and contrast [**these two maps**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1MJa81YCMzMAWGqAo_f4EFORbDOQPxv4BJh-t41N2Omw/edit?usp=sharing) - HDI versus GDP. Why might some countries with a high GDP have a lower HDI overall?.\n\n{{youtube 1e8xgF0JtVg}}\n\n**The Gender Inequality Index (GII)**\nThe GII measures gender inequalities in three aspects of human development.\nIn your own words and using the diagram below, explain the criteria used to calculate the GII.\n\n![Picture](assets/gii_orig.png)\n\n**Extension:** Check out the latest UN data on the HDI and Gender Inequality Index country rankings from [**this extract of the 2022 Human Development Report**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1yipvOr8CnFPIkDFfYzA4bjaJk2DydX2k/view?usp=sharing).\n\n**Homework follow-up: Considering alternative perspectives to how human development should be measured:****1.** Read [**this short article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13zmUNQZN72D0f4Fg0d2SzhQulH4NMzWk6uNRCF05NC4/edit?usp=sharing) that suggests human development should be measured in different ways today, not just via economic indicators. \n**2.** Watch [**this TED talk**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1e0Mm0BARouV2-FPUDkclL34oSloqA3pP/view?usp=sharing) by Kate Raworth, \"Why it's time for doughnut economics\". Summarise her main arguments as to why there are flaws in the way development is traditionally measured.\n\nGo to the next page, [**Affirmative Action**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/empowerment--affirmative-action.html)\n"},"new--emerging-threats.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/new--emerging-threats.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"New & emerging threats\"\nparent: geopolitical--economic-risks\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/new--emerging-threats.html\n---\n\n**Starter:**[**Complete the top and tails**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/11a3HrXZa7nTJBy3D1W5Auy8zHzafR6cDqR8x-orIw70/edit?usp=sharing) independently[.](https://docs.google.com/document/d/11a3HrXZa7nTJBy3D1W5Auy8zHzafR6cDqR8x-orIw70/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n[**A short read**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1uw0LnSrsk4V0_VFt7d4lTBOL4dKZRSQo/view?usp=sharing)to understand the scale of the issue in $**,** and then watch the CNN video below.\n\n{{youtube 7rwrAKOJFE4}}\n\n**TNCs and tax avoidance**\n\n**Using the map:**\na) Describe the variations in corporate tax around the EU. \nb) Comment on Apple's tax deal with Ireland. \nc) Explain **arms** [**length/ transfer pricing**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MuDSuX3M4Qg0O_izHHGaHOLZ0qSiwgNW/view?usp=sharing). How might this lead to TNCs paying less tax?\n\n![Picture](assets/ireland-tax-orig_orig.png)\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-10-09-at-1-34-09-pm.png)\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-10-09-at-1-35-30-pm_orig.png)\n\nLed by the OECD, 136 nation-states collectively agreed to impose a 15% tax on TNCs, and a greater share of their profits in October 2021. [**Read about it here**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BEFhp4t6pouzJlvBcnfSL-J6a7SdgP2C/view?usp=sharing).\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-10-11-at-4-36-01-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Tax avoidance by the wealthy individuals**[**Watch Rutger Bregman**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1uw0LnSrsk4V0_VFt7d4lTBOL4dKZRSQo/view?usp=sharing), a historian who questioned the amount of tax avoidance at the Davos summit, 2019. Summarise his arguments. \n[**\"The Paradise Papers\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WnocI53nFfowO4Nqvy6IJPkq4RShhvGi/view?usp=sharing)  - two-minute introduction to **the Panama Papers**, a major exposé of the scale of tax avoidance by individuals and companies, and the role of offshore tax havens.\n**Extension:** Watch BBC Panorama's [**\"The Pandora Papers\"**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/194jItE9kUQnwdVQa5dQNqRMVC__ypudX?usp=sharing), an update on this story in two parts (October 2021).\n\n**Disruptive tech:** 3D printers and drones. Explore the [**resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1yKo7eEWo2SKT2D4cCmrJm1DF2Z26wDEV?usp=sharing)**.**\nHow might drones improve your lifestyle? \nOutline the pros and cons of 3D printing & drone use.\n\n**Critical Thinking:** You have looked at the economic threats posed by these innovative pieces of tech. How might political sovereignty be threatened?\n\n**Homework:**\na) [**Watch**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UVlZqMb-QMJ7SFAjfuF2kDrdKw8JhXFy/view?usp=sharing) The town that took on the Taxman (an example of offshore tax avoidance scheme)\nb) Complete the section marked [**New and emerging threats to the political and economic sovereignty of states**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/17Td2u4I7Y2vnwtbcncI-eAsw83dO8pQQwQjYw6c42Ng/edit?usp=sharing)**.**\n"},"our-shrinking-world.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/our-shrinking-world.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Our shrinking world\"\nparent: human--physical-influences\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/our-shrinking-world.html\n---\n\n**Introductory class presentation**[**here**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1hkjUZqqIE-Bk8sGIG3my-nxXCKLJPDpl137f3aFAEi0/edit?usp=sharing)**Definitions:** in the context of travel and communication!\n[**Frictional effect of distance**](http://geographylaunchpad.weebly.com/the-friction-of-distance.html)/ distance decay. \n[**Time space convergence**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_OQrcGk5gbZl_tgH43wnikB_UA_tUvzF/view?usp=sharing)[**Transport Networks**](https://www.dictionary.com/)[**Links**](https://www.dictionary.com/)[**Transport Nodes**](https://www.dictionary.com/)[**Transport Hubs**](https://www.dictionary.com/)**1. The growth of container ships and its impact** \nUsing the resources in [**this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1_LUWTNBEP1jnQOYKy9YMpibK5qFVfOAc?usp=sharing): \nDiscuss the volume of container ship traffic seen from space. Name at least three traffic \"hot spots\". \nDescribe the evolution of the containers and ships used to move them. \nExplain the impact of containers on trade and the global economy.\n\n![Picture](assets/the-ever-given-suez_orig.jpeg)\n\n**What's the story behind this image - where, when, why?**\n\nWrite a caption for it to explain what it illustrates about the impact of the global flow of container ships.\n\n**2. The environmental impacts of shipping lanes and cruise ships**\nListen to [**this podcast**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l7YlPfN3ZE0fjCp24KipLiQ-h1UsMcTy/view?usp=sharing). Make notes on the environmental impacts of shipping under the headings of:\nLocal \nGlobal **How can the shipping industry be greener?** [**Read this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i5Xprj83UBiqR-6H9L67IHbuRBc3FDJr/view?usp=sharing) [and add to your notes.](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i5Xprj83UBiqR-6H9L67IHbuRBc3FDJr/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**The growing environmental impact of cruise ships** \nWatch the video below and read [**this one page summary from InThinking**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1c5w3pNDzSFBCEFX7EQsMnBUG0DamG9CN/view?usp=sharing) on the environmental impact of cruise ships. Make notes on the impacts of cruise ships at both the local and global scale.\n**Extension:** How [**cruise ships**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CwLmtqrc6C05N0ESQznJmt-aT4rAoXHZ/view?usp=sharing) can be harmful, focusing on Marseilles, southern France.\n\n{{youtube CNer4IvzhDA}}\n\n**3. The changes in air travel** \nUse this[**Planes interactive site**](https://www.pbslearningmedia.org/resource/aeroeng-sci-eng-100years/100-years-of-passenger-air-travel/)and this[**article.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jed8OepxueGqI6G7dxtaaGLNXDykYsu7/view?usp=sharing)\na) Explore the timeline and describe how air travel has developed over time. Don’t overdo it! \nii) Explore only four of the five “buttons” and make notes on each section:  \nPassenger experience (add a line in your notes for each “dot”), \nSafety innovations (read the innovations and write a couple of sentences),\nSocial impacts (more detail is needed here) and\nEnvironmental Issues (focus mainly on the section called “climate conscientious).\n\n**Plenary:** [**Quizlet**](https://quizlet.com/292220230/match)**Homework:** Watch[**Supersized Earth - innovations in travel**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_z5uqotH4-yfiyL2qt5nQmQTDzIPJZ08/view?usp=sharing)\n\n[**Further reading**](http://www.vision.org/visionmedia/reviews/global-issues-the-world-is-flat/167.aspx)on the concept of the \"shrinking world\"\n"},"patterns-and-trends-in-communication-and-infrastructure-use.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/patterns-and-trends-in-communication-and-infrastructure-use.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Patterns and trends in communication and infrastructure use\"\nparent: human--physical-influences\norder: 50\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/patterns-and-trends-in-communication-and-infrastructure-use.html\n---\n\n**Starter: Changing global data flow patterns -** What is data and what is its role/ importance today?\n\n{{youtube xhMMuKJzQnQ}}\n\n**Task 1:** [**The overall patterns and trends in data flows**](https://giphy.com/gifs/mgi-global-flows-mckinsey-l2JJp2n1Ck8dCzwsg?utm_source=iframe&utm_medium=embed&utm_campaign=Embeds&utm_term=http%3A%2F%2Fgeographillips.weebly.com%2Fpower-places--networks.html)**a)** Describe the change in the size and pattern of cross border data flows over time as shown in the link above.  \nAs an extra, check out [these](http://www.internetlivestats.com/) [**real-time statistics**](http://www.internetlivestats.com/) on internet use.\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-05-17-at-10-37-14-am.png)\n\n**b)** Categorise [**these impacts**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1QJJ9tq3sI2Kzdb9AugjimOiAqaqsGF9a6G7tzRwjB4k/edit?usp=sharing) on how data flows have influenced different kinds of globalisation.\n**c)**[**Read this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_pZPCWJnskK1P2Lc1l3G5-UwTj-NFs6F/view?usp=sharing): Describe, and suggest reasons for, changes in globalisation between the 20th and 21st century. \n**d)** How has the digital form of globalisation made it possible for even the smallest firms to compete with the largest TNCs?\n**e)** Explain what is meant by the phrase \"global digital platforms\" and suggest examples you are familiar with.\n\n**The digital divide and the changes - Focus on Kenya.** \nOutline the impact of the mobile phone (and now Smartphone) [**on Sub-Saharan Africa**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1P_SpSZWBpX8bf8nu6qeLphIXAjImZ-w3/view?usp=sharing), and specifically [**Kenya**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1mnjcpHnOrYwhs7Pk3tg58p-aNfTW4TPp/view?usp=sharing)**.** \nInclude ideas related to scale.\n\n**Plenary:** [**Bill Gates: How mobile phones can change the lives of the poor**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/19-K17U7fx6KMcsQc6yCTKgmNrU5oTFLm/view?usp=sharing) (video short)\n\n**Exceptions - China's Great Firewall/Myanmar's military coup**\nThe effect of [**China's firewall on innovation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1P35a-LrsxohYe-fQCw8AZhvkhh-uAft5/view?usp=sharing) (and read [**here too**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1VqorCde6HSU4QutjWHxnoTGNR1ZRCILL/view?usp=sharing)**).**\nMyanmar since the coup in Feb 2021 - How is the Myanmar military disrupting telecommunications/social media? Read about it[**here**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Utj7Phar_H07tCUHO4lVIoZ3L9xNaZiR?usp=sharing).\n"},"physical-environment-on-global-interactions.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/physical-environment-on-global-interactions.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Physical Environment on global interactions\"\nparent: human--physical-influences\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/physical-environment-on-global-interactions.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** [**Card Sort**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-227GPs3rb-KCvHgFQ6-OB0x88kTulL7v9gT7YPUIOw/edit?usp=sharing)**:** Read, sort, pair, group, categorize and stitch together. What's the story? Write a paragraph using as many of the words from the card sort as possible (tip: check today's learning objective for clues).\n\n**The power of oil as a resource**[**Changes in the top 10 oil producers 2000-2020 animation**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/19ittB1blZyfBuNUCDu4hcNoFTklNi6iQ/view?usp=sharing) - what trends do you notice?\nWhat are the options for countries with their own oil supplies? What is the future for oil-producing countries? \nRead [**this one-page BBC article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1w87fqmsvrlvP10-Fois-ZP0BsAwHRLtn4AKCCnUU3eE/edit?usp=sharing) (Jan 2021) to summarise the consequences of changing power from oil. \nAs an update, read about the [**\"great energy pivot\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1lc8Z2AvcJmX9jwN5ptZGj-zJU_YgiJp5/view?usp=sharing) as a result of the Iran war (The Guardian, April 2026).\n\n**Does resource availability necessarily lead to greater connectivity, power and possibility?** \nThe case of the Democratic Republic of Congo: [**Use these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1uu6j9VF7AbEUU5LEwWSI3CR8VsHXpuMY?usp=sharing) to draw your own conclusions.\n\n**Being landlocked and/or isolated as a place**\nWhat does [this](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1z-fMbaSYKDuQjYBwUkapKLENyI4chiLz/view?usp=sharing) [**video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1z-fMbaSYKDuQjYBwUkapKLENyI4chiLz/view?usp=sharing) suggest about the impact of the physical environment on global interactions?\n\n**Inquiry:** Each of you will be given a particular \"influence\" to research. Use the resources in the folders to begin with.\nResearch further information in order to [**complete your capture sheet**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HnNAe40iESuT9AyAuiyYbb1MB-H95k8ytNS4olnglTE/edit?usp=sharing)**.** \na) [**Landlocked countries (LLDC)**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1_NXHKWf6KWrxws7vugFnFlAoXuwfzpRJ?usp=sharing) \nb) [**Extremely isolated countries**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1GXmnwiDAHr4pChTeaJPTwe2AOln00rCE?usp=sharing)\n\nWhat does LLDC stand for? Which country in **our** region can be classified as a LLDC? \nOutline the potential disadvantages of being landlocked.\n\n**Plenary:** [**Pyramid.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1kcwcjxXdPEtGA3YNQeDgd8aHTDDw1yx3/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Homework**:\nPlease watch [**this in-depth documentary**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-dTsmiac-fTixqnmc72xamaYTKzfQ3ze/view?usp=sharing)**,** \"The Congo Connection\" (24 mins) about the Democratic Republic of Congo to add substance and further understanding to your class inquiry.\n"},"political-factors-multi-governmental-organisations.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/political-factors-multi-governmental-organisations.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Political Factors: Multi-governmental Organisations\"\nparent: human--physical-influences\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/political-factors-multi-governmental-organisations.html\n---\n\n[**A recap on the four Ps (and scale)**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1tjUOF_V7g3zbPgdLTOXTewNRrGbLOGrDF_Or7_HDkxg/edit?usp=sharing) - **keep all of these concepts in mind throughout the activities on this page!**\n\n**Part 1:****Introducing multi-governmental organizations** **A case study of the EU**\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-01-01-at-3-25-15-pm.png)\n\nIntroductory class presentation [**here**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/18O_WTyZyQVZcYkTuX9S_oFy4y2cYp7tOdN6-g90qx1g/edit?usp=sharing).\n\n[Answer the following questions using each hyperlink to help you:](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Ww3I8O_Ll4VOQibfh0rv17qDlc2Jw0XS/view?usp=sharing)[**How does the EU affect the flow of goods?**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1o43XADja57VGRhUGgl50g1WCIpewpo04q9ozjAqtcSA/edit?usp=sharing)[**How does the EU affect the flow of capital?**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/16uFJMzvivno58Phl6Tpsu_dX7BUKcP1POX--b2kBbU4/edit?usp=sharing)[**How does the EU affect the flow of labour?**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-CUmd8jLsMpWG_k4FhdgBjBA2tDLOr5S6v2LVrFL9N4/edit?usp=sharing)\n\nPlenary activity \n[**Rank your top three reasons for and against being an EU member.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FRPpzpAzvt4IeY44WTLN6kGl7Jcl0Dw1/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Homework**: As a link to our next lesson consider the issue of **sovereignty**.\nHow is sovereignty being lost/nation-state borders being diminished?\nDownload [**this table**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1JNGejw0rSCiZBgefJre4QMdh6MMwTpY7eZVm4lP-OPw/edit?usp=sharing). Complete it by adding the advantages and disadvantages of this **process**.\n\n{{youtube WDqayC1sR7g}}\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-01-01-at-2-58-57-pm_orig.png)\n\n**Part 2:****Globalisation versus Nationalism, Populism, and Tribalism? Focus on 'Brexit'** [**(overlap with Global Risks)**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/correlation-between-globalization-and-nationalism.html)\nOn January 1, 2021, the UK finally ended its membership of the EU following a referendum vote by the British people narrowly won by the 'Leave' campaign in June 2016.\n\nIntroductory class presentation [**here**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1H22IYlwXJ8qaLQOiC4XTMtEhbUy_hPZKZc8TMApQpXk/edit?usp=sharing). \n1. Watch these two short videos by two famous politicians in the UK: [**Nigel Farage, now leader of the Reform Party**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FJL3v7aJCdwew_d9ZjOXmHTO8WGdlclf/view?usp=sharing), and [**Tony Blair, former Prime Minister**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/18tOuOre_MDNBWshdkR4qD-PMeEzsvTko/view?usp=sharing). Summarise their contrasting viewpoints on the implications of Brexit. \n2. Read [**this article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1RihovrBkNJZyqh6u8j3MELDWRPHsgF4V/view?usp=sharing) and take notes on:\na) The definitions of nationalism, populism, and tribalism. \nb) The reasons behind the support for Brexit. \nc) The economic and social risks to European integration today. \n3. Watch [**this short video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/10184NAYCn3fDxxME_IYHLFvlpQg_iQo4/view?usp=sharing) (Financial Times) and add to your notes regarding the implications of Brexit for globalisation. \n**Extension**: A bit of light relief: [**Charlie Brooker**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1F7XQVJH6NM3UvFzAcxeRVZ4MkecOkCSl/view?usp=sharing) explains the build-up and aftermath to Brexit in 2016.\n\n**Plenary: Be able to define the following key terms:**\nMulti-governmental organization\nNationalism\nSovereignty\nTrading bloc\nA 'single market'\nThe 'Free Movement Directive'.\n\nGO TO THE NEXT PAGE, \"[**ECONOMIC MIGRATION AND MIGRATION CONTROL**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/economic-migration--migration-control.html)\"\n"},"power-places-and-networks.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/power-places-and-networks.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Power, places and networks\"\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/power-places-and-networks.html\n---\n\n**There are three sections in this unit:**\n\n1. [Global Interactions and global power](https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-interactions-and-global-power.html)\n2. [Human and physical influences](https://geogjon.weebly.com/human--physical-influences.html)\n3. [Global networks and flows](https://geogjon.weebly.com/global-networks-and-flows.html)\n"},"powerful-organizations.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/powerful-organizations.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Powerful Organizations\"\nparent: global-interactions-and-global-power\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/powerful-organizations.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** \"Gs all over the place!\" What is the purpose of these groups? Explain the variations of the Gs.\n\n{{youtube m54CvSWKiMI}}\n\n**Use or abuse of power?** [**Here**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/170K6RxJxzMFy4JaA2LnKxN9gMBYY3q29/view?usp=sharing)is the activity.\nStart with reading [**the profiles**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Lb7cfbZKPIoDUDOIA01PRntE08D5mI-LFDAqnkvxXyE/edit?usp=sharing) of each international organisation/global group.\nYou will need [**this graphic organiser**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1K7Q0bBGy8J6LmW7_DPv9bDtQe8--tlbyba3242Mc4XQ/edit?usp=sharing) and you can start your research by using the sources below:\n\n**1: G7/G8**[**Video**](https://youtu.be/sAKbNKdK-30)[**Article**](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2017/may/26/trump-set-to-clash-with-other-g7-leaders-over-refugees-and-climate)**2: The G20/OECD** [**Video**](https://youtu.be/1v5Qx1E-Vc4) [**Article**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/15Tdy3qSROaAJ4eqhRKYmTq1QiwrvamIC/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) [**Video**](https://youtu.be/yQJOWRYeKlQ) **3: OPEC** [**Video**](https://youtu.be/qGxOtalqpzk)[**Article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XiPm0lS9XarE-XqQxfRPJjKgNUr0YthT/view?usp=sharing)**4: World Bank** [**Video**](https://youtu.be/F59fF-xu-bY)[**Article**](https://www.theguardian.com/business/2017/dec/12/uk-banks-join-multinationals-pledge-come-clean-climate-change-risks-mark-carney)**5: IMF** [**Video**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bnagSbIX2E8)(Al Jazeera, 2023)\n[**Video**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_y0JOevkE8vyprmpLcpomHaw0Qbjrd9Q/view?usp=sharing)- Trevor Noah meets Christine Lagard, head of the IMF\n[**Article**](https://www.theguardian.com/business/2018/jan/26/imf-chief-warns-trumps-tax-cuts-could-destabilise-global-economy)**6: New Development Bank**[**Video 1**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1R57BIHWgeQY8Z4HHd1E_MqcclHI2Uj4W/view?usp=sharing) & [**video 2**](https://youtu.be/fjyaGLFWWNA)[**Article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eCoFRVbzVIZKtmtaoSupXkxOYyk6L9vg/view?usp=sharing)[**Article evaluating the Brics, July 2025**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Lnr9i497vTsrX9vUmQP2QRFIDvz0A89B/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**NEXT PAGE:** **Global Interactions and Power (Part 2)** - [**The economic, geographical and cultural influence of superpowers**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/globalizationsuperpowers.html)\n"},"resistance-to-gi.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/resistance-to-gi.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Resistance to GI\"\nparent: local-responses-to-global-interactions\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/resistance-to-gi.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Rejection of globalized production including campaigns against TNCs. \nWhy eat local (include facts and figures)? How can eating and buying locally produced products (food and other goods) reduce pollution? What is the opposing argument?\n\n{{youtube DhaG_Zi6izU}}\n\nUsing the [**article and/or videos**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Nj1InQzzCIwQC_YcGfo8k6nIvVFlAUEz?usp=sharing), outline the argument against one TNC by a civil society (Greenpeace).\n\n**Plenary:**[**Test yourself**](https://quizlet.com/50543101/costs-and-benefits-of-global-vs-local-production-flash-cards/) - cost benefits of local vs global (**tip:** make a note of wrong answers and look up the right ones!)\n**You! Yes, you:** [**Glossary love**](https://www.buddinggeographers.com/global-interactions/)\n"},"social-entrepreneurship-fair-trade--csr.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/social-entrepreneurship-fair-trade--csr.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Social Entrepreneurship, fair trade & CSR\"\nparent: development-opportunities\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/social-entrepreneurship-fair-trade--csr.html\n---\n\n**Social Entrepreneurship** explained - How might this approach be important to [**human development**](https://sustainabledevelopment.un.org/partnership/?p=23553)?\n\n{{youtube 9_g5RqwW51I}}\n\n**Team Task**: In your groups explore the relevant resources to build up understanding using [**these resources**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1B8-5ibaXs7iYlLpBjukDhoHAuYH3ALuF?usp=sharing) which focus on either Micro Finance, Fair Trade or Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR). Create a brief presentation for the class (share your [**presentation here**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Me-gUGac5prJGllOqJMpaWZucnXuMDgP?usp=sharing)).\nRemember to:\nExplain the concept/ idea,\nResearch & describe an example (how does it create positive social change in communities?) and\nCritique the concept.\n\n**Independent comprehension.** \nOutline the benefits of micro finance. \nExamine the critics view of micro finance.  \nBriefly explain the idea of the Fair Trade example you have seen. \nDiscuss the most significant critique of Fair Trade.    \nChoose a company that operates in Vietnam or your home country. Research its CSR. Outline the potential advantages of the goals and any potential omissions **OR** examine the advantages and disadvantages of CSR in general. \nWhy is CSR likely to become a greater issue in Vietnam over the next decade? Outline the advantages and disadvantages of TNCs using Vietnam as a source of cheap labor.  \n**Extension**\nExam style extended written question, which asks you to apply your knowledge and understanding of a fairly narrow topic. This time, the command word is \"explain\" instead of \"analyse\". \n**\"Explain why it might be hard to establish how human development varies between different countries\"** (12 marks)\n\n**Plenary:** Q&A\n[**Glossary additions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bBTbt_3RvELaW8iRawv7DSfdu3QQGfBGLTYTBbY2HL8/edit?usp=sharing)\n"},"strategies-to-build-resilience.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/strategies-to-build-resilience.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Strategies to build resilience\"\nparent: local-and-global-resilience\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/strategies-to-build-resilience.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Joseph Stiglitz discusses the \"solutions\".\nBut what, from what you have learned, have been the problems created by globalisation?\n**Mind map your ideas!**\n\n{{youtube h1EdTGsObaU}}\n\n**1. How are governments building resilience through TNC reshoring strategies?**\n\n**Reshoring** - Also known as on-shoring or back-shoring, this involves a TNC abandoning length supply chains and instead returning production operations to the country  where it is headquartered. The company will no longer make use of a spatial division of labor.\n\n\"What people are waking up to is the interconnectedness of global trade - a single missing chip from Japan can shut down an American factory on the other side of the world.\" R. Ward (Chief Exec of Lloyd's of London).\n\nMany TNCs have discovered that outsourcing can increase their exposure to different kinds of risks. So how do they respond?\n\n**a)** [**Identify the economic sectors**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1gb65-fqIonRjPptZU9v1sBGhXCgE-2UB/view?usp=sharing) and types of company that are most likely to reshore from this infographic. Can you suggest reasons for this?\n**b)** Read [**this Deloitte article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oH5SkDhHMElgsHcc9CtDMhi48sER2Kg5/view?usp=sharing) (September 2021) to answer the following:\ni) With reference to examples, give at least **three** reasons (risks) for why the trend for reshoring has increased in recent years. \nii) What kinds of industries seem to favour reshoring? Explain why. \n**c)** As a counterargument, read [**this Financial Times article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1pv2AEdvCXHGG0gObTaOpa1--mD5Ysa5B/view?usp=sharing) (January 2022) to explain why the trend towards reshoring may be exaggerated today.\n\n2. How are governments and civil society groups building greater resilience through crowdsourcing technologies?\n**Crowdsourcing** -The process of sourcing ideas, services, finances and information from the public via the Internet in order to benefit from the collective abilities of a large group of people.\n**Crowdfunding -** Raising sums of money for a good cause or innovation by asking a large number of people to donate a small amount each using an online platform.\n\n{{youtube -38uPkyH9vI}}\n\nUsing the [**crowdsourcing umbrella**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ysMwcW6l8uHNesNvHTy0g5AOayxXgpU6/view?usp=sharing)summarise the different ways in which crowdsourcing is used to build resilience.\n\nCrowdsourcing has proved particularly effective in the event of a natural disaster to coordinate assistance for those affected. It has also been used in the event of conflict, including the most recent in Ukraine. Read [**this BBC article**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CdlJQWEu2BIQKCfflOOr_1kZBmsESZph/view?usp=sharing) (March 2022) to explain how crowdsourcing has been used to support the victims of the war in Ukraine.\n\n**3. How are new technologies for the management of global flows of data and people being used to manage risks?**\n\n**a) Cyber-espionage** - When computer hackers gain illegal access to confidential government or company information. \nWatch the following video on the threats of cybercrime in the UK and how cybersecurity measures are fighting it. \nExtra - **Could \"deepfakes\" weaken democracy?** A short video from The Economist.\n\n{{youtube 1pxbQh-2gzs}}\n\n**b)****e-Passports****Dual citizenship** - citizenship of two countries concurrently (also known as dual nationality).\n**Biometrics** - Physical characteristics that are unique to a person. This includes fingerprints, retinal scans and information about a person's face, like the distance between eyes, nose, mouth and ears.\n\n**Countries with biometric passports**\n\nWatch the following video. What is a digital passport and what are the pros and cons of having \"e-passports\"?\n\n{{youtube 5F6CyR_Uu70}}\n\nLook at [**this image**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UKIBK-8iNuiM5RruHSYw7i1wJgfvLiQY/view?usp=sharing) that describes the evolution of the e-passport. Note down the advantages of fourth generation e-passports.\nWhat new threats may emerge through e-passports?\n\n**Plenary: Q&A - Which group can answer these Qs the fastest? Go!**\nWhat is meant by \"heightened global security risk\"? Give an example from this course. \nIdentify the information contained in an e-passport. \nHow do e-passports improve security? \nWhy are TNCs reshoring? \nOutline the main advantages of crowdsourcing. \nWhy is it difficult to manage cyber security? \nWhat are the pros and cons of e-passports?\n[**Another watch**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1bun7DN4a9uI-FKW2i17tTcm3NKo7xq19/view?usp=sharing)- the craziest five cyber attacks of all time\n"},"threats-to-individuals-and-business.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/threats-to-individuals-and-business.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Threats to individuals and business\"\nparent: geopolitical--economic-risks\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/threats-to-individuals-and-business.html\n---\n\n![Picture](assets/screen-shot-2021-10-09-at-1-41-52-pm_orig.png)\n\nCyber-crime is \"a crime in which a computer is the object of the crime (hacking, phishing, identify theft or online surveillance) or is used as a tool to commit an offense (child pornography, hate crimes). Cyber-criminals may use computer technology to access personal information, business trade secrets or use the internet for exploitative or malicious purposes\".\n\n**Starter:** According to the letter you have been given watch [**video A**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1W3gWYmplIIKZn6UR7crriUt9LJndMsEO/view?usp=sharing), [**video B**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Yylk28sgylq8S-BPeKoru6wnsMLI2A8O/view?usp=sharing) or [**video C**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ptwTDXvonY8f55_wrHndgZlUspxDMNTA/view?usp=sharing)**.** Then answer the questions appropriate for your video.\n\n**A - Identity Theft:** \nWhat is identify theft? Describe the main forms of identity theft? \nHow can you guard against it? \n**B - Hacking:** \nWhat is it? How is it done? \nHow can hacking be a threat to individuals AND businesses?\n**C - Implications of surveillance**\nWhat is online surveillance? How is data harvested? \nWhat has this information been used for? What is your opinion of this?\n\nMake a copy of [**this document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/17Td2u4I7Y2vnwtbcncI-eAsw83dO8pQQwQjYw6c42Ng/edit?usp=sharing) and complete the activities on it independently.\n\n**Exam style questions:**[**Evaluate this infographic.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/147VcG943ce91M3b4WGQyevF7q8nIRjRL/view?usp=sharing) \nAccording to the infographic - how can cyber-criminals steal money from individuals? \nIn your opinion, why are \"users\" (individuals rather than big business/ companies) soft targets?\n\n**Extension:** [**Crash Course**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1SBm5BpCpJpqu9E8yubYp2ZoMW9nj5qcB/view?usp=sharing)\n"},"transboundary-pollution.md":{"path":"content/5-hl-only-global-interactions/transboundary-pollution.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Transboundary Pollution\"\nparent: environmental-risks\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/transboundary-pollution.html\n---\n\n**Our case study: The Great Pacific Garbage Patch**\n\n![Picture](assets/graph-plastic_orig.png)\n\n**Tuning in:** \nWatch [**this clip of \"Drowning in Plastic\" (BBC 2018)**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/195vKP4Te7Dn9fykbTSIWyJd0BkkANP0n/view?usp=sharing)for a 'big picture' view on the issue. Follow the instructions at the beginning of the video.\n\n[**Introductory presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1TnKzfB1nm02R3_JVCZmCgmHcDTiSh9yL39wT57QFzgE/edit?usp=sharing) - to the causes, consequences and solutions of the issue, AND how to apply the four Ps!\n\n**Causes** (natural and human **processes**) and **consequences** (on **places**). Use these three resources:\n\n[**Article by National Geographic**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1MAPU2Vuuiyet7r5EbXXL0MYg-NFyBh8G/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) - causes, consequences, and a little on the problem of solving this transboundary issue.\n\nVideo - [**The Great Pacific Garbage Patch explained**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i4xZGTGAntjfBfwVDpgfRdohBokG2KOp/view?usp=sharing) (3 mins)\n\nBlog article - [**Who is to blame?**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1PjvlUJcV_VbydRpOZotBi2JaTkmj-k57/view?usp=sharing)\n\n{{youtube 9znvqIkIM-A}}\n\n**Possibilities/Solutions****1. Cleaning up the ocean** - investigate these resources of the Ocean Clean-up initiative. Evaluate its strengths and weaknesses.\n[**Article/interview**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rHlIMifWtorBx4-gc5a7HrHZo2T6EryS-UUZv-y9Hj0/edit?usp=sharing) with Boyan Slat, the founder\nWebsite [**theoceancleanup.com**](https://theoceancleanup.com/oceans/)\n\n**2. International agreements** - read these two recent articles. How **possible** is an international agreement on plastic pollution to solve the problem in oceans like the Pacific? What sources of **power** may be preventing it? \nNational Geographic (June 2021) [**Global treaty to regulate plastic pollution gains momentum**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FAY5Eb_Ak7BU7qCiviVN-L_KREjlxCSX/view?usp=sharing) \nThe Guardian (March 2022) [**A treaty could stem the tide**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1sg0z2LzNkMx6CQnrboYkkK5yOFAMsBmI/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary:** [**What's so funny?**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DJ9Jbd_X0YH1IRecatLCPd3s9Ckwl0Zs/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Extra:** There is so much on this issue! Extend your learning via these:\n[**Drowning in Plastic**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CJ6JP66MXlCRvnPeL6OzWxLubJIfavym/view?usp=sharing)(BBC 2018 - the full movie)[**A Plastic Ocean**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rDXoIcf_IzO1YP5R1jX_c-LZehoz_ZAP/view?usp=sharing) **-** The filmmakers of this movie visited us in 2018 ... \n[**Here is the famous TED Talk**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-rGcocU-9HvUHbJ-P0PxFUpkpMgQzD2p/view?usp=sharing) made by Boyan Slat, when he was about your age!\n"}},"6-resources-and-support":{"command-terms.md":{"path":"content/6-resources-and-support/command-terms.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Command terms\"\norder: 20\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/command-terms.html\n---\n\n[**This website is great for command terms**](https://www.geoib.com/command-terms.html)\n\nMake a copy of [**this document**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mWd2FX84NbEfmefBKTnTvLbgGoQWUX-XmEzfnkIpqNU/edit?usp=sharing) that breaks down the command terms further, with an example of how each one is used in Geography.\n"},"extended-response-writing.md":{"path":"content/6-resources-and-support/extended-response-writing.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Extended response writing\"\norder: 30\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/extended-response-writing.html\n---\n\n**Here is the support you need to write an extended response in Geography.**\n\nThe Paper 1 and 2 rubric for long responses with some more explanation is [**found here.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1JcmjlHUG9iSbZmWkR_ny1Xw76cmiSRSfZxiZHk1dx0E/edit?usp=sharing) \nFor Paper 3, [**here is the rubric**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1GXpN5fApWeLn_oVUAUYubKiWY2lHcUd7/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) for Part A questions.\n\n[**A template**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13qEsOykPZbzThAiDvN_B3CIEjBvjXQSVbK0Re1WmtB4/edit?usp=sharing) for planning an extended response\n[**A presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1nAyisGCOVH7Uf1IbGZ9hcGTRyRPQPnhnMrrYHFnEzbU/edit?usp=sharing) [that guides you through the stages of an extended response](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13qEsOykPZbzThAiDvN_B3CIEjBvjXQSVbK0Re1WmtB4/edit?usp=sharing)[**A word mat**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/103OmBZw7Ck2Jn1ty5QCjb2VZ5kCnLbLkkhpUNNqBl7I/edit?usp=sharing) for writing an extended response\n\n[**An example of a detailed plan**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Jh0Fwk7YlztjnrEU0e2U3uCc_HrTfvQv/view?usp=sharing)\n"},"glossary.md":{"path":"content/6-resources-and-support/glossary.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Glossary\"\norder: 80\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/glossary.html\n---\n\n[trilingual\\_glossary\\_of\\_geography\\_terms.pdf](assets/trilingual_glossary_of_geography_terms.pdf)\n\n[command\\_terms\\_new\\_guide.docx](assets/command_terms_new_guide.docx)\n"},"grade-10-dp-transition-day.md":{"path":"content/6-resources-and-support/grade-10-dp-transition-day.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Grade 10 DP Transition Day\"\norder: 70\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/grade-10-dp-transition-day.html\n---\n\n**Starter:** Student A -[**Would you trust Obama**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1YB5MA2ZqpbPTwjrLLoewm3f-mZh-5uJQ/view?usp=sharing)**,** student B - or perhaps [**HRH Prince William, Duke of Cambridge**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1G-6W1qlLo-74CtZ6hRgcUxFQoweqgfn7/view?usp=sharing)**?**\n\n[**Presentation**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1RRuDsL0h4naYTkwdXNJ9QVbv4ONNa4iJJP_comVLqCQ/edit?usp=sharing) **and** [**true or false activity**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1dLmVtZH2C0HYM4a_mQV0HokmVjqcSBVb/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Task 1: A typical lesson about a global, ongoing, important issue.** \na) Use [**this link**](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m1BLsySgsHM) and your mobile phones to watch the content. \nb) You are on the same journey and you can take one bag with you – what belongings will you take? No more than 10. Be ready to justify your answers.\nc) Come up with 5 questions of your own to better understand the picture below. [**Use the question grid.**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1AMyn8CB--rdeLaBwn1TW7TXDWHmqOjNR0MBSkelX0fg/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n![Picture](assets/boats_orig.png)\n\n**Task 2:** [**Card Sort Activity**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1PMncZ6zpZQIRDdMmrg4FZlhOYVN_G1Np/view?usp=sharing)\na) Organize the cards into groups/ categories you feel are appropriate.\nb) What were the groups/ categories you picked? Why? \nc) Using the information you have learned, through discussion with a partner work out an explanation to what you can see in the picture.\nd) Write it down independently. \ne) Now go over your explanation and try to use these word:\nForced Migration\nRefugee\nMigrant\nEmigrant\nPush factor & pull factor\nIDP (internally displaced people) **Extension:**[**Extra propaganda if needed.**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ZQrSGU7A2kPbsOEhlzCk44ucc9UPXL1F/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**Plenary:** \nWhere can you find the syllabus for DP Geo? \nHow can you work out what is being taught (options) over the two year course? \nWhere is the geography field trip to? For what unit? \nWhat are the 6 concepts in DP Geo?\n"},"ibdp-overview.md":{"path":"content/6-resources-and-support/ibdp-overview.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"IBDP Overview\"\norder: 10\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/ibdp-overview.html\n---\n\n[overview\\_of\\_geography\\_student\\_version\\_2018-2020.docx](assets/overview_of_geography_student_version_2018-2020.docx)\n"},"internal-assessment.md":{"path":"content/6-resources-and-support/internal-assessment.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Internal Assessment\"\norder: 50\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/internal-assessment.html\n---\n\n**Your IA is significant for both SL (35%) and HL (30%).****This page will be vital to your success.**\n\nStart by making a copy of [**this Google Doc--**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/14UXl-v2ucJUAGF1hSaSuGRelmug1W-EKf6HE7IfPviU/edit?usp=sharing)how to make a document with pre-loaded sections/page numbers.\n\n[**A digital copy of \"The\"**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1qEjbMLrNAJwbWsFJqsjpqd10G3F0SGkz/view?usp=sharing) **Bible—instructions** section-by-section, the mark scheme, how to collect data/methodology, how to present it, and how to use a statistical test in your analysis.\n\n[**Using AI in your Geography IA report - Policy and Guidance**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-3_SizeOCPh4WUNC8U9lywT5kaAJDNUt/view?usp=sharing)\n\n**To break down \"The Bible,\" here is further guidance for some of the sections for your IA report:****1.**[**Introduction**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1HBJDn4nsFLVUoPQpPWALybBD-uzHTgy4?usp=sharing): making maps, the geographic context.\nMaking a base map is really important. You will use it to overlay data that you collect many times in your IA report. \nGood choices are [**Google MyMaps**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1VA5YnZ-RBuzWwZxNBVZwwGn2MSJ2goD-/view?usp=sharing) (Mr. Gill's 'how to' guide screencast) and [**Data Wrapper**](https://www.datawrapper.de/). \nRemember to refer to [**this Weebly page**](https://geogjon.weebly.com/vung-tau-a-tourist-hotspot.html) for background on Vung Tau as a tourist hotspot. \n**2.**[**Methodology**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1UKvRF9mUeSOw2S5KlYnRZ0HGpmXOzdDf?usp=sharing)**3.**[**Visuals and Analysis**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Ww8Ph5pdtTeoJJakt1HRf8KjvjmjrnUF?usp=sharing) (including further guidance on how to complete a Spearmans Rank correlation test). \n**New in June 2026**: [**Flourish Studio**](https://flourish.studio/) - another online tool for you to make visual representations of data.\n\n**As you get closer to finishing your IA report, refer to:****1**. [**The advice from the IB examiners**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1wJEBTYGhS5vpY7kqbpokbwg1cMjAR3xO/view?usp=sharing) (May 2024, which was more informative than May 2025) on how to be successful—two pages that you MUST read! \n**2**. [**A checklist**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sWsWquAVwwCgews00UBudfD6jxmOIhjE/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=101400556587948969931&rtpof=true&sd=true) for you to ensure you have done everything you need to do, again written by an IB Geo examiner.\n\n**Extra**[**Images of V**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1pDhQqZlKcNtnjU-ljSgKK1SQaYY0np62?usp=sharing)**ũ**[**ng T**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1pDhQqZlKcNtnjU-ljSgKK1SQaYY0np62?usp=sharing)**à**[**u in the past**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1pDhQqZlKcNtnjU-ljSgKK1SQaYY0np62?usp=sharing). You could use one or two of these as a way of illustrating change over time—annotate it/them!\n\n**Articles that you might read/use/refer to as background/secondary sources:**[**Beaches packed, hotels fully booked in V**](https://news.tuoitre.vn/beaches-packed-hotels-fully-booked-in-vung-tau-on-third-day-of-lunar-new-year-103260220120248088.htm?fbclid=IwY2xjawQFUidleHRuA2FlbQIxMABicmlkETJ6dVk0cVhjRnVFeGVvVmJVc3J0YwZhcHBfaWQQMjIyMDM5MTc4ODIwMDg5MgABHjFN8XxKbWW5lHxu3Uj8smUBvGKzBn9S7wrcST_D0qXOOBuD0x7CqnyMvo4Z_aem_9NYeG2ioy410GYGHDq485A)**ũ**[**ng T**](https://news.tuoitre.vn/beaches-packed-hotels-fully-booked-in-vung-tau-on-third-day-of-lunar-new-year-103260220120248088.htm?fbclid=IwY2xjawQFUidleHRuA2FlbQIxMABicmlkETJ6dVk0cVhjRnVFeGVvVmJVc3J0YwZhcHBfaWQQMjIyMDM5MTc4ODIwMDg5MgABHjFN8XxKbWW5lHxu3Uj8smUBvGKzBn9S7wrcST_D0qXOOBuD0x7CqnyMvo4Z_aem_9NYeG2ioy410GYGHDq485A)**à**[**u on the third day of Lunar New Year**](https://news.tuoitre.vn/beaches-packed-hotels-fully-booked-in-vung-tau-on-third-day-of-lunar-new-year-103260220120248088.htm?fbclid=IwY2xjawQFUidleHRuA2FlbQIxMABicmlkETJ6dVk0cVhjRnVFeGVvVmJVc3J0YwZhcHBfaWQQMjIyMDM5MTc4ODIwMDg5MgABHjFN8XxKbWW5lHxu3Uj8smUBvGKzBn9S7wrcST_D0qXOOBuD0x7CqnyMvo4Z_aem_9NYeG2ioy410GYGHDq485A) (February 2026)\n[**Holiday crowds make beeline for Vung Tau beach amid scorching heat**](https://e.vnexpress.net/photo/places/holiday-crowds-make-beeline-for-vung-tau-beach-amid-scorching-heat-5068810.html) (May 2026)\n[**V**](https://e.vnexpress.net/news/news/environment/vung-tau-s-iconic-beach-reborn-cleared-revitalized-open-to-all-4937387.html)**ũ**[**ng T**](https://e.vnexpress.net/news/news/environment/vung-tau-s-iconic-beach-reborn-cleared-revitalized-open-to-all-4937387.html)**à**[**u's iconic beach reborn: Cleared, revitalized, and open to all**](https://e.vnexpress.net/news/news/environment/vung-tau-s-iconic-beach-reborn-cleared-revitalized-open-to-all-4937387.html) (September 2025)\n[**V**](https://e.vnexpress.net/photo/news/vung-tau-beach-gets-new-look-after-5-months-of-renovation-4865494.html)**ũ**[**ng T**](https://e.vnexpress.net/photo/news/vung-tau-beach-gets-new-look-after-5-months-of-renovation-4865494.html)**à**[**u beach gets new look after five months of renovation**](https://e.vnexpress.net/photo/news/vung-tau-beach-gets-new-look-after-5-months-of-renovation-4865494.html) (March 2025)\n**Toàn cảnh siêu dự án hơn 1.000 tỷ chỉnh trang Bãi Sau Vũng Tàu**[(images of Back Beach being rejuvenated, November 2024)](https://www.vietnam.vn/toan-canh-sieu-du-an-hon-1-000-ty-chinh-trang-bai-sau-vung-tau)\n"},"revision.md":{"path":"content/6-resources-and-support/revision.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Revision\"\norder: 40\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/revision.html\n---\n\n[**A guide to the Geo mock exams**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1gNrf0jR6351SiJ4RMwhZlVjHvHSrBSo7TpK6T8l691o/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Read through this presentation,**[**\"The Art of Revision\"**](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1en7shxWOBM4jkJy9woCwZfuG-oW-CsVj8OvVbU8SNhs/edit?usp=sharing).\nThe guidance here is useful for any of your DP subjects that involve you revising content.\n\n[**A guide to Geography revision including some activities (Geography Pods)**](https://www.ibgeographypods.org/revision.html)[**Command words and how they are used in Geography**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mWd2FX84NbEfmefBKTnTvLbgGoQWUX-XmEzfnkIpqNU/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Revision Outlines****Paper One (The Options)**[**Leisure, Sport & Tourism**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1oPPbFiXhwoZQOg5pQuYGhep2ExYL4BaO1nJFOaDQR38/edit?usp=sharing)[**Geophysical Hazards**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1nnSukT7zWvSxycvZF1R48cdiWabJACerqusrd0g5L88/edit?usp=sharing)[**Urban Environments**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1r_LDBES6bzzuun_uo2z7Z0QhQhHgNkfLIDvWLvGRubs/edit?usp=sharing)[**Food & Health**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1jH3jc-FJOTmew4EGHM3dJsbnMfVgAC5HK90IU75FLKg/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n**Paper Two (The Core units)**[**Changing Populations**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1xJXnRJMIMdxn8lLdLoqVaNdtmvgmW87zkygi3ydBlRI/edit?usp=sharing)[**Global Climate Change**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1GyTpaCCa2axGrEztXORqc2Xi9we7q5eayLNzMa3qErU/edit?usp=sharing)[**Global Resource Consumption & Security**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mXZIJqO2X4hqvjnHuFkw1xkGeVFuYrFtxOm49tkwTc0/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n[**Paper 3 - HL Global Interactions**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/10KjB10OtsovUACFfuEFXvpkz1UhL_9xSLL8i6ik4jIY/edit?usp=sharing)[**Here is the revision outline**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1aTSEjrE0l5rBn_szP81mwEe1V4w0Cnrqt3DJ8GkVjbc/edit?usp=sharing) [**for the whole HL Global Interactions course**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/10KjB10OtsovUACFfuEFXvpkz1UhL_9xSLL8i6ik4jIY/edit?usp=sharing)**.** **Be an examiner! Open** [**this folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1bsmqa_xkAgMB3pAq7X8uLlooZsBQOZwH?usp=sharing) **to see samples of three students' work for a former Paper 3. Bear in mind that in the previous guide, part a) was out of 10, and part b) out of 15. Read the questions/mark schemes first, then have a look at the students' efforts. Finally, look at the examiners' comments on each.**\n\n**Sample exam papers**[**This folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1uf1ApIFohfJrg808VH6BBB46OiRfiobu?usp=sharing) **contains a sample of a full Paper 1 and Paper 2, including the markscheme for each.**[**This document**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1V5JZo2TM6nfhbw_KiX7iS5x8JSox2FNU/view?usp=sharing) **has a sample of long response questions/markschemes for Paper 2 from the previous syllabus.**[**This folder**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B1D3UOqNjNuuMHBXSTlYZ2dkOFk?usp=sharing) **contains a number of exam papers from the previous syllabus. Bear in mind that the old Paper 1 is now Paper 2 (focusing on The Core), and the old Paper 2 is now Paper 1 (focusing on the option units).**\n"},"websites.md":{"path":"content/6-resources-and-support/websites.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Websites\"\norder: 60\nsource: https://geogjon.weebly.com/websites.html\n---\n\n"}}}};
